    "homepage_text": "Keyulu Xu\nKeyulu Xu\nEmail:\nkeyulux [at] csail (dot) mit (dot) edu\nOffice:\nMIT Stata Center, 32-G480\nMail:\n2-1-2 Hitotsubashi, Chiyoda-ku, Tokyo 101-8430\n[CV]\n[Google Scholar]\nI am a researcher, trader, and entrepreneur. My current goal is to revolutionize the global financial system. I received my Ph.D. in\nEECS\nfrom\nMIT\n, where I  was affiliated with\nCSAIL\nand the\nMachine Learning\ngroup.\nAt MIT, I was advised by\nStefanie Jegelka\n. Previously, I was an undergraduate at\nUBC\n, advised by\nNick Harvey\n. I seasonally visited\nKen-ichi Kawarabayashi\nat\nNII\nin Tokyo.  I am  looking for passionate and talented researchers, coders, and generalists  to join our team for a great adventure.\nRecent News\nCurrently working on projects with the goal of revolutionizing the global financial system.\nSummer 2020: Mid-frequency trading at\nTwo Sigma Investments\nwith statistical insights.\nSummer 2019:  High-frequency trading at\nHudson River Trading (HRT)\nwith AI.\nChristmas 2019:\nERATOæè¬ç¥­ & æå ±ç³» Winter Festa Episode 5\n[Ver. X]\n[Ver. Y]\nPublications\nEmail me\nif you have any questions about my papers or code, or if you would like to collaborate with me.\nModeling Intelligence via Graph Neural Networks\nPh.D. Thesis, MIT.\nMIT George M. Sprowls Best Thesis Award.\n[Paper]\n[Slides]\nHow does a Neural Network's Architecture Impact its Robustness to Noisy Labels?\nJingling Li, Mozhi Zhang, Keyulu Xu, John P. Dickerson, Jimmy Ba.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems (\nNeurIPS\n) 2021.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nOptimization of Graph Neural Networks: Implicit Acceleration by Skip Connections and More Depth\nKeyulu Xu, Mozhi Zhang, Stefanie Jegelka, Kenji Kawaguchi.\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (\nICML\n)  2021.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\nInformation Obfuscation of  Graph Neural Networks\nAlex Liao, Han Zhao, Keyulu Xu, Tommi Jaakkola, Geoffrey Gordon, Stefanie Jegelka, Russ Salakhutdinov.\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (\nICML\n)  2021.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nGraphNorm: A Principled Approach to Accelerating Graph Neural Network Training\nTianle Cai, Shengjie Luo, Keyulu Xu, Di He, Tie-Yan Liu, Liwei Wang.\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (\nICML\n)  2021.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nHow Neural Networks Extrapolate: From Feedforward to Graph Neural Networks\nKeyulu Xu, Mozhi Zhang, Jingling Li, Simon S. Du, Ken-ichi Kawarabayashi, Stefanie Jegelka.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (\nICLR\n)  2021\n(Oral)\n.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nWhat Can Neural Networks Reason About?\nKeyulu Xu, Jingling Li, Mozhi Zhang, Simon S. Du, Ken-ichi Kawarabayashi, Stefanie Jegelka.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (\nICLR\n)  2020\n(Spotlight)\n.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nGraph Neural Tangent Kernel: Fusing Graph Neural Networks with Graph Kernels\nSimon S. Du, Kangcheng Hou, Barnabas Poczos, Ruslan Salakhutdinov, Ruosong Wang,  Keyulu Xu.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems (\nNeurIPS\n) 2019.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nAre Girls Neko or ShÅjo?\nCross-Lingual Alignment of Non-Isomorphic Embeddings with Iterative Normalization\nMozhi Zhang, Keyulu Xu, Ken-ichi Kawarabayashi, Stefanie Jegelka, Jordan Boyd-Graber.\nAssociation for Computational Linguistics (\nACL\n) 2019.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nHow Powerful are Graph Neural Networks?\nKeyulu Xu, Weihua Hu, Jure Leskovec, Stefanie Jegelka.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (\nICLR\n)  2019\n(Oral)\n.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nRepresentation Learning on Graphs with Jumping Knowledge Networks\nKeyulu Xu, Chengtao Li, Yonglong Tian, Tomohiro Sonobe, Ken-ichi Kawarabayashi, Stefanie Jegelka.\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (\nICML\n)  2018\n(Long Talk)\n.\n[Paper]\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nDistributional Adversarial Networks\nChengtao Li,  David Alvarez-Melis, Keyulu Xu, Stefanie Jegelka, Suvrit Sra.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations workshop track (\nICLR\n)  2018.\n[arXiv]\n[code]\nGenerating Random Spanning Trees via Fast Matrix Multiplication\nNicholas J. A. Harvey and Keyulu Xu.\nLatin American Theoretical Informatics Symposium (\nLATIN\n) 2016.\n[Paper]\n[Web]\nExperience\nMy academic and business experience mostly happen in NYC and Tokyo.\nTokyo  is my favorite place in the world. I seasonally visit\nERATO Kawarabayashi Large Graph Project\nat NII, where many\nSTOC, FOCS, SODA best papers\nwere written.\nI visit NYC now and then for business and social stuff.\nTalks\nUniversity of Chicago\n, Department of Statistics, Nov 2021, Chicago, USA.\n[Slides]\nCambridge University\nand\nTUM\n,\nGraph and Geometry Learning\n, Aug 2021, Online.\n[Slides]\nMicrosoft Research\n, ML Ideas Seminar, April 2021, New England, USA.\n[Slides]\nFoundations of Data Science Institute\n, ML for Algorithms, April 2021, Cambridge, USA.\n[Slides]\nImperial College London\n,\nMatchLab\n, Feb 2021, London, UK.\n[Slides]\nHarvard University\n,  Department of Mathematics,\nCMSA Seminar\n, Nov 2020, Cambridge, USA.\n[Slides]\nERATOæè¬ç¥­ & æå ±ç³»\nWinter Festa Episode 5\n, Dec 2019, Tokyo, Japan.\n[Slides]\nçåå­¦ç ç©¶æ RIKEN AIP\n,\nSeminar\n, Aug 2019, Tokyo, Japan.\n[Slides]\nPreferred Networks\n, Seminar,  Aug 2019, Tokyo, Japan.\n[Slides]\nHudson River Trading\n, AI Labs Seminar,  Jul 2019, New York, USA.\n[Slides]\nKyoto University\n,\nKashima & Yamada Lab\n, Feb 2019, Kyoto, Japan.\n[Slides]\nçåå­¦ç ç©¶æ RIKEN AIP\n,\nSeminar\n, Aug 2018, Tokyo, Japan.\n[Slides]"
