    "homepage_text": "Hanrui Zhang's Homepage\nHanrui Zhang's Homepage\nContact: hanrui at cse dot cuhk dot edu dot hk (preferred), or hanruizha at gmail (backup)\nOffice: SHB 1014\nHello!  I am an Assistant Professor of Computer Science and Engineering at the Chinese University of Hong Kong.  Prior to that, I:\nwas a visiting faculty researcher at Google Research NYC, working with the OMEGA Market Algorithms team led by Vahab Mirrokni;\nwas a postdoctoral fellow at Simons Laufer Mathematical Sciences Institute (SLMath, formerly MSRI), affiliated with UC Berkeley;\ngot my PhD in Computer Science from Carnegie Mellon University, advised by Vincent Conitzer;\ndid my undergraduate in Yao Class, Tsinghua University, where I worked closely with Wei Chen and Pingzhong Tang.\nI am interested in Economics and Computation – problems with economic motivations that can be approached using techniques from computer science.  My recent research focuses on learning and decision making in complex environments, in the presence of strategic behavior, with limited means of interaction, under uncertainty of the future.  I also work extensively on mechanism / information design problems related to online platforms and data markets (\nhere\nis a survey on ad auctions and autobidding that I coauthored during my time at Google).  Technically, most of my research can be characterized as “applied theory”: I use tools (and sometimes craft new ones) in various branches of Theoretical Computer Science, Microeconomic Theory, and Machine Learning Theory to solve problems that are both practically motivated and mathematically well-defined.  Accordingly, I publish in a wide range of venues, whose themes cover all these areas.\nI am constantly looking for students\ninterested in developing algorithmic solutions for / theoretical understanding of problems with economic or social motivations.  Applicants are expected to\nenjoy (and ideally, be good at) discovering and proving mathematical facts\n(because this constitutes 80%-100% of the research conducted in our group).  Feel free to drop me an email if you are interested.  (I try to be responsive, although that is becoming increasingly hard given the number of such inquiries.  To increase your chance of getting a response, you may want to mention any relevant research experience or awards, including OI, MO, ICPC, etc.)\nPublications and (Some) Manuscripts\n*: alphabetical order\n2025+\nNonbossy Mechanisms: Mechanism Design Robust to Secondary Goals.\n[arXiv]\nRenato Paes Leme*, Jon Schneider*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nManuscript.\nNo-Regret Online Autobidding Algorithms in First-price Auctions.\nYilin Li, Yuan Deng, Wei Tang,\nHanrui Zhang\n.\nThirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025).\nClock Auctions: Allocation-Based Characterization, Computational Complexity, and Economic Efficiency.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n.\nTwenty-Sixth ACM Conference on Economics and Computation (EC 2025).\n2024\nStrategic Littlestone Dimension: Improved Bounds on Online Strategic Classification.\n[arXiv]\nSaba Ahmadi*, Kunhe Yang*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).\nComputational Aspects of Bayesian Persuasion under Approximate Best Response.\n[arXiv]\nKunhe Yang*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).\nEfficiency of the First-Price Auction in the Autobidding World.\n[arXiv]\nYuan Deng*, Jieming Mao*, Vahab Mirrokni*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*, Song Zuo*.\nThirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).\nAutobidder's Dilemma: Why More Sophisticated Autobidders Lead to Worse Auction Efficiency.\n[pdf]\nYuan Deng*, Jieming Mao*, Vahab Mirrokni*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*, Song Zuo*.\nThirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).\nAggregating Quantitative Relative Judgments: From Social Choice to Ranking Prediction.\n[pdf]\nYixuan Even Xu,\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).\nAuto-bidding and Auctions in Online Advertising: A Survey.\n[pdf]\nGagan Aggarwal et al. (20+ authors, alphabetically ordered).\nSIGecom Exchanges 22.1.\nEfficiency of the Generalized Second-Price Auction for Value Maximizers.\n[arXiv]\nYuan Deng*, Mohammad Mahdian*, Jieming Mao*, Vahab Mirrokni*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*, Song Zuo*.\nThe 2024 ACM Web Conference (WWW 2024).\nNon-Excludable Bilateral Trade Between Groups.\n[arXiv]\nYixuan Even Xu,\nHanrui Zhang\n, Vincent Conitzer.\n38th AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2024).\n2023\nEfficiently Solving Turn-Taking Stochastic Games with Extensive-Form Correlation.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nTwenty-Fourth ACM Conference on Economics and Computation (EC 2023).\nAutobidding Auctions in the Presence of User Costs.\n[pdf]\nYuan Deng*, Jieming Mao*, Vahab Mirrokni*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*, Song Zuo*.\nThe 2023 ACM Web Conference (WWW 2023).\nEligibility Mechanisms: Auctions Meet Information Retrieval.\n[pdf]\nGagan Goel*, Renato Paes Leme*, Jon Schneider*, David Thompson*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThe 2023 ACM Web Conference (WWW 2023).\n2022\nPosted Pricing and Dynamic Prior-independent Mechanisms with Value Maximizers.\n[pdf]\nYuan Deng*, Vahab Mirrokni*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\n36th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2022).\nNear-Optimal Reviewer Splitting in Two-Phase Paper Reviewing and Conference Experiment Design.\n[arXiv]\nSteven Jecmen,\nHanrui Zhang\n, Ryan Liu, Fei Fang, Vincent Conitzer, Nihar B. Shah.\n10th AAAI Conference on Human Computation and Crowdsourcing (HCOMP 2022),\nHonorable Mention for Best Paper\n.\nEfficient Algorithms for Planning with Participation Constraints.\n[arXiv]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nTwenty-Third ACM Conference on Economics and Computation (EC 2022).\nPlanning with Participation Constraints.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\n36th AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2022).\nLearning Influence Adoption in Heterogeneous Networks.\n[pdf]\nVincent Conitzer*, Debmalya Panigrahi*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\n36th AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2022).\n2021\nAutomated Dynamic Mechanism Design.\n[arXiv]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-fifth Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021).\nPrior-independent Dynamic Auctions for a Value-maximizing Buyer.\n[pdf]\nYuan Deng*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThirty-fifth Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021).\nAutomated Mechanism Design for Classification with Partial Verification.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-Fifth AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2021).\nIncentive-Aware PAC Learning.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-Fifth AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2021).\nClassification with Few Tests through Self-Selection.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-Fifth AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2021).\nClassification with Strategically Withheld Data.\n[arXiv]\nAnilesh Krishnaswamy*, Haoming Li*, David Rein*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-Fifth AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2021).\nOnline Combinatorial Auctions.\n[pdf]\nYuan Deng*, Debmalya Panigrahi*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThirty-second Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA 2021).\n2020\nMitigating Manipulation in Peer Review via Randomized Reviewer Assignments.\n[arXiv]\nSteven Jecmen,\nHanrui Zhang\n, Ryan Liu, Nihar B. Shah, Vincent Conitzer, Fei Fang.\nThirty-fourth Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2020).\nA Generic Truthful Mechanism for Combinatorial Auctions.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n.\n16th Conference on Web and Internet Economics (WINE 2020).\nImproved Prophet Inequalities for Combinatorial Welfare Maximization with (Approximately) Subadditive Agents.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n.\n28th Annual European Symposium on Algorithms (ESA 2020),\nBest Student Paper\n.\nLearning the Valuations of a \\(k\\)-demand Agent.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-seventh International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2020).\nLearning Opinions in Social Networks.\n[pdf]\nVincent Conitzer*, Debmalya Panigrahi*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThirty-seventh International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2020).\nNearly Linear Row Sampling Algorithm for Quantile Regression.\n[arXiv]\nYi Li*, Ruosong Wang*, Lin F. Yang*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThirty-seventh International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2020).\nCombinatorial Ski Rental and Online Bipartite Matching.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Vincent Conitzer.\nTwenty-First ACM Conference on Economics and Computation (EC 2020).\nA Graph-Theoretical Basis of Stochastic-Cascading Network Influence: Characterizations of Influence-Based Centrality.\n[arXiv]\nWei Chen*, Shang-Hua Teng*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nTheoretical Computer Science (TCS).\n2019\nDistinguishing Distributions When Samples Are Strategically Transformed.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-third Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2019).\nProvably Efficient Q-learning with Function Approximation via Distribution Shift Error Checking Oracle.\n[arXiv]\nSimon S. Du*, Yuping Luo*, Ruosong Wang*,\nHanrui Zhang\n*.\nThirty-third Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2019).\nWhen Samples Are Strategically Selected.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-Sixth International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2019).\nA PAC Framework for Aggregating Agents’ Judgments.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-Third AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2019).\nLearning Set Functions with Limited Complementarity.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n.\nThirty-Third AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2019).\nA Better Algorithm for Societal Tradeoffs.\n[pdf]\nHanrui Zhang\n, Yu Cheng, Vincent Conitzer.\nThirty-Third AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2019).\nCapturin...[truncated]"
