    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/dongkuan-dk-xu-05038087/",
    "homepage_text": "Dongkuan (DK) Xu\nDongkuan (DK) Xu\nThis website is no longer actively maintained. For the latest updates on my research,\n                publications, and activities, please visit my current homepage:\nhttps://i.dk-lab.xyz/\n.\nI'm an Assistant Professor in\nComputer Science\nat NC State University, where I lead the Generative Intelligent Computing (GIC) Lab. My research focuses on deep learning, language models, and computer vision, with an emphasis on efficient and reliable agentic AI systems. I received the NC State College of Engineering Dean’s Applied AI Research Accelerator Award 2026, the AAAI Best Demonstration Award 2025, the AAAI New Faculty Highlights 2025, the Best Poster Award at AAAI 2025 Spring Symposium on GenAI@Edge, the NVIDIA Academic Grant Program Award 2025, the Microsoft Accelerating Foundation Models Research Award 2024, the NC State Carla Savage Award 2024, the Best Paper Award Runner-Up at IEEE IPCCC 2024, and the Best Paper Award at ICCCN 2023. I received my Ph.D. from Penn State in 2022.\nI'm also involved in\nMARINA\n, a research initiative at the intersection of AI and marine sciences, focusing on advancing ocean modeling, environmental monitoring, and AI-driven scientific discovery. As part of my work on AI systems, I have contributed to the development of\nSynthora\nand\nGentopia\n.\nSynthora\nis an extensible AI platform designed to enhance the computational efficiency of LLM agents through full-stack optimization.\nGentopia\nis a lightweight framework providing essential components for building and evaluating LLM agents.\nOutside of work, I'm a fan of American football (Wolfpack, Nittany Lions, Eagles).\nEmail\n/\nGoogle Scholar\n/\nTwitter\n/\nLinkedIn\n/\nCV (Sep 2025)\n📣\nSeeking 1-2 PhD students, encouraging candidates from\nunderrepresented groups\n. Interested applicants should email\n\"PhD Fall 2026\"\nwith their\nCV\n.\nAbout\nGIC Lab\nPublications\nProjects\nOutreach\nTeaching\nNews\n02/2026: Received the\nDean’s Applied AI Research Accelerator Award\n($100k, PI) to advance scalable agentic AI for automated scientific discovery\n02/2026:\nPreprint\n: Execution-Driven Reasoning Augmentation for Mathematical Problem Solving in LLMs\n[link]\n02/2026:\nPreprint\n: Exploration–Exploitation Optimization for Scalable Dataset Distillation\n[link]\n02/2026:\nPreprint\n: SafeTalkCoach: Diversity-Driven Multi-Agent Simulation for Health Dialogue Generation\n[link]\n02/2026: RelKD’26 (4th Workshop on Resource-Efficient Learning) was accepted at\nKDD'26\n02/2026: Our ICLR'26 paper on adaptive LLM value measurement was selected for an\nOral Presentation\n(top 5%)\n02/2026: Invited to serve as New Faculty Highlights Chair at\nIEEE Big Data'26\n02/2026: The website for our OceanAI platform is live\n[web]\n[paper]\n02/2026: A paper on unified token compression for joint generation and classification was accepted to\nCVPR'26\n01/2026: A paper on adaptive measurement of LLM value differences was accepted to\nICLR'26\n01/2026: A paper on project-based AI bias education tools was accepted to\nCHI'26\n01/2026: A paper on deterministic agentic control for Open RAN was accepted to\nICC'26\n01/2026: A paper on AI as a design partner in creative computing was accepted to\nISLS'26\n01/2026: Two works on conversational ocean AI and multi-agent reporting\n[link 1]\n[link 2]\nwere presented at\nAMS'26\n01/2026: Invited to serve as an\nNSF\npanelist\n12/2025: Received the\nNSF NAIRR Pilot Award\n(PI) to scale trustworthy retrieval-augmented AI systems\n12/2025: OceanAI was accepted to the\nWACV'26 Demo Track\n11/2025: Received the\nCMI Ideation Award\n($30k, Co-PI) to pioneer agentic AI for next-generation scientific imaging\n11/2025: A paper on multi-scale topological learning for medical image classification was accepted to\nWACV'26\n11/2025:\nPreprint\n: Inference-Scaled GraphRAG for Multi-Hop Knowledge Reasoning\n[link]\n11/2025:\nPreprint\n: OceanAI: Retrieval-Grounded Conversational Platform for Trustworthy Scientific Data\n[link]\n09/2025: Two papers on multimodal reasoning and report generation were accepted to\nICDM’25 SAIMBio workshop\n09/2025: Awarded a grant from the\nNSF ExLENT program\n($1M, Co-PI) to advance trustworthy & scalable generative AI for augmenting teaching and learning in in-service contexts\n08/2025: Congratulations to Noah for receiving the\nNCSU COE REU Award\n($3k)\n08/2025: Awarded a\nDELTA Grant\n($8k, Co-PI) to scale and evaluate trustworthy LLM agents for education\n08/2025: Our work on AI single-molecule imaging denoising was featured as the\ncover article\nin Analytical Chemistry\n06/2025: A paper on non-overlap-aware pose estimation for multi-robot perception was accepted as\nan oral\nto\nIROS'25\n06/2025: Keynote speakers for the\n2nd DCgAA\nworkshop at DAC'25 were announced:\nPeter A. Beerel\n(USC),\nYiran Chen\n(Duke),\nTulika Mitra\n(NUS),\nDavid Z. Pan\n(UT Austin),\nYizhou Sun\n(UCLA)\n06/2025: We launched ArtAlgo\n[link]\n, a creative coding platform for generating visual art through code and AI\n05/2025: The website for our NSF project\nEcoTern\nis now live\n[link]\n05/2025: The website for our\n2nd DCgAA\nworkshop @DAC'25 is now live\n[link]\n05/2025: Congrats to my undergraduate students Aditya and Noah for being selected to present at NC State’s 34th Annual Spring Undergraduate Research & Creativity Symposium\n05/2025: Noah received a Project Supply Grant from the NC State Office of Undergraduate Research ($1400)\n05/2025: A paper on tool-augmented LLM agents for multi-modal causal discovery was accepted to\nACL'25\n05/2025: A paper on achieving real-time vision Transformers on mobile was accepted to\nIJCV\n05/2025: A paper on augmenting LLMs for HPC I/O performance diagnosis was accepted to\nSSDBM‘25\n05/2025: A paper on generative AI-driven design for communication systems was accepted to\nIEEE Comm. Magazine\n04/2025: Gave talks at\nSAS\nand\nNCSU MSE Department\n:\nBuilding Scalable and Efficient Agentic AI Systems\n04/2025: News about our AAAI-2025 Best Demonstration Award\n[link]\n04/2025: Received the\nBest Poster Award\nat the AAAI 2025 Spring Symposium on GenAI@Edge\n03/2025: Awarded a grant from the\nNC State TELS Department\n($1.6k, Co-PI) for AI-powered creative computing research\n03/2025: Awarded a grant from the\nNVIDIA Academic Grant Program\nfor networked scientific simulation research\n03/2025: Aditya received\nNCSU COE REU Award\n($3k)\n03/2025:\nMerryQuery\nwon the\nAAAI-2025 Best Demonstration Award\n03/2025: Our 2nd workshop on deep learning-hardware co-design for GenAI acceleration was accepted at\nDAC'25\n02/2025: The\nI/O Navigator Demo v1\n[link]\n, developed w/ UDel, OSU, LBNL and NCSU, is available\n02/2025: Our work\nAdaptix\n, training-free accelerated LLM speculative decoding, was selected for\nan Oral\nat\nAAAI'25\n01/2025: Invited to serve as an\nNSF\npanelist\n01/2025: Our work\nIOAgent\n, LLM-powered I/O performance diagnosis for HPC storage, was accepted to\nIPDPS'25\n01/2025: Noah's (undergraduate) work on\nLLM bias evaluation\nwas accepted at\nNCUR'25\n01/2025: Gave a talk at\nABB Research Center\n:\nAdvancing Real-World Edge Intelligence\n12/2024: Selected for the\nAAAI New Faculty Highlights 2025\n12/2024: Awarded a grant from the\nNC State Data Science and AI Academy\n($30k, Co-PI) to support research on developing responsible LLM agents for enhancing access to large environmental datasets\n[link]\n12/2024: Our work on adaptive accelerated LLM decoding was accepted to\nAAAI'25\n12/2024: Our tutorial, Resource-Efficient Learning for the Web, has been accepted by\nTheWebConf'25\n11/2024: Our paper on network-oriented LLMs won\nBest Paper Award Runner-Up\nat\nIPCCC'24\n11/2024:\nMerryQuery\nwas accepted to\nAAAI'25 (Demo Track)\n11/2024: Noah had her research accepted by the AAAI'25 Undergraduate Consortium\n11/2024: Talks at Purdue ECE-570 and NCSU DSC-595:\nBuilding Reliable Cost-Effective Sparse Models\n11/2024: Our\nGenAI@Edge Symposium\nwas accepted at the AAAI 2025 Spring Symposium Series\n11/2024: Our workshop, RelWeb\n[link]\n, was accepted at\nTheWebConf'25\n09/2024: Paper on black-box LLM-generated text detection was accepted to\nNeurIPS'24\n09/2024: Noah received\nthe Federal Work-Study Award\n($1,000)\n09/2024: Will attend the\n2024 Oak Ridge Lab’s Core Universities AI Workshop\n. See you in Raleigh\n09/2024: Gave a talk at\nABB Research Center, US\n:\nAgentic LLM Diagnostic Chatbot for SAP Manufacturing\n08/2024: Noah received\nCOE REU Award\n($3,000)\n08/2024: A paper on integrating LLMs with network simulators for script-free simulations was accepted to\nIPCCC'24\n08/2024: Awarded a grant from the\nNSF CyberTraining program\n($979k, Co-PI) to support AI-driven, cross-disciplinary training for sustainable environmental science research\n[link]\n08/2024: Will attend the\nNSF CSSI/CyberTraining/SCIPE PI Meeting 2024\n. See you in Charlotte\n07/2024: Will attend the\nNSF Workshop on Sustainable Computing 2024\n. See you at Purdue\n07/2024: The research paper of our\nworkshops\non Integrating ChatGPT into K-12 Classrooms was accepted to\nFIE'24\n07/2024: Keynote speakers for\nRelKD\nworkshop\nat KDD'24 were announced:\nDerek Cheng\n(Google DeepMind),\nHanghang Tong\n(UIUC),\nJing Gao\n(Purdue)\n07/2024: A paper on algorithm-hardware co-design for real-time vision Transformer was accepted to\nCODES+ISSS'24\n07/2024: A paper on accelerating diffusion generative models via adaptive computation was accepted to\nECCV'24\n06/2024: Awarded a grant from the\nNSF HEGS program\n($396k, Co-PI) to support research on multimodal data analysis of neighborhood dynamics\n[link]\n06/2024: Received a\nGift Fund\nfrom Microsoft\n06/2024: Successfully concluded our 3rd workshop on integrating Large Language Models into\nK-12\nclassrooms\n[News]\n06/2024: Our research work on black-box LLM detection is available\n[link]\n06/2024: Our research work on scaling LLM with massive tools is available\n[link]\n05/2024: Keynote speakers for\nDCgAA\nworkshop\nat DAC'24 were announced:\nAndreas Andreou\n(JHU),\nFarinaz Koushanfar\n(UCSD),\nMassoud Pedram\n(USC),\nVijay Janapa Reddi\n(Harvard),\nZhibin Xiao\n(\nCASPA\n)\n05/2024: A paper on digital twin-assisted reliable edge caching for wireless networks was accepted to\nIEEE JSAC (IF=16.4)\n0...[truncated]"
