      "weichen@genai4all.org"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/wei-chen-60980627/",
    "homepage_text": "Wei Chen\nUConn page\nLinkedIn\nGoogle Scholar\nGenAI-related\nAcademic (UConn)\nWei Chen\nis an\nAssociate Professor\nof\nOperations and Information Management\n(OPIM) at the\nSchool of Business\n,\nUniversity of Connecticut\n. He also serves as the Academic Director of the UConn\nDigital Frontiers Initiative\n, which promotes collaborations between industry and academia, with a particular emphasis on organizational AI transformation and governance.\nWei's research spans both scholarly inquiry and practice-oriented frameworks, focusing on how organizations design and govern generative AI capabilities to transform decision-making, knowledge work, and operations. His earlier work also explored platforms, crowds, and financial technologies (FinTech). His work has been published in leading academic journals, such as\nManagement Science\n,\nInformation Systems\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tResearch\n, and\nMIS Quarterly\n.\nHis textbook,\nGenerative AI for Business: Frameworks, Techniques, and Governance\n, is now available.\nGenAI for Business Textbook\nWhat's New\n2026\nJan 14\nOur working paper\nOptimizing Prompts for Large Language Models: A Causal Approach\nis available on SSRN.\n2025\nOct 28\nTextbook published:\nGenerative AI for Business: Frameworks, Techniques, and Governance\n.\nAug 16\nOur paper\nImpacts of Process Automation on Software Development and the IT Workforce\nwon the\nBest Paper Award\nat the Americas Conference on Information Systems (AMCIS 2025).\nJul 22\nSuccessfully finished the GenAI Workshop for the CT Library Consortium.\nMay 21\nPresented our paper on Generative AI and Organizational Structure at the Wharton Future of Work Conference. [\nvideo\n]\nMay 16\ngenai4all.org\nis online at the\nIT Teaching Workshop\n—one step closer to making GenAI accessible. [\nmy post\n]\nApr 10\nModerated a panel discussion in the\nData Disrupted\nevent.\n2024\nNov 8\nPresented\nGen AI for Knowledge Workers\nat Southwest CT Tech Hub.\nOct 29\nDiscussed\n\"GenAI and the Future of Business Intelligence\"\nwith Stamford BIT network.\nOct 20\nDeeply honored to receive the\nSandra A. Slaughter Early Career Award\nfrom the INFORMS Information Systems Society.\nApr 24\nFinished our first\nGenAI Workshop\n. Enjoyed the discussion and learned a lot from the\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tparticipants.\nApr 5\nWe successfully launched our\nDigital Frontiers Initiative\nwith the first annual\nDigital Frontiers Banquet\n.\nMar 27\nTalked at\nHartford AI Day 2024\nabout GenAI and the future of business, and how to\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcustomize large language models with prompt engineering and retrieval augmented generation. [\nUConn Today\n]\nMar 22\nDiscussed \"\nHow Has Generative AI Affected Education, Research, and Practice\n\" in a panel at the MIS\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t50th academic conference.\nMar 21\nGave a talk about our\nrecent paper\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tabout the transprancy of foundation models\nand firms' strategic fine-tuning efforts in a\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tresearch seminar at the Rady School of Management, UC San Diego.\nFeb 23\nWe started the Digital Frontiers Seminar series with a talk about \"\nGenerative AI and the Future of Business\n\" at UConn Stamford.\nJan 11\nGave\na talk to high school students\nat the Academy of Information Technology and\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tEngineering (AITE) in Stamford.\n2023\nAug 23\nJoined the UConn School of Business as an Associate Professor in the Operations\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tand Information Management Department, with an additional appointment as the Academic Director of the\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tConnecticut Information Technology Institute.\nApr 28\nObtained my tenure letter from the University of Arizona."
  },
