    "homepage_text": "Lei Zhao\r\n | Civil & Environmental Engineering | Illinois\nBlock I logo\nUniversity of Illinois Urbana-Champaign\nGive\nCorporate Engagement\nMy.CEE\nThe Grainger College of Engineering\nCivil & Environmental Engineering\nSearch\nMenu\nLei Zhao\nLei Zhao\nBeckman Fellow, Center for Advanced Study\nOffice of Risk Management and Insurance Reseach (ORMIR) Faculty Fellow\nAssistant Professor\n(217) 300-9546\nleizhao@illinois.edu\n3211 Newmark Civil Engineering Building\nPrimary Research Area\nEnvironmental Engineering and Science\nResearch Areas\nEnergy-Water-Environment Sustainability\nEnvironmental Engineering and Science\nSustainable and Resilient Infrastructure Systems\nFor More Information\nGoogle Scholar\nZhao Research Group\nBiography\nDr. Lei Zhao is an assistant professor in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, and assistant professor affiliated with the Institute for Sustainability, Energy, and Environment (iSEE), and the National Center of Supercomputing Applications at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign (UIUC). His research concerns the physical and engineering processes in the Atmospheric Boundary Layer where most human activities and environmental systems are concentrated, with a particular focus on built surfaces and urban environments. He combines theory, Earth system modeling, remote sensing and in situ observations, and cutting-edge artificial intelligence/machine learning (AI/ML) methods to study land-atmosphere interaction and environmental fluid mechanics that relate to urban climatology and hydrology, urban climate change, and climate impacts and adaptation. Lei is the recipient of the U.S. NSF CAREER Award, the Timothy Oke Award from the International Association for Urban Climate (2023), and the AGU Global Environmental Change Early Career Award from the American Geophysical Union (2023). He received his Ph.D. degree in atmospheric physics from Yale University and B.S. degree from Nanjing University. Before joining at UIUC, he worked as a postdoctoral fellow at Princeton University.\nEducation\nB.S., Physics & Atmospheric Physics, Nanjing University, 2009\nPh.D., Environmental Science, Yale University, 2015 (Advisor: Dr. Xuhui Lee)\nPostdoctoral Research Associate, Princeton University, 12/2015 - 05/2018 (Supervisor: Dr. Michael Oppenheimer)\nAcademic Positions\nAssistant Professor, Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, 10/2018 - present\nAssistant Professor (affiliated), National Center for Supercomputing Applications, 11/2018 - present\nAssistant Professor (affiliated), Institute for Sustainability, Energy, and Environment (iSEE), 03/2023 - present\nAssistant Professor (affiliated), Department of Climate Meteorology and Atmospheric Sciences, 08/16/2025 - present\nJournal Editorships\nEditor, Earth's Future (a top-ranked journal of the American Geophysical Union), 2026 - present\nAssociate Editor, Urban Climate (a top-ranked journal in urban climate science field by Elsevier ScienceDirect), 2024 - 2025\nAssociate Editor, Earth's Future (a top-ranked journal of the American Geophysical Union), 2023 - 2026\nProfessional Societies\nUnion Fellows Committee, American Geophysical Union (AGU), 2026 - present\nExecutive Committee, Global Environmental Change Section, American Geophysical Union (AGU), 2025 - present\nResearch Interests\nUrban climate\nMulti-scale Earth system modeling\nLand-atmosphere interactions\nMachine learning and artificial intelligence\nClimate change impacts, adaptation and mitigation\nEnvironmental fluid mechanics and turbulence\nRemote sensing\nBoundary-layer meteorology\nGraduate Research Opportunities\nI am looking for highly motivated graduate students to join my group. Please contact me for questions about potential projects or about the application process at our department.\nPrimary Research Area\nEnvironmental Engineering and Science\nResearch Areas\nEnergy-Water-Environment Sustainability\nEnvironmental Engineering and Science\nSustainable and Resilient Infrastructure Systems\nSelected Articles in Journals\nJ. Zhao, L. Yu, L. Zhao, H. Fu, P. Gong Significant contribution of urban morphological diversity to urban surface thermal heterogeneity. Urban Climate 61, 102383 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.uclim.2025.102383 (2025).\nJ. Zhao, L. Yu, L. Zhao, H. Fu, P. Gong Asymmetric influence of urban morphology changes on land surface temperature between daytime and nighttime. Sustainable Cities and Society 124, 106307 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scs.2025.106307 (2025).\nK. Zhang, B. Fang, K. Oleson, L. Zhao, C He, Q Huang, Z Liu, C Cao, X Lee Urban land expansion amplifies surface warming more in dry climate than in wet climate: A global sensitivity study. Journal of Geophysical Research - Atmospheres 130 (4), e2024JD041696 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JD041696 (2025).\nH. Du, W. Zhan, B. Zhou, Y. Ju, Z. Liu, A. Middel, K. Huang, L. Zhao, T. Chakraborty, Z. Wang, S. Wang, J. Li, L. Li, F. Huang, Y. Ji, X. Li, & M. Li Exacerbated heat stress induced by urban browning in the Global South. Nature Cities 2, 157-169, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1038/s44284-024-00184-9 (2025).\nK. Zhang, L. Zhao, K. Oleson, X. Li, X. Lee Enhancing Urban Thermal Environment and Energy Sustainability With Temperature-Adaptive Radiative Roofs. Earth's Future 13 (1), e2024EF005246 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1029/2024EF005246 (2025).\nY. Cheng, L. Zhao, T. Chakraborty, K. Oleson, M. Demuzere, X. Liu, Y. Che, W. Liao, Y. Zhou, & X. Li U-Surf: a global 1 km spatially continuous urban surface property dataset for kilometer-scale urban-resolving Earth system modeling, Earth Syst. Sci. Data, DOI: https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-2024-416 (2025).\nL. Wang, Y. Liu, L. Zhao, X. Lu, L. Huang, Y. Jin, S. Davis, A. Aghakouchak, X. Huang, T. Zhu, Y. Qin Unraveling climate change-induced compound low-solar-low-wind extremes in China. National Science Review 12 (1), nwae424 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwae424 (2025).\nY. Sun, B. Fang, K.W. Oleson, L. Zhao, D.O. Topping, D.M. Schultz, & Z. Zheng Improving urban climate adaptation modeling in the Community Earth System Model (CESM) through transient urban surface albedo representation. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems 16, e2024MS004380 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1029/2024MS004380 (2024).\nT. Chakraborty, Z. Venter, M. Demuzere, W. Zhan, J. Gao, L. Zhao, & Y. Qian Large disagreements in estimates of urban land across scales and their implications. Nature Communications 15, 9165 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52241-5 (2024).\nX. Li, L. Zhao, Y. Qin, K. Oleson, & Y. Zhang Elevated urban energy risks due to climate-driven biophysical feedbacks. Nature Climate Change 14, 1056-1063 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-024-02108-w (2024).\nN. Nazarian, B. Bechtel, G. Mills, M. Hart, A. Middel, E.S. Krayenhoff, G. Langendijk, L. Zhao, A. Pitman, & W. Chow Integration of urban climate research within the global climate change discourse. PLoS Climate 3 (8), e0000473 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pclm.0000473 (2024).\nG. Huang, K. He, X. Lyu, Y. Zang, H. Shu, L. Zhao, J. Li, C. Wang High precision Intracity Temperature Estimation based on Generated Point Clouds. IEEE Transactions on Geoscience and Remote Sensing 62, 5705615 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2024.3439608 (2024).\nX. Li, L. Zhao, K.W. Oleson, et al. Enhancing Urban Climate-Energy Modeling in the Community Earth System Model (CESM) through Explicit Representation of Urban Air-conditioning Adoption. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems 16 (4), e2023MS004107 DOI: https://doi.org/10.22541/essoar.169945607.70663662/v1 (2024).\nL Yang, Q Li, Q Li, L Zhao, Z Luo, Y Liu Different explanations for surface and canopy urban heat island effects in relation to background climate. iScience 27 (3) DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2024.108863 (2024).\nH Du, W Zhan, Z Liu, ES Krayenhoff, TC Chakraborty, L Zhao, L Jiang, P Dong, L Li, F Huang, S Wang, Y Xu Global mapping of urban thermal anisotropy reveals substantial potential biases for remotely sensed urban climates. Science Bulletin 68 (16), 1809-1818 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scib.2023.06.032 (2023).\nL. Gray, L. Zhao, A. Stillwell Impacts of climate change on global total and urban runoff. Journal of Hydrology, 620 (A), 129352 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2023.129352 (2023).\nF. Li, Q. Zhu, W.J. Riley, L. Zhao, L. Xu, K. Yuan, M. Chen, H. Wu, Z. Gui, J. Gong, J. Randerson AttentionFire_v1.0: interpretable machine learning fire model for burned-area predictions over tropics. Geoscientific Model Development, 16 (3), 869-884 DOI: https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-16-869-2023 (2023).\nJ. Yang, L. Zhao, K. Oleson Large humidity effects on urban heat exposure and cooling challenges under climate change. Environmental Research Letters, 18(4), 044024 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/acc475 (2023).\nK. Zhang, C. Cao, H. Chu, L. Zhao, J. Zhao, X. Lee Increased heat risk in wet climate induced by urban humid heat. Nature, 617, 738-742 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-05911-1 (2023).\nH. Lin, Y. Li, L. Zhao Partitioning of sensible and latent heat fluxes in different vegetation types and their\r\nspatiotemporal variations based on 203 FLUXNET sites. Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres\r\n127 (21), e2022JD037142 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1029/2022JD037142 (2022).\nD. Wu, W. Liu, B. Fang, L. Chen, Y. Zang, L. Zhao, S. Wang, C. Wang, J. Marcato, J. Li Intracity\r\nTemperature Estimation by Physics Informed Neural Network Using Modeled Forcing Meteorology and\r\nMultispectral Satellite Imagery. IEEE Transactions on Geoscience and Remote Sensing 60, 1-15 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2022.3201284 (2022).\nQ. Zhu, F. Li, W.J. Riley, L. Xu, L. Zhao, K. Yuan, H. Wu, J. Gong, J. Randerson Building a machine learning surrogate model for wildfire activities within a global Earth system model. Geoscientific Model Development 15 (5), 1899-1911 DOI: https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-15-1899-2022 (2022).\nJ. Zhu, X. Li, Y. Fan, H. Shi, L. Zhao Effect of ca...[truncated]"
