    "homepage_text": "Post-Doctoral Fellow\nThe Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong, China.\n[\nGoogle Scholar\n] [\nGitHub\n]\nBiography\nHe got his Ph.D. degree in Sep. 2023 in the College of Computer Science and Technology at Zhejiang University, advised by Prof.\nJiajun Bu\nand Prof.\nHaishuai Wang\n.  \n    His research interests are transfer learning, and graph representation learning, with their healthcare applications, e.g., video analyses for infant healthcare, ultrasonic cardiograms, and surgical intervention scenarios. He is also a junior reviewer for top-tier conferences from 2020, such as ICLR, NeurIPS, ICML, CVPR, ICCV and ECCV.\nSelected Publications\nNing Ma\n, Jiajun Bu, Jieyu Yang, Zhen Zhang, Chengwei Yao, Zhi Yu, Sheng Zhou, Xifeng Yan.\nAdaptive-Step Graph Meta-Learner for Few-Shot Graph Classification.\nIn The Conference on Information and Knowledge Management (CIKM), 2020.\n[\ncode\n]\nNing Ma\n, Hongyi Zhang, Xuhui Li, Sheng Zhou, Zhen Zhang, Jun Wen, Haifeng Li, Jingjun Gu, Jiajun Bu.\nLearning Spatial-Preserved Skeleton Representations for Few-Shot Action Recognition\n.\nIn The European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV), 2022.\n[\ncode\n]\nNing Ma\n, Jiajun Bu, Lixian Lu, Jun Wen, Sheng Zhou, Zhen Zhang, Jingjun Gu, Haifeng Li, Xifeng Yan.\nContext-guided entropy minimization for semi-supervised domain adaptation.\nIn Neural Networks.\n[\ncode\n]\nNing Ma\n,  Feng Liu, Haishuai Wang, Sheng Zhou, Bo Han, Jiajun Bu.\nGeneralizing to Unseen Domains for Regression.\nIn  submission.\nResearch Experiences\n2019.11-2023.09.\nEagle Lab\nat Zhejiang University. Working on\nhealth care project\nfor babies: \n\t\t    we are developing a method to detect early risk of the nervous system for infants via machine learning. The research is supported by the National Clinical Medical Research Center for Child Health and reported by\nThe Children's Hospital of Zhejiang University School of Medicine\n. The website is available by email request and the paper will also be released soon!\n2021.11- 2022.06.\nEagle Lab\nat Zhejiang University. Working on ultrasonic cardiogram project to achieve real-time quantitative video analysis for cardiac function. The research has got several\nawards\nand become a\nstartup\nfor cardiac \nfunction examination.\n2022.6-2023.6.   Visiting at\nTMLR Group\nin HKBU. Working on algorithm project for domain adaptation and generalization.\nAwards\nAnnual Outstanding Student of ZJU Eagle-Lab (Top 3), 2022\nOutstanding Doctoral Student Grant, 2020\nAdmission Scholarship for Doctoral Students, 2018\nNational Scholarship, 2016"
  },
  "serper::George Chen": {
    "homepage": "https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/georgech/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/georgehc",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/georgehchen",
    "homepage_text": "George H. Chen\nGeorge H. Chen\nAssociate Professor,\nHeinz College of Information Systems and Public Policy\nAffiliated Faculty,\nMachine Learning Department\nCarnegie Mellon University\nEmail: georgechen [at symbol] cmu.edu\nCV\nâ¢\nGoogle Scholar\nâ¢\nGitHub\nResearch overview:\nI study trustworthy machine learning methods for reasoning about\n            time, often in the context of health applications. Much of my work\n            is on predicting time durations before critical events happen (also\n            called \"time-to-event prediction\" or \"survival analysis\"), or on\n            analyzing time series such as electronic health records and EEG\n            data. I am interested in developing new methods for these\n            time-related problems as well as understanding when and why these\n            methods work in terms of statistical guarantees. In these\n            endeavors, I have worked extensively on \"nonparametric\" methods\n            that work under very few assumptions on the data.\nTime-to-event prediction/survival analysis\n:\n            How much time will elapse before a coma patient wakes up, a disease\n            relapses, or a customer cancels a subscription service? These time\n            durations are examples of what are called \"time-to-event outcomes\".\n            I have a\nnew book/monograph\n            (published December 2024)\nin\nFoundations and Trends in\n            Machine Learning\nthat aims to be a reasonably self-contained\n            introduction to deep learning methods for predicting time-to-event\n            outcomes, targeted toward a machine learning audience; a preprint\n            is on\narXiv\n.\n            Previously, I taught a\nsurvival\n            analysis tutorial\nat CHIL 2020 and at SIGMETRICS 2021, and I\n            co-organized a\nsurvival\n            analysis symposium\nas part of the 2023 AAAI Fall Symposium\n            Series.\nCoolCrop:\nI occasionally also work on machine learning for the developing world.\n            I co-founded and now am an advisor for\nCoolCrop\n, an AgriTech startup based\n            in India that works on providing farmers with cold storage units\n            (such as a refrigerator shared by a village) and market forecasts.\n            We currently serve over 9000 farmers across 7 states in India\n            at over 40 sites.\nMy educational background:\nPhD in\nElectrical Engineering and Computer Science\n, MIT, June 2015\nSM in\nElectrical Engineering and Computer Science\n, MIT, June 2012\nBS with dual majors in\nElectrical Engineering and Computer Sciences\nand in\nEngineering Mathematics and Statistics\n, UC Berkeley, May 2010\nTeaching:\nAt CMU in spring 2026 mini 4, I am teaching \"Unstructured Data\n            Analytics\" to public policy master's students (course number\n94-775\n) and to information\n            systems master's students\n            (\n95-865\n)."
  },
