    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "http://www.linkedin.com/in/stanley-h-chan",
    "homepage_text": "Stanley H. Chan\nIntelligent Imaging Lab\nHome\nProf Chan\nStudents\nOpportunities\nGallery\nResearch\nGenerative Photography\nPhoton-Limited Imaging\nImaging Through Turbulence\nLiDAR\nRobust Machine Learning\nPlug and Play ADMM\nOther Projects\nPublication\nSoftware\nSeminar and Reading Group\nComputational Imaging Seminar\nMachine Learning Reading Grp\nBook\nIntro to Probability\nImaging through Turbulence\nDiffusion Models for Imaging and Vision\nTeaching\nSummer Outreach\nECE 295 Python for Data Science\nECE 302 Intro to Probability\nECE 513 Fourier Optics and Cameras\nECE 50024 Machine Learning\nECE 645 Estimation Theory\nSTAT/ECE 695 Sparse Modeling\nGeneral Advices\nWhich Purdue courses to take?\nWriting journal review\nWriting graduate statement\nHow to write good papers?\nStanley H. Chan\nElmore Professor of\nElectrical and Computer Engineering\nand\nStatistics\nPurdue University\nContact Information:\nSchool of Electrical and Computer Engineering,\n465 Northwestern Ave,\nWest Lafayette, IN 47907-2035.\nOffice: MSEE 338\nPhone: +1 (765) 496 0230\nEmail: stanchan AT purdue DOT edu\nFollow me at:\nEducation\nPostdoctoral Research Fellow, Harvard University, 2012-2014\nPhD in Electrical Engineering, University of California, San Diego, 2011\nMA in Mathematics, University of California, San Diego, 2009\nBEng in Electrical Engineering, University of Hong Kong, 2007\nBio Sketch\nStanley H. Chan is an Elmore Professor in the\nSchool of \r\nElectrical and Computer Engineering\nand the\nDepartment of Statistics\nat\nPurdue University\n, West Lafayette, IN.\nDr. Chan received the Ph.D. degree in Electrical Engineering and the M.A. \r\ndegree in Mathematics from the\nUniversity of California at \r\nSan Diego\n, in 2011 and 2009, respectively, and the B.Eng. degree (with first \r\nclass honor) in Electrical Engineering from the\nUniversity of Hong Kong\nin 2007. He was a postdoctoral research fellow at\nHarvard John A. Paulson School of Engineering \r\nand Applied Sciences\nfrom 2012 to 2014. He joined Purdue University in 2014.\nDr. Chan is the recipient of the IEEE Signal Proecssing Society Best Paper \r\nAward 2022 for the Plug-and-Play ADMM paper he co-authored with students \r\nXiran Wang and Omar Elgendy. He is the recipient of the Best Paper Award \r\nin IEEE International Conference on Image Processing 2016 (with PhD \r\nstudent Omar Elgendy), and 2025 (with PhD student Prateek Chennuri). \r\nHe has won numerous teaching awards, including the \r\nRuth and Joel Sipra Outstanding Teaching Award in 2019, Purdue College of \r\nEngineering Exceptional Early Career Teaching Award in 2019, Purdue Teaching \r\nfor Tomorrow Fellow, 2018, Eta Kappa Nu (HKN) Purdue Outstanding Professor \r\nAward in 2018, Purdue College of Engineering Outstanding Graduate Mentor \r\nAward in 2016, and Eta Kappa Nu (HKN) Purdue Outstanding Teaching Award 2015. \r\nPrior to joining Purdue, he was a recipient of the Croucher Foundation \r\nPostdoctoral fellowship, and the Croucher Foundation PhD Scholarship.\nHe is currently serving on the editorial board of the IEEE Transactions on \r\nComputational Imaging (Deputy Editor in Chief, 2025-present, Senior Area Editor \r\n2022-2024, Associate Editor 2019-2022), and IEEE Open Journal on Signal Processing (Senior Area \r\nEditor, 2022-2025). He was a former Associate Editor of the OSA Optics \r\nExpress (2016-2019). Dr. Chan is the Chair of IEEE Signal Processing \r\nSociety Technical Committee on Computational Imaging (2026-2027). Previously, \r\nhe was an elected member of the IEEE Signal \r\nProcessing Society Technical Committee on Image, Video, and Multidimensional \r\nSignal Processing (2022-2025). He is the recipient of the IEEE Signal Processing Society \r\nOutstanding Editorial Board Award 2021, and the CVPR 2022 Outstanding \r\nReviewer Award.\nDr. Chan does research in computational imaging. His research mission is to \r\nbuild intelligent cameras by co-desigining sensors and algorithms to enable \r\nseeing in all imaging conditions. His research is supported by the National \r\nScience Foundation, Air Force Research Lab, Army Research Office, and other \r\nfederal and industrial sponsors.\nProfessional Service\nIEEE Senior Member\nDeputy Editor in Chief,\nIEEE Trans. Comp. Imaging\n, 2025-present.\nSenior Area Editor,\nIEEE Trans. Comp. Imaging\n, 2022-2025.\nSenior Area Editor,\nIEEE Open Journal of Signal Processing\n, 2022-2025.\nAssociate Editor,\nIEEE Trans. Comp. Imaging\n, 2018-2022.\nAssociate Editor,\nOSA Optics Express\n, 2016-2018.\nChair, IEEE Signal Processing Society\nComputational Imaging Technical Committee\n, 2026-2027\nVice Chair, IEEE Signal Processing Society\nComputational Imaging Technical Committee\n, 2024-2025\nMember, IEEE Signal Processing Society\nComputational Imaging Technical Committee\n, 2015-2017, 2018-2020\nMember, Steering Committee IEEE ICIP (2025 - 2027)\nTechnical Program Committee: IEEE ICIP 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2023, 2024, 2025\nTechnical Program Committee: IEEE ICASSP 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2024, 2025\nFinance Chair, IEEE MMSP 2024\nTechnical Program Committee:\nOSA Imaging and Applied Optics Congress, Mathematics in Imaging 2018\nTechnical Program Committee:\nMidwest Machine Learning Symposium 2018\nCo-Organizer and Co-Chair, Special Session on Integration of Imaging Systems and Computational Algorithms in\nICIP 2016\nCo-Organizer,\nIMA Computational Imaging 2019\n.\nAwards\nIEEE ICIP, Best Paper Award, 2025\nDARPA CIDAR Challenge White Paper Winner 2025\nSamsung Global Research Outreach Award 2022\nBest Paper Award, IEEE Signal Processing Society, 2022\nOutstanding reviewer for CVPR 2022\nIEEE Signal Processing Society Outstanding Editorial Board Member 2021\nRuth and Joel Spira Outstanding Teaching Award 2019\nPurdue College of Engineering Exceptional Early Career Teaching Award 2019\nPurdue Teaching for Tomorrow Fellow, 2018\nEta Kappa Nu (HKN) Purdue Outstanding Professor Award, 2018\nIEEE ICIP, Best Paper Award, 2016\nSecond Prize, IEEE Signal Processing Cup 2016\nPurdue College of Engineering Outstanding Graduate Mentor Award, 2016\nEta Kappa Nu (HKN) Purdue Outstanding Teaching Award, 2015\nCroucher Foundation Postdoc Fellowship 2012-2013\nCroucher Foundation PhD Scholarship 2008-2010\nPage generated 2025-12-24 11:08:01 Eastern Standard Time, by\njemdoc\n."
  },
  "serper::Shan Chen": {
    "homepage": "https://shanchen.dev/",
    "emails": [
      "bittermanlab@gmail.com"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/shan23chen",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/schen73/",
    "homepage_text": "Shan Chen\nHome\nThesis\nPublications\nMentoring\nHonors\nKyudo retreat @ NY 2023 Spring.\nGoogle Scholar\nGithub\nLinkedIn\nTwitter\nHarvard Website\nContact\n''.join(list('ude.dravrah@358chs')[::-1])\nRead My Thesis\nFull web version with chapters, figures, and references\nView My Application Materials\nIn the interest of improving equity in access to application materials\nHi, I am a Ph.D. student at\nHarvard-MGB AIM\n, jointly\n                        with Maastricht University, under the guidance of\nHugo Aerts, Ph.D.\nand\nDanielle S. Bitterman, M.D.\nMy works are supported\n                        by the\n2024 Google PhD Fellowship in Natural Language Processing\n.\n                        I am also affiliated with the\nBoston Children's Hospital\n                            Computational Health Informatics Program (CHIP)\n, where we have the privilege of\n                        collaborating closely with\nGuergana\n                            Savova, Ph.D.\nand\nTim Miller, Ph.D.\nMy research bridges technical rigor and clinical responsibility, balancing the drive to\nenhance AI capabilities\nwith the need to\nmitigate risks\n. I probe the foundational\n                        limitations of LLMs—uncovering vulnerabilities like\ndata bias\nand hallucinations—while\n                        creating scalable benchmarks to measure real progress. Simultaneously, I focus on\nlast-mile\n                            alignment\nand robust safety evaluations for high-stakes tasks, aiming to transform opaque models into\n                        transparent, reliable\n                        instruments that improve patient care and trust.\nMy research has been featured in major media outlets such as Bloomberg, The New York Times, NBC,\n                        and New Scientist, among others. It has also been highlighted by government agencies including\n                        the FDA, NCI, and NIH, and has been cited in U.S. congressional hearings.\nDuring COVID-19, I completed with M.S. in Computational Linguistics from\nBrandeis University\n, where I was\n                        fortunate to be advised by Professor\nNianwen Xue Ph.D.\nwhere I fully explored my interests and met many\n                        wonderful people and friends.\n                        Before Brandeis, I spent 4 years as an undergraduate in Math, Japanese and Linguistics at\nSt. Olaf College\nenjoying the snow!\nDuring my free time, I enjoy basketball, dragonboat and kyudo 🏹.\nIf you want to work with me or my group, please email\nbittermanlab@gmail.com\ninstead!\nNews\n[11/11/2025]\nOur work \"When helpfulness backfires: LLMs and the risk of false medical information due to\n                            sycophantic behavior\" got hightlight on New York Times, Nature and oral at AMIA 2025!\n[01/04/2025]\nOur paper on using LLMs to identify social determinants of health in electronic health\n                            records was the most cited journal-wide (Nature/NPJ Digital Medicine) in 2024! This paper\n                            was also selected for the AI and Data Science Year in Review 2024 at AMIA!\n[11/11/2024]\nHeading to EMNLP and wrote a\nblog\n                                post\non what we learnt this year on various things in AI4healthcare.\n[10/10/2024]\nOur paper \"WorldMedQA-V: a multilingual, multimodal medical examination dataset for\n                            multimodal language models evaluation\" is now available on\narXiv\n.\n[09/15/2024]\nHonored to receive the\n2024 Google PhD Fellowship in Natural\n                                Language Processing\n!\n[06/19/2024]\nRABBITS\nis out! We examined current biomedical benchmarks and found\n                            the language models are more familar with generic terms!\n[05/09/2024]\nCross-Care\nis out! The first grounded\n                            bias benchmark that analyzes how pre-training data impacts model misalignment with\n                            real-world medical concepts.\n[04/02/2024]\nLCD Benchmark is out! Try this long clinical documents benchmark that LLMs are bad at!\n[11/07/2023]\nOur SDoH paper got accepted at Nature Digital Medicine, front page featured article from\n                            Jan-May 2024. Highlight research at NCI!\n[08/24/2023]\nCheck out our work and editorial highlights @\nJAMA Onc\n. Also used during US congress hearing!\nSelected Publications\n(* indicates equal contribution)\nWhen helpfulness backfires: LLMs and the risk of false medical\n                            information due to sycophantic behavior\nShan Chen\n, Mingye Gao, Kuleen\n                            Sasse, Thomas Hartvigsen, Brian Anthony, ... Hugo Aerts, Jack Gallifant, Danielle S\n                            Bitterman\nnpj Digital Medicine | AMIA 2025 Oral\nTweet\nNature\n                                Featured\nNYTimes\nWhen Models Reason in Your Language: Controlling Thinking Trace\n                            Language Comes at the Cost of Accuracy\n*Jirui Qi,\n*Shan Chen\n, Zidi\n                            Xiong, Raquel Fernández, Danielle S. Bitterman, Arianna Bisazza\nEMNLP 2025\narXiv\nPDF\n🤗 Dataset\n🤗 Blog on\n                                improvement\nMedBrowseComp: Benchmarking Medical Deep Research and Computer\n                            Use\nShan Chen\n, Pedro Moreira, Yuxin\n                            Xiao, Sam Schmidgall, Jeremy Warner, Hugo Aerts, Thomas Hartvigsen, Jack Gallifant, Danielle\n                            S. Bitterman\narXiv 2025\narXiv\nPDF\nProject Page\nMeasuring the Faithfulness of Thinking Drafts in Large Reasoning\n                            Models\nZidi Xiong,\nShan Chen\n, Zhenting\n                            Qi, Himabindu Lakkaraju\nNeurips 2025\narXiv\nPDF\nKScope: A Framework for Characterizing the Knowledge Status of\n                            Language Models\nYuxin Xiao,\nShan Chen\n, Jack\n                            Gallifant, Danielle Bitterman, Thomas Hartvigsen, Marzyeh Ghassemi\nNeurips 2025\narXiv\nPDF\nSparse autoencoder features for classifications and\n                            transferability\n*Jack Gallifant,\n*Shan Chen\n,\n                            Kuleen Sasse, Hugo Aerts, Thomas Hartvigsen, Danielle S Bitterman\nEMNLP 2025\narXiv\nPDF\n🌏 WorldMedQA-V: a multilingual, multimodal medical examination\n                            dataset for multimodal language models evaluation\n*Joao Matos,\n*Shan Chen\n, ... A.\n                            Ian Wong, Danielle S. Bitterman, and Jack Gallifant\nNAACL 2025\n🤗 Dataset\nTweet\narXiv\nCode\n🐰 RABBITS: Language Models are Surprisingly Fragile to Drug\n                            Names in Biomedical Benchmarks\n*Jack Gallifant,\n*Shan Chen\n,\n                            Pedro Moreira, ... Thomas Hartvigsen, and Danielle S. Bitterman\nEMNLP 2024\n🤗\nTweet\narXiv\nIndustrial adaptation\nCode\nCross-Care: Assessing the Healthcare Implications of Pre-training\n                            Data on Language Model Bias\n*Shan Chen\n, *Jack Gallifant,\n                            Mingye Gao, Pedro Moreira, ... William G. La Cava, and Danielle S. Bitterman\nNeurips 2024\nWebsite\nCode\nData\nLCD Benchmark: Long Clinical Document Benchmark on Mortality\n                            Prediction for Language Models\nWonjin Yoon,\nShan Chen\n, ...\n                            Danielle S. Bitterman, Majid Afshar, and Timothy Miller\nJAMIA\nmedrXiv\nCode\nCoda Bench\nOncQA: The impact of using an AI chatbot to respond to patient\n                            questions\nShan Chen\n, Marco Guevara ...\n                            Hugo Aerts, Timothy Miller, Guergana Savova, Raymond Mak, Majid Afshar, and Danielle S.\n                            Bitterman\nLancet Digital Health\narXiv\nCode\n🤗\nArticle\nNYTimes\nMeasuring Pointwise V-Usable Information In-Context-ly\nSheng Lu,\nShan Chen\n, Yingya Li,\n                            Danielle S. Bitterman, Guergana Savova, and Iryna Gurevych\nEMNLP 2023\nEMNLP\nCode\nTweet\nTutorial\nLarge Language Models to Identify Social Determinants of Health\n                            in Electronic Health Records\n*Marco Guevara,\n*Shan Chen\n,\n                            Spencer Thomas ... Hugo Aerts, Guergana Savova, Raymond Mak, and Danielle S. Bitterman\nNature Digital Medicine\nFeatured & most cited journal wide in 2024\nPaper\nDataset\narXiv\nCode\n🤗\nUse of Artificial Intelligence Chatbots for Cancer Treatment\n                            Information\nShan Chen\n, Benjamin Kann,\n                            Michael Foote, Hugo Aerts, Guergana Savova, Raymond Mak and Danielle S. Bitterman\nJAMA ONC\narXiv\nCode\nData\nArticle\nNews\nEvaluation of ChatGPT Family of Models for Biomedical Reasoning\n                            and Classification\n*Shan Chen\n, *Yingya Li, Sheng\n                            Lu, ... Hugo Aerts, Guergana Savova, and Danielle S. Bitterman\nJAMIA\nJAMIA\narXiv\nCode\nNatural language processing to automatically extract the presence\n                            and severity of esophagitis in notes of patients undergoing radiotherapy\nShan Chen\n, Marco Guevara,\n                            Nicolas Ramirez ... Hugo Aerts, Tim Miller, Guergana Savova, Raymond Mak, and Danielle S.\n                            Bitterman\nJCO CCI\nOral Presentation @ ASTRO 2023 < 9%\nASTRO\nPaper\narXiv\nCode\nMedications detection in tweets using transformer networks and\n                            multi-task learning\nDongfang Xu,\nShan Chen\n, and Tim\n                            Miller\nProceedings of the BioCreative VII Challenge 2021\n🏆 First Place\nPaper\narXiv\nCode\nMentoring\nStudents and projects\nPedro Moreria - MIT Master Thesis student, Now MLE at Google\nKuleen Sasse - Undergrad student, Now PhD at JHU\nShayan Chowdhury - Undergrad student at Columbia\nJavier Mora, MD - Harvard Medical School Residency research year 2025\nKraig Tou - Master student, Now MLE at AWS Annapurna Labs\nYanan (Lance) Lu - Harvard DBMI Master Thesis student, Now MLE at Tiktok\nNikolaj Munch - Master Thesis student, Now Chief AI Advisor at The Ministry of Foreign\n                                Affairs of Denmark\nVikram Goddla - High school student, Now at Harvard College\nHonors and Service\nHonors\nGoogle...[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Zhenzhe Zheng": {
    "homepage": "https://zhengzhenzhe220.github.io/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Zhenzhe Zheng (郑臻哲）\nZhenzhe Zheng\nHome\nResearch\nGroup\nTeaching\nHonors\nServices\nResume\nZhenzhe Zheng (郑臻哲）\nProfessor,\nPh.D. Advisor,\nSchool of Computer Science\n,\nShanghai Jiao Tong University\n3-509 SEIEE Building\n800 Dongchuan Road\nShanghai 200240, P.R. China\nE-mail:\nzhengzhenzhe\n[@] sjtu [DOT] edu [DOT] cn\nAbout me\nWelcome to my homepage! I am currently a  professor in the\nSchool of Computer Science\n@\nShanghai Jiao Tong University\n.\nI received my Ph.D. from the\nCS\ndepartment @\nShanghai Jiao Tong University\nin March 2018.\nMy advisors were Prof.\nFan Wu\nand\nProf. Guihai Chen\n.\nI was a visiting research scholar and then a postdoctoral researcher in the\nCoordinated Science Laboratory (CSL)\n@\nUIUC\nfrom September 2016 to October 2019,\nadvised by Prof.\nR. Srikant\n.\nI am the recipient of the\nChina Computer Federation (CCF) Excellent Doctoral Dissertation Award\nin 2018. During my Ph.D. program, I was supported by\nGoogle Ph.D. Fellowship\nand\nMicrosoft Research Asia Ph.D. Fellowship\n.\nMy research interests are in the intersection of economics, learning and system. In particular, I have focused on game theory, machine learning, mobile computing and their applications for Agentic AI system, such as On-device AI Agents and Strategic AI Agents with applications to personal AI assistants and online platform markets.\nStudents & Postdoc\n: I am constantly looking for well motivated students (both undergrad and graduate) and Postdoc who are interested in\ngame theory, machine learning and reinforcement learning, mobile computing, performance analysis and algorithm design, and system implementations.\nPlease email me if you are interested.\nRecent News\n[2026.01] We have two papers “\nSmaller but Better: Plasticity-Preserving On-Device\nContinual Learning for Embedded AI\n” and “\nA Creator-Aware Recommendation System for Content Platforms\n” accepted to ACM\nWWW\n, 2026, Congratulations to Chenxin and Wenjing!\n[2025.12] Our Paper “\n”Resource-Efficient LLM Customization on Mobile Devices through Proxy Submodel Tuning\n\" has been accepted to IEEE/ACM Transactions on Networking (\nTON\n). Congratulations to Yan!\n[2025.12] I will serve as a TPC member for ACM MobiHoc 2026.\n[2025.12] Our Paper “\nGuiding the Recommender: Information-Aware Auto-Bidding for Content Promotion\n” has been accepted to ACM\nSIGMETRICS\n2026. Congratulations to Yumou!\n[2025.12] Our Paper “\nBudget-Constrained Federated Bandits for Mobile Applications\n” has been accepted to IEEE\nINFOCOM\n2026. Congratulations to Anran!\n[2025.09] Our Paper “\nOptimizing Storage Overhead of User Behavior Log for ML-embedded Mobile Apps\n” has been accepted to ACM\nSIGMETRICS\n2026. Congratulations to Chen!\n[2025.09] Our Paper “\nOptimizing Feature Extraction for On-Device Model Inference with User Behavior Sequences\n” has been accepted to ACM\nSenSys\n2026. Congratulations to Chen!\n[2025.08] We have two papers “\nOnline Contract Design for Creator Economy in the Era of GenAI\n” and “\nDRAGON: Enhancing On-Device Model Performance with Distributed Retrieval-Augmented Generation\n” accepted to ACM\nMobiHoc\n, 2025, Congratulations to Yumou and Shangyu!\n[2025.07] We have two papers “\nUser Context Generation for Large Language Models from Mobile Sensing Data\n” and “\nFrom Non-IID to IID: Mobility-aware Hierarchical Federated Learning with Client-Edge Association Control\n” accepted to IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing (\nTMC\n), Congratulations to Rui and Haibo!\n[2025.05] We have five papers accepted to ACM\nSIGKDD\n, 2025, February Cycle. Congratulations to Chen, Qilong, Chufeng, Yidan and Jianqing!\n[2025.01] Our Paper “\nEnabling Real-Time Inference in Online Continual Learning via Device-Cloud Collaboration\n” has been accepted to ACM\nWWW\n2025. Congratulations to Haibo!\n[2025.01] I will serve as a TPC member for ACM MobiHoc 2025, IEEE WiOpt 2025, ACM KDD 2025, IJCAI 2025.\nResearch Interests\nAgentic AI\nOn-Device AI Agents & Personal Intelligence\nStrategic AI Agents & Decision Intelligence"
  },
