    "homepage_text": "Home - Ren Wang\nRen Wang\nAssistant Professor\nFollow\nChicago, IL, US\nGoogle Scholar\nHome\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Electrical and Computer Engineering\nIllinois Institute of Technology, Chicago, IL 60616\nThe Institute for Data, Econometrics, Algorithms, and Learning\nEmail: rwang74 (you can make the “at”) illinoistech (dot) edu\nOpenings\nProspective graduate students:\nI am looking for highly motivated PhD/Master students with a background in Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning/Signal Processing/Mathematics/Power Systems. Please contact me along with your CV and transcript if you are interested.\nExternship/internship/visiting students:\nI am open to collaboration with highly motivated students. Interested candidates are strongly encouraged to contact me by email. Students interested in these positions must commit to a continuous project duration of\nat least five months\n. Please\nensure this requirement is met\nbefore reaching out to me.\nShort Bio\nRen Wang is an Assistant Professor in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at the Illinois Institute of Technology and a faculty member of the Institute for Data, Econometrics, Algorithms, and Learning. He was a postdoctoral research fellow (and a lecturer) in the Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science at the University of Michigan. He received his Ph.D degree from the Department of Electrical, Computer, and Systems Engineering at Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute. He received both his bachelor’s degree and master’s degree in Electrical Engineering from Tsinghua University. His research interests include Trustworthy Machine Learning, Population-Based Machine Learning, Machine Learning for Smart Grids, and Machine Learning for Biology and Healthcare. His long-term vision is to develop next-generation trustworthy and intelligent machine learning systems that accelerate progress in engineering and scientific discovery. (see research lab intro\nhere\n). Dr. Wang has published widely in top-tier conferences and journals spanning machine learning, signal processing, computer vision, power systems, and bioinformatics, and has served as an area chair for several premier conferences. He received the 2023 ORAU Ralph E. Powe Junior Faculty Enhancement Award and, as Principal Investigator, has led multiple research projects supported by U.S. federal agencies such as the NSF and DoE.\nWelcome! You’re the\nth\nvistor here.\nRecent Activities\n03/2026\nI was invited to give a talk on \"Welcome to the Intelligence Age: Building Artificial Brains and Keeping Them Safe\" for Techpalooza Events at Illinois Tech.\n03/2026\nOur paper on PINNs-Based Uncertainty Quantification was accepted by CEEGE 2026.\n03/2026\nI was invited to give talks on AI trustworthiness through collective intelligence at the Workshop on UAV-based Computing Platform and the Department of Data Science, City University of Hong Kong.\n03/2026\nThree papers on Dimension-Wise Interpretability and Domain Decomposition for PINNs, Geometry-Aware Evolutionary Attacks, and Multimodal LLMs Jailbreaks have been accepted to ICLR 2026 workshops. Congrats to Xu, Hyo Seo, Gang, and Shuyuan!\n02/2026\nOur paper on Data-Driven Control of T-Product-Based Dynamical Systems was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control.\n01/2026\nOur paper \"MuSHIN: A multi-way SMILES-based hypergraph inference network for metabolic model reconstruction\" was accepted by Communications Biology (Nature). Congrats to Yanlong and Yixiao!\n01/2026\nOur paper \"Post-hoc Popularity Bias Correction in GNN-based Collaborative Filtering\" was accepted by WWW 2026 (acceptance rate 676/3370=20.1%). Congrats to Aminul!\n12/2025\nOur paper on Data-driven model order reduction was accepted by Automatica.\n11/2025\nI was invited to give a talk at the UIUC in the\n\"Machine Learning Seminar\"\n.\n10/2025\nI was invited by the\n\"\"IEEE Systems Council – Early Career Speakers Program\"\"\nto give a talk on Population-Based Methods for Adversarially Resilient Deep Models.\n10/2025\nI served as the local arrangements chair of\n\"The 22nd IEEE International Conference on Mobile Ad-Hoc and Smart Systems (MASS 2025, IEEE Computer Society)\"\n, which was held in Chicago from Oct.6-8.\n10/2025\nI am serving as an Area Chair for ICLR 2026.\n10/2025\nI attended the EcoCAR Year 4 Fall Workshop.\n09/2025\nOur paper DREAM-GNN: Dual-route embedding-aware graph neural networks for drug repositioning was accepted by Briefings in Bioinformatics. Congrats to Yanlong!\n09/2025\nWe are pleased to announce new computational resource support from Argonne National Laboratory for our population-based machine learning project.\n09/2025\nPaul (High school student under my supervision, sophomore at IMSA) developed engaging educational websites focused on 1. Jailbreak attacks/defenses on LLMs (\n\"Website on Jailbreaks\"\n); 2. Neural network adversarial/backdoor robustness (\n\"Website on adversarial attacks and backdoor attacks\"\n) as part of his high school project, specifically designed for high school and university students.\n09/2025\nOur paper on Identifying Backdoored Graphs in Graph Neural Network Training was accepted by the IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics & Security. Congrats to Jane!\n07/2025\nOur paper on Efficient and Robust Continual Graph Learning was accepted by the IEEE Transactions on Signal and Information Processing over Networks. Congrats to Ding!\n06/2025\nGrateful for receiving\nfunding from the U.S. Department of Energy (DOE)\nfor the 2MC (Midwest Center for Microgrid Cybersecurity) project!\n06/2025\nI co-organized the\n\"Midwest Machine Learning Symposium (MMLS) 2025\"\n.\n06/2025\nYingdan's two papers - one on Breaking Machine Unlearning Weight Entanglement and the other on Rethinking Evaluation Metrics for Machine Unlearning have been accepted to ICML 2025 Workshop on Machine Unlearning for Generative AI. Congratulations, Yingdan!\n05/2025\nXu's paper on Optimizing Robustness and Accuracy in Mixture of Experts: A Dual-Model Approach is accepted to ICML 2025 (acceptance rate=3228/12000=26.9%). Congratulations, Xu!\n05/2025\nI co-organized the\n\"Workshop on Efficient Machine Learning and Optimization\"\n.\n02/2025\nOur paper Prediction Shift Uncertainty Unlocks Backdoor Detection was accepted by CVPR 2025 (acceptance rate=2878/13008=22.01%). Congrats to Wei!\n02/2025\nOur paper on Purification Of Contaminated Convolutional Neural Networks Via Robust Recovery was accepted by the IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Signal Processing. Congrats to Hanxiao and Zeyu!\n02/2025\nOur paper on Revisiting Mode Connectivity in Neural Networks with Bezier Surface was accepted by ICLR 2025 (acceptance rate=3689/11500=32.08%). Congrats to Jie!\n01/2025\nOur paper on Heterogeneous Entity Representation for Medicinal Synergy Prediction was accepted by the Bioinformatics.\n01/2025\nOur paper on Data-Driven Analysis of T-Product-Based Dynamical Systems was accepted by the IEEE Control Systems Letters.\n12/2024\nOur paper on Towards Uncertainty Estimation in Free-Form Medical Question Answering Applications was accepted by the International Journal of Intelligent Real-Time Automation.\n11/2024\nI am organizing a workshop on\n\"Privacy and Interpretability in Generative AI: Peering into the Black Box\"\n, which will be held on Nov.22 (Friday) in-person at Illinois Tech. If you're interested in the workshop, please consider registering for it (The registration is free).\n10/2024\nI am serving as an Area Chair for ICLR 2025.\n06/2024\nIDEAL is organizing the Annual Meeting and the Industry Day on June 6th and 7th. You are welcome to register for the\nevent here\n.\n04/2024\nJane receives\nfirst place in the Best Presentation Award for Ph.D. Research at 2024 ECE Day - Student Research Competition\n. Congrats! .\n04/2024\nOur paper on Deep Adversarial Defense Against Multilevel-Lp Attacks was accepted by 2024 IEEE International Workshop on Machine Learning for Signal Processing.\n02/2024\nOur paper on Towards Multi-Task Privacy Preserving In Deep Learning was accepted by IEEE Access.\n01/2024\nOur paper on Identifying Backdoor Data with Optimized Scaled Prediction Consistency was accepted by ICLR 2024. Congrats to Soumeyadeep!\n01/2024\nIgnacio's paper on Comprehensive Analysis of PINNs for Power System Transient Stability was accepted by Electronics. Congrats!\n10/2023\nI am organizing the Frontiers of Data Science: Theory, Applications, and Trustworthiness Seminar at Illinois Tech.\n10/2023\nI attended the\n\"ASEE DELTA Junior Faculty Institute\"\n.\n09/2023\nI am organizing a workshop on\n\"Trustworthiness in the Presence of Adversaries and Strategic Agents in ML\"\n, which will be held on Oct.12 (Thursday) in-person at Northwestern. If you're interested in the workshop, please consider registering for it.\n08/2023\nI've been honored with the\nNSF FMitF Award\non\nTowards Verified Robustness and Safety in Power System-Informed Neural Networks\n. A huge thank you to NSF for the support!\n07/2023\nI was invited to give a talk on Deep Learning Attacks and Defenses at the Oak Ridge National Laboratory\n07/2023\nI enrolled in the IEEE PES GM 2023 Mentoring Program as an academic mentor\n06/2023\nI received the\n2023 ORAU Ralph E. Powe Junior Faculty Enhancement Award\n(35 among 167 applications from 93 institutions). Thanks, ORAU!\n04/2023\nOur paper on Enhancing Healthcare Model Trustworthiness through Theoretically Guaranteed One-Hidden-Layer CNN Purification was accepted by ICLR 2023 TML4H. Congrads to Hanxiao and Zeyu!\n03/2023\nI will serve as a guest editor for the upcoming special issue on\nAdvances in Enhancing Energy and Power System Stability and Control\nin the journal, Electronics (check our\nbanner\nand\nflyer\n). This is an incredible opportunity to contribute to the cutting-edge research in this field and I would like to invite you to join me in this endeavor. Please consider submitting your paper to this special issue!\n02/2023\nI received the\nNSF CISE Research Initiation Initiative (CRII) Award\n[\nNews\n] on\nImmune-Inspired Learning Foundations of Neural Network General Robustness\n....[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Andrew M. Dai": {
    "homepage": "https://marquisemergingleaders.com/andrew-m-dai-phd/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrewdai/",
    "homepage_text": "Andrew M. Dai, PhD - Emerging Leaders\nBack to all Leaders\nJuly 29, 2024\nTechnology\nAndrew M. Dai, PhD\nRELATED POSTS\nKevin Prior\nRead More »\nShivkumar Kaushik Murthy\nRead More »\nJeffrey A. Stivers, MS, PMP, Col, USMC (Ret.)\nRead More »\nNAME: Andrew M. Dai, PhD\nTitle: Principal Research Scientist, Director\nCompany: Google\nEducation: PhD in Machine Learning, The University of Edinburgh (2012); BA in Computer Science, University of Cambridge (2006)\nCareer History: Principal Research Scientist, Director, Google (2012); Software Engineer Intern, Google (2008, 2009); Software Developer, Autonomy (2006-2007); ISAAS Intern, EY (2005)\nAndrew Dai, Principal Research Scientist and Director at Google, has been recognized as a Marquis Emerging Leader for their contributions and achievements in the field of technology.\nAttributing his success to blending his extensive research abilities with software engineering, Dr. Dai has excelled since 2012 in his role at Google, where he focuses on advancing technology and product development and wields particular expertise in large language models and machine learning. As a testament to his prominence there, he was recognized with a Tech Impact Award from the company in 2023. In the lead-up to this appointment, he gained experience as a software engineer intern at Google while earning a PhD in machine learning from the University of Edinburgh. He also previously worked as a software developer at Autonomy and an ISAAS intern at EY.\nGrowing up in China, Dr. Dai built on a fascination he had with the immersive world of video games to explore a passion for artificial intelligence in his high school years. This early intrigue with AI’s ability to simulate human-like behavior sparked a lasting interest that led him to pursue a career in this field. Upon graduating from high school, he obtained a bachelor’s degree in computer science at the University of Cambridge in England, where he authored a paper on the effectiveness of training and fine-tuning a language model to improve its ability to understand language, resulting in state-of-the-art performance. This paper was widely cited in subsequent GPT papers and published throughout Europe, having a far-reaching impact on the technology community.\nOutside of his primary responsibilities, Dr. Dai has served as an area chair and peer reviewer for NeurIPS, nCino, and ICOR conferences and regularly donates to various charities. He also holds a dedicated membership with the Association for Computing Machinery. Looking toward the future, Dr. Dai aspires to assume leadership roles in a company and establish a start-up. His plans include leveraging AI to make substantial contributions to global advancements.\nLinkedI\nn"
  },
