  "serper::Jie Yin": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.google.com/site/csyinjie/",
    "emails": [
      "jie.yin@sydney.edu.au",
      "yin@jie.yin"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Welcome to A/Prof. Jie Yin's Homepage\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nWelcome to A/Prof. Jie Yin's Homepage\nHome\nJie Yin, Ph.D.\nAssociate Professor\nDiscipline of Business Analytics\nThe University of Sydney\nAddress\n: Room 4046, Abercrombie Building H70, The University of Sydney, NSW 2006, Australia\nEmail\n: jie.yin (AT) sydney.edu.au\nBiography\nI am an\nAssociate Professor of Machine Learning with the Discipline of Business Analytics at the University of Sydney, Australia. My primary research interests lie in machine learning and artificial intelligence, with applications to text analytics and natural language processing. I was a Senior Lecturer at the University of Sydney from 2018 to 2021. Prior to joining the University of Sydney, I worked as a Senior Research Scientist at Data61/CSIRO, leading innovative research on a wide range of research projects across various disciplines such as social media monitoring, healthcare, and environmental monitoring.\nI received the Ph.D. in Computer Science from the Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (HKUST), and Bachelor of Engineering and Bachelor of Economics from Xi’an Jiaotong University (XITU), China. I have published 70+ research papers in top-tier conferences and premier journals in the field of data mining and machine learning. I have served as Area Chair, Senior Program Committee, or Program Committee of the major conferences in my area including KDD, NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, AAAI, IJCAI, ICDM, CIKM, etc. I am an Associate Editor of IEEE Transactions on Big Data from 2021.\nResearch Interests\nMy main research interests include data mining, machine learning, and AI, with a focus on the following areas:\nGraph machine learning\nFew-shot and continual learning\nKnowledge graphs\nInterpretable AI\nSocial network and social media mining\nNews\nI have openings for PhD students (\n2026/2027 in-take\n) in the field of machine learning, data mining and their application to natural language processing.\nI am seeking highly motivated PhD students to work with me on these research topics.\nThe requirements of PhD applicants are as follows:\nBachelor or Master degree in Computer Science, Information Engineering, or Business Analytics in well-known universities. Preferences are given to applicants with some research experience in machine learning or large language models.\nGood programming skills in at least one mainstream language such as Python and C++.\nGood reading/writing skills in English. Preferences are given to applicants who score 6.5+ in IELTS, or equivalent in TOEFL.\nThose interested please send your CV, publication, and academic transcripts to A/Prof. Jie Yin at\njie.yin@sydney.edu.au\n.\nOther PhD scholarship information to read:\nUSYD general postgraduate research scholarships\nfor international students (\nlink\n)\nEnhanced Business School Research scholarship\ninformation (\nlink\n):\nStudents from China\n: You are likely eligible to apply for the USYD-CSC scholarship that has generous coverage for stipend and tuition fee waiver. More details can be found here (\nlink\n)\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "serper::Jun-Juan Zhu": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.google.com/view/junzhulab/home",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Jun Zhu Lab\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nGraduate students welcome!\nPostdoc job opening\nJun Zhu Lab\nWelcome to the Zhu Lab\nRecent Research Highlight\nzhulab web home slides\nWelcome to the Jun Zhu Lab at Penn State\nWe are a condensed matter experimental group that focuses  on the exploration of fundamental and novel electronic properties of low-dimensional materials and their nanostructures. Our current research interests include two-dimensional layered materials such as graphene and transitional metal chalcogenides. We are interested in manipulating the multiple internal electronic degrees of freedom of the materials to explore the formation of collective phenomena, such as the quantum Hall and fractional quantum Hall effects in a strong magnetic field, and the emergence of one-dimensional chiral and helical edge states as a result of topology. We employ surface functionalization, interface engineering and complex device structures to engineer new properties and device functionalities in layered materials and their heterostructures. Please browse the current and past research topics for more details and\ncontact us\nif you are interested in joining the lab or starting a collaborative project.\nGroup Video Osmond & Davey.mp4\nNews in the Zhu Lab\nDr. Dohun Kim joins the lab. Welcome! (March, 2026)\nCequn's paper reporting on Josephson junctions built using a via transfer approach is published in ACS Nanoscience Au. Congratulations! (December, 2025)\nMarcus\nreceived 3\nrd\nplace poster award winner at the 2025 Physics and Astronomy Congress, Congratulations!\nXiaoshu Ma\njoins the lab. Welcome! (\nSept\n, 2025)\nPatrick Barfield\npassed his comprehensive exam. Congratulations! (Aug, 202\n5\n)\nKe'\ns paper\nreporting a new type of two-component fractional quantum Hall effect has\nbeen accepted by\nPhys. Rev. X\n. Congratulations! (\nMay\n, 202\n5\n)\nMarcus Smith\njoins the lab as a REU student. Welcome back! (May, 2025)\nZach Naumovski\njoins the lab as a REU student. Welcome! (May, 202\n5\n)\nHeung Kit Kenny Yip joins the lab. Welcome! (March, 2025)\nZack Jernigan passed his comprehensive exam. Congratulations! (Aug, 2024)\nDr.\nKe Huang\nleft State College to take on a postdoc position at\nStanford\nUniversity. Congratulations and farewell! (\nAugust\n2024)\nDr. Cequn Li left State College to take on a postdoc position at Cornell University. Congratulations and farewell! (July 2024)\nKe's paper on high-temperature quantum valley Hall with quantized resistance and a topological switch appears in Science First Release. Congratulations! (July 2024)\nKe\ndefended his PhD dissertation. Congratulations, Dr. Huang! (May, 2024)\nDylan Davenport joins the lab as a REU student. Welcome! (May, 2024)\nCequn\ndefended his PhD dissertation. Congratulations, Dr. Li! (\nMay\n, 20\n24\n)\nPatrick Barfield joins the lab. Welcome! (May 2024)\nMarcus Smith joins the lab. Welcome! (Feb 2024)\nKe w\nas\nawarded David C. Duncan Graduate Fellowship and W. Donald Miller Grad Fellowship in Physics. Congratulations! (Jan, 2024)\nCequn\nwas\nawarded David C. Duncan Graduate Fellowship and W. Donald Miller Grad Fellowship in Physics. Congratulations! (Jan, 202\n4\n)\nHailong's paper on charge oscillations in\nbilayer graphene\nquantum Hall interferometers has been accepted by Nano Lett.\nCongratulations! (\nOct\n, 202\n3\n)\nZachary Jernigan\njoins the lab. Welcome! (May, 202\n3\n)\nJun starts her new job as an Associate Editor of Nano Letters. (Jan, 2023)\nKe\nand Cequn\nwere\nawarded David C. Duncan Graduate Fellowship in Physics. Congratulations! (Jan, 202\n3\n)\nCequn's paper on the proximity-induced superconductivity in (Bi,Sb)2Te3/graphene/gallium heterostructures has been accepted by Nat. Mat. Congratulations! (\nJan\n, 202\n3\n)\nHailong's paper on\nbilayer graphene-based Fabry-Pérot quantum Hall interferometers has been accepted\nby Nano Lett.\nCongratulations! (\nDec\n, 2022)\nMonalisa Yadav\npassed h\ner\ncomprehensive exam. Congratulations! (\nDec\n, 2022)\nChengqi Guo\npassed h\ner\ncomprehensive exam. Congratulations! (\nAug\n, 2022)\nCarlton Drew passed his comprehensive exam. Congratulations! (Jul, 2022)\nKe\n's paper on the even-denominator fractional quantum Hall states in bilayer graphene\nhas\nbeen accepted by PRX. Congratulations! (\nJun\n, 202\n2\n)\nEmma and Loc join the lab as REU students and will stay with us for the summer. Welcome! (Jun, 2022)\nDr. Hailong Fu departed State College to take on faculty position in Zhejiang University. Congratulations and farewell! (Feb, 2022)\nKe Huang was\nawarded Downsbrough Department Head's Chair in Physics Fellowship. Congratulations! (Jan, 2022)\nCequn Li was awarded Downsbrough Department Head's Chair in Physics Fellowship. Congratulations! (Jan, 2022)\nDr. Yangyang Chen joins the lab. Welcome!\n(Jan, 2022)\nNick Mitran joins the lab for REU summer research. Welcome!\n(\nJun\n, 2021)\nChengqi Guo\njoins the lab. Welcome! (May, 2021)\nHailong\n's paper on the gapless spin wave in the CAF state in bilayer graphene has been accepted by PRX. Congratulations! (Feb, 2021)\nMonalisa Yadav joins the lab. Welcome! (Feb, 2021)\nJun elected fellow of the APS. Congratulations! (Sept, 2020)\nKe Huang passed his comprehensive exam. Congratulations! (Jul, 2020)\nNSF announces the 2020 MRSEC centers!\nYangyang Chen defended her thesis. Congratulations! (Jun, 2020)\nYa-Wen Chuang defended her thesis. Congratulations! (Jun, 2020)\nLe Yi joins the lab. Welcome! (Jun, 2020)\nCarlton Drew joins the lab. Welcome! (Jun, 2020)\nAlumnus Dr. Jing Li received an Outstanding Dissertation Award from the International Organization of Chinese Physicists and Astronomers (OCPA). Congratulations! (Oct, 2019)\nBasyirah Zulkifli joins the lab. Welcome! (Feb, 2019)\nJing's paper on the nu=0 quantum Hall state of bilayer graphene has been accepted by PRL. Congratulations! (Feb, 2019)\nJing's topological valleytronics paper has been accepted by\nScience\n. Congratulations! (Oct, 2018)\nKe Huang joins the lab. Welcome! (Oct, 2018)\nDa Zhou joins the lab. Welcome! (Aug, 2018)\nYangyang Chen joins the lab. Welcome! (Sept, 2018)\nXianzhe Zeng joins the lab for summer research. Welcome! (Jul, 2018)\nEdward and Louis join the lab. Welcome! (Feb, 2018)\nOur new website is launched! (Feb, 2018)\nCequn Li joins the lab. Welcome! (Jan, 2018)\nEberly postdoc fellow Hailong Fu joins the lab. Welcome! (Aug, 2017)\nJing defended his PhD dissertation. Congratulations, Dr. Li! (Aug, 2017)\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "serper::Hang Zhou": {
