    "homepage_text": "Professor Sae-Young Chung - KAIST 전기 및 전자공학부\nKorean\nEnglish\nINTRANET\nSITEMAP\nLOGOUT\nMenu\nKorean\nEnglish\nINTRANET\nLOGOUT\nMenu\nINTRANET\nKorean\nEnglish\nINTRANET\nLOGOUT\nMenu\nPeople & Life\nTo Students\nHome\nPeople & Life\nStudent Activities\nFaculty\nFull-time Professor\nEmeritus Professor\nJoint-appointment/Adjunct Professor\nResearch Professor\nIn Memoriam\nStaff\nAdministration Team\nStudent Activities\nStudent Activities Introduction\nIntroduce representative commitee\nExecutive branch\nClubs\nComments to the Students\nRegular events\nEE International Office\nStudent Facilities\nWelfare and Facilities\nHaedong Lounge Guide\nTransportation\nOutstanding Lecture Award\nBest Paper Award\nDean's List\nProfessor Sae-Young Chung\n2015.07.21\n1635\nDuring my time at MIT, I came to realize that Korean students, especially the ones from KAIST, are outstanding and nothing short of the very best students at MIT. Judging by the qualities of the students, there should have been a number of Nobel Prize winners in Korea. But this is not the case. These outstanding students should keep their composure and look further into the future when developing new technologies. Students should conduct research based on fundamental theories, which are considered rather difficult, to increase their competitiveness. While KAIST students are outstanding in many ways, they should learn more to think outside of a box and regain their curiosity. KAIST should not be an institute for simply acquiring knowledge; rather, it should be a place where the students can learn to think creatively.\nPrev\nProf. Takgon Kim\nNext\nEukyoung Sung (Fellow EE student)\nShow List\nAbout Us\nOverview\nWelcome Message\nHistory\nOrganization Chart\nEE Division\nEE Visual Identity\nEE Publication\nEE Facilities\nMaps & Directions\nVirtual Campus Tour\nResearch\nResearch Areas\nResearch Centers\nResearch Highlights\nResearch Labs\nEE-X\nAI in EE\nAI in Wave Division\nAI in Communicat