  "serper::Xiaorui Liu": {
    "homepage": "https://archive.csc.ncsu.edu/people/xliu96",
    "emails": [
      "xliu96@ncsu.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Department of Computer Science at North Carolina State University\nDr.  Xiaorui Liu,\nAssistant Professor\n2296 EB II\nPhone: 919-513-7196\nxliu96@ncsu.edu\nWeb Site\nDr.  Xiaorui Liu\nAssistant Professor\n2296 EB II\nPhone: 919-513-7196\nxliu96@ncsu.edu\nWeb Site\nCampus Map\nBiography\nXiaorui Liu joined North Carolina State University in August 2022 as an Assistant Professor in Computer Science Department. He received his Ph.D. degree in Computer Science from Michigan State University in 2022. Before that, he received his Master and Bachelor degrees from South China University of Technology. He was awarded the Best Paper Honorable Mention Award at ICHI 2019, MSU Engineering Distinguished Fellowship in 2017, and Cloud Computing Fellowship in 2021. His research interests include large-scale machine learning, trustworthy artificial intelligence, and deep learning on graphs. He has published innovative works in top-tier conferences such as NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, KDD, AISTATS, and SIGIR. He also organized and co-presented five tutorials related to these research topics in KDD 2021, IJCAI 2021, ICAPS 2021, and WWW 2022. He regularly serves as organizer, (senior) PC member, and reviewer for multiple international conferences and journals in machine learning and data science such as ICML, NeurIPS, ICLR, KDD, AAAI, IJCAI, WWW, WSDM, MLSys, CIKM, TMLR, TNNLS, and TPAMI.\nResearch Areas\nAlgorithms and Theory of Computation\nArtificial Intelligence and Intelligent Agents\nData Sciences and Analytics\nParallel and Distributed Systems\nScientific and High Performance Computing\nEducation\nPh.D., 2022, Michigan State University\nM.S., 2017, South China University of Technology\nB.S., 2015, South China University of Technology\nCSC Spring Courses\nCSC 422-001 LEC Auto Lrn Data Anly    MW 4:30pm-5:45pm   Room: 1007  Engineering Building I\nCSC 591-038 LEC SP Topic CSC - Machine Learning with Graphs   W 4:30pm-7:15pm   Room: 1229  Engineering Building 2\nCSC 791-038 LEC Advncd Topics CSC - Machine Learning with Graphs   W 4:30pm-7:15pm   Room: 1229  Engineering Building 2\nPublications\nAvailable at\nGoogle Scholar\nor\nHomepage\n."
  },
  "serper::Yizhou Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~yizhou/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/yizhouzhang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/yizhouzhang",
    "homepage_text": "Yizhou Zhang\nYizhou Zhang\nAssistant Professor\nCheriton School of Computer Science\nUniversity of Waterloo\nMy research home base is programming languages, extending into formal verification and AI. Recent projects span language design, compilers, machine-assisted proofs, and probabilistic AI.\nI invite broad-minded, technically strong students\n(\nPhD\nand\nundergraduate\n)\nto join me in exploring fun ideas in PL research and beyond.\nGraduate Students\nJianlin Li\nCong Ma\nEnde Jin\nZhaoyi Ge\nAlumni\nOghenevwogaga Ebresafe\nSelected Peer-Reviewed Publications\nOOPSLA 2025\nZero-Overhead Lexical Effect Handlers\nNo raise, no pay: rethinking the cost of lexically scoped handlers for exceptional effects.\nCong Ma, Zhaoyi Ge, Jonghyun Jung, Yizhou Zhang\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n|\nLexa\n]\nICFP 2025\nCompiling with Generating Functions\nA compiler uses tools from combinatorics to compactly and exactly represent probabilistic programs.\nJianlin Li, Yizhou Zhang\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n|\nGeni\n]\nPLDI 2025\nCertified Compilers à la Carte\nExtensible, modular compiler proofs via new proof-assistant abstractions.\nOghenevwogaga Ebresafe, Ian Zhao, Ende Jin, Arthur Bright, Charles Jian, Yizhou Zhang\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n|\nRocqet\n]\nOOPSLA 2024\nLexical Effect Handlers, Directly\nCompiling high-level, modular control effects to low-level, swift stack switching\nCong Ma, Zhaoyi Ge, Edward Lee, Yizhou Zhang\n[\nfull paper\n|\nthe\nLexa\nlanguage\n]\nPLDI 2024\nCompiling Probabilistic Programs for Variable Elimination with Information Flow\nA compiler uses information-flow types to factorize recursive probabilistic programs for scalable inference.\nJianlin Li, Eric Wang, Yizhou Zhang\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n]\nOOPSLA 2024\nPersimmon: Nested Family Polymorphism with Extensible Variant Types\nReconciling algebraic data types with scalable extensibility\nAnastasiya Kravchuk-Kirilyuk, Gary Feng, Jonas Iskander, Yizhou Zhang, Nada Amin\n[\nfull paper\n]\nPLDI 2023\nExtensible Metatheory Mechanization via Family Polymorphism\nAn object-oriented idea lends itself to reusable proofs!\nEnde Jin, Nada Amin, Yizhou Zhang\nACM SIGPLAN Distinguished Paper Award\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n|\nimplementation\n]\nPOPL 2023\nType-Preserving, Dependence-Aware Guide Generation for\nSound, Effective\nAmortized Probabilistic Inference\nSymbolic methods (a type system and an analysis) aid in neural-network–based inference.\nJianlin Li, Leni Aniva, Pengyuan Shi, Yizhou Zhang\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n]\nPOPL 2022\nReasoning about “Reasoning about Reasoning”\nReasoning principles for probabilistic programs that model rational agents\nYizhou Zhang, Nada Amin\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n|\nmechanized proof\n]\nOOPSLA 2020\nHandling Bidirectional Control Flow\nHandlers for “effectful effects”? Yes, with a type system to defend abstractions.\nYizhou Zhang, Guido Salvaneschi, Andrew C. Myers\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n|\nmechanized proof\n]\nRA-L 2020\nPath Planning under Malicious Injections and Removals of\nPerceived Obstacles: a Probabilistic Programming Approach\nRobots plan paths against both noise and adversarial uncertainty.\nJacopo Banfi, Yizhou Zhang, G. Edward Suh, Andrew C. Myers, Mark Campbell\n[\nfull paper\n|\ncode\n]\nPOPL 2019\nAbstraction-Safe Effect Handlers via Tunneling\nAlgebraic effects (and hence exceptions, asynchrony, ...)\nmade modular\nYizhou Zhang, Andrew C. Myers\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n|\nmechanized proof\n]\nOOPSLA 2017\nFamilia: Unifying Interfaces, Type Classes, and\nFamily Polymorphism\nA lot of polymorphism and extensibility in a lightweight package\nYizhou Zhang, Andrew C. Myers\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n]\nPLDI 2016\nAccepting Blame for Safe Tunneled Exceptions\nThe flexibility of unchecked exceptions, the static guarantees\nof checked exceptions, and good performance too\nYizhou Zhang, Guido Salvaneschi, Quinn Beightol,\nBarbara Liskov, Andrew C. Myers\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n]\nPLDI 2015\nLightweight, Flexible Object-Oriented Generics\nAn expressive, lightweight genericity mechanism with better\ncode reuse and stronger static checking\nYizhou Zhang, Matthew C. Loring, Guido Salvaneschi,\nBarbara Liskov, Andrew C. Myers\n[\nfull paper\n|\nTR\n]\nPhD Thesis\nDesigning Flexible, Modular Linguistic Abstractions\n[\ndissertation\n]\nRecently Taught Courses\nWinter 2026\nCS 747\nFormal Reasoning with Proof Assistants\nSpring 2025\nCS 245\nLogic and Computation\nWinter 2025\nCS 747\nFormal Reasoning with Proof Assistants\nWinter 2025\nCS 444/644\nCompiler Construction\nSpring 2024\nCS 842\nProbabilistic and Differentiable Languages\nOther Activities\nCommittee Chairs:\nThe Programming Journal (Vol. 10 and Vol. 11) Artifact Evaluation\nReview Committees & Program Committees:\nICFP 2026\n,\nLAFI 2026\n,\nRocqPL 2026\n,\nHOPE 2025\n,\nPLDI 2025 SRC\n,\nPLDI 2024\n,\nOOPSLA 2024\n,\nPOPL 2024 SRC\n,\nPOPL 2024\n,\nPLDI 2023\n,\nHOPE 2022\n,\nPLDI 2022\n,\nAPLAS 2020\n."
  },
