    "github": "https://github.com/wsshin",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Changxiao Cai\nChangxiao Cai\nHome\nResearch\nGroup\nTeaching\nChangxiao Cai\nI am an assistant professor of\nIndustrial and Operations Engineering\nand an affiliate faculty member of\nElectrical and Computer\nEngineering\nat\nUniversity of Michigan\n.\nPreviously, I was a postdoctoral researcher at University of Pennsylvania, advised by\nT. Tony Cai\nand\nHongzhe Li\n. I obtained my Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering at Princeton University in 2021, advised by\nYuxin Chen\nand\nH. Vincent Poor\n. I have also worked with Sergio Verdú at Princeton. Prior to this, I received my B.E. in Electronic Engineering from Tsinghua University in 2016.\nI am broadly interested in mathematical data science, with a recent focus on diffusion models, reinforcement learning, high-dimensional statistics, and nonconvex optimization.\nE-mail: cxcai [at] umich [dot] edu\nOffice: 2797 IOE, 1205 Beal Ave., Ann Arbor, MI 48109\nGoogle Scholar\nNews\nA new paper on dependence-adaptive unmasking schedules for diffusion LMs:\nAdaptation to Intrinsic Dependence in Diffusion Language Models\nA new paper on sampling convergence analysis of diffusion LMs:\nBreaking AR's Sampling Bottleneck: Provable Acceleration via Diffusion Language Models\naccepted to NeurIPS 2025\nA new paper on (nearly) dimension-free convergence rate of DDPM:\nDimension-Free Convergence of Diffusion Models for Approximate Gaussian Mixtures\nA new paper on minimax optimality of ODE-based samplers in diffusion models:\nMinimax Optimality of the Probability Flow ODE for Diffusion Models\nA new paper on a training-free accelerated sampling procedure for diffusion models:\nProvable Acceleration for Diffusion Models under Minimal Assumptions\nA new paper on the trust-aware multi-armed bandit:\nMinimax-Optimal Trust-Aware Multi-Armed Bandits\nPage generated 2026-02-25 14:13:13 EST, by\njemdoc+MathJax\n."
  },
