    "homepage": "http://english.ict.cas.cn/people/scien/bln/202303/t20230315_328241.html",
    "emails": [
      "ictoffice@ict.ac.cn",
      "sunxiaoming@ict.ac.cn"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "SUN Xiaoming----Institute of Computing Technology\nFaculties\nBy Last Name\nBy Research Division\nBy Last Name\nHome\n/\nPeople\n/\nFaculties\n/\nBy Last Name\nSUN Xiaoming\nProfessor\nResearch Areas:\nTheoretical Computer Science;Quantum Computing;Algorithm and Complexity;\nDepartment:\nProspective Research Laboratory\nTutor Category:\nContact:\nsunxiaoming@ict.ac.cn\nPersonal Page:\nhttp://theory.ict.ac.cn/\nXiaoming Sun is a professor at Institute of Computing Technology (ICT), Chinese Academy of Sciences, and the director of laboratory for quantum computation and theoretical computer science, ICT. His research interests span quantum computing, computational complexity, approximation algorithms, and combinatorics. He is currently the director of the CCF Technical Committee on Theoretical Computer Science. He is a member of the steering committee of the COCOON conference and an editorial board member of Information and Computation, SCIENCE CHINA: Information Sciences, JCST, and Frontiers of Computer Science."
  },
  "serper::Ruihao Zhu": {
    "homepage": "https://rzhu.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "ruihao.zhu@cornell.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/ruihaozhu/",
    "homepage_text": "Ruihao in HTML Format\nHome\nResearch\nAwards\nRuihao Zhu\nAssistant Professor of Operations, Technology, and Information Management\nPeter and Stephanie Nolan School of Hotel Administration\nCornell SC Johnson College of Business\nContact\nEmail: ruihao.zhu[AT]cornell.edu\n[Official Page]\n[LinkedIn]\nBiography\nRuihao Zhu is an Assistant Professor at the Cornell SC Johnson College of Business. He works on developing agents and algorithms for decisiong-makings in the digital economy, revenue management, and supply chain.\nRuihao's work has received multiple recognitions, including a 2nd place in the\nPOMS Chelliah Sriskandarajah Early Career Research Accomplishments Award\nand the\nINFORMS Innovative Applications in Analytics Award\n. He currently serves as an Associate Editor for the\nINFORMS Journal on Data Science\nand as an Area Chair for the Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS) and the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\nRuihao received his Ph.D. degree (advisor:\nDavid Simchi-Levi\n) from MIT (2021) and his bachelor degrees from the Shanghai Jiao Tong University (2015) and the University of Michigan (2015)."
  },
  "serper::Yasha Wang": {
    "homepage": "https://www.icbddm.org/?post_type=products&page_id=36104",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "BDDM | Yasha Wang\nProf. Yasha Wang\nNational Engineering Research Center of Software Engineering, Peking University, China\nPractice of Electronic Health Record Analysis Based on Deep Learning\nBio:\nYasha\nWang\nis a Boya distinguished professor at Peking University, a Changjiang Scholar appointed by the Ministry of Education, the Director of the National Engineering Research Center of Software Engineering, Peking University. He has long been engaged in research in the fields of artificial intelligence and data mining and has published over a hundred papers in top-tier academic journals and conferences, such as TKDE, ICML, KDD, ICDE, AAAI. His main research interests focus on high-dimensional and small-sample learning, domain-specific LLM, and the intersection of artificial intelligence and medicine. His research achievements have won the second prize of the National Science and Technology Progress Award and the first prize of the Science and Technology Progress Award from the Ministry of Education.\nAbstract\nTraining artificial-intelligence models on electronic health record (EHR) data is of great importance for both medical discovery and clinical decision support. Yet the high dimensionality, small sample size, and extreme sparsity of EHRs pose a series of technical challenges for model development. This talk will first outline the principal technical hurdles in EHR-based learning, then illustrate—through concrete examples—several innovative solutions proposed by the speaker to address them. A live demonstration of a clinical decision-support system developed by the speaker’s team will follow. The presentation will conclude by highlighting several key directions for future research in this field."
  },
