    "homepage_text": "About Me - Jiaxian Guo\nJiaxian Guo\nSenior Research Scientist, Google Research\nFollow\nSydney, Australia\nEmail\nLinkedIn\nGithub\nGoogle Scholar\nAbout Me\nI am now a Senior Research Scientist at Google Research Australia, dedicated to enhancing the inference efficiency of large foundation models and proposing efficient model architectures.\nAt Google, I contributed to Gemini Pre-Training, e.g. some model changes of\nGemini-3\nand\nGemini-2.5\nseries. In addition to the Gemini contributor, I was the main contributor of\nGemma3n\n, the open-sourced LLM for on-device scenarios, where I launched one of the main architectural changes.\nWorking on the next Gemini and Gemma\n.\nConcurrently, I serve as a visiting researcher at\nMatsuo Lab\nat the University of Tokyo, where I mentor students exploring\nLLM-based agents, causality in LLMs and multi-modal large language models\n. I am looking for outstanding and highly motivated students or collaborators to work together in the above directions.\nPlease feel free to contact me if you are interested\n.\nI was a PostDoc at the University of Tokyo, advised by Dr.\nShane Gu\nand Prof.\nYutaka Matsuo\n.\nI was a Ph.D. student in the Computer Science Department at The University of Sydney, advised by Prof.\nDacheng Tao\nand Dr.\nMingming Gong\n.\nI received my Bachelor’s Degree in\nShanghai Jiao Tong University\nand was the leader of Text Generation Group in\nApex\nLab from 2017 to 2018. My undergraduate research advisors are Prof.\nYong Yu\n, Prof.\nWeinan Zhang\nand Prof.\nJun Wang\n.\nMy current research interests lie in the general area of machine learning, particularly in causal learning, reinforcement learning and domain adaptation methods, as well as their applications in sequential decision making, computer vision and natural language processing.\nAcademic Service\nConference Reviewer: ICML 2021-2023, NeurIPS 2021-2022, ICLR 2021-2023, CVPR 2021-2023, ICCV 2021-2023, AAAI 2022.\nJournal Reviewer: IEEE Journal of Cybernatics, IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence (TPAMI), NeuroComputing, Machine Learning Journal (Springer), Transactions on Machine Learning Research"
  },
