    "homepage_text": "Zhiwei Zhong (仲志伟) - Homepage\nZhong Zhiwei\nPostdoctoral Researcher\nCity University of Hong Kong (CityU)\nAsia/Hong_Kong\nEmail\nGithub\nGoogle Scholar\n微信: zhwzhong01\nZhiwei Zhong received his Ph.D. from the Faculty of Computing，Harbin Institute of Technology (HIT), where he was supervised by Professor\nXianming Liu\nand Professor\nDebin Zhao\n. He is currently a postdoctoral researcher at City University of Hong Kong (CityU) under the supervision of Professor\nShiqi Wang\n. His primary research areas are computer vision and image processing, with a focus on image restoration, guided image super-resolution, and deep learning.\n🔥 News\n2025.06\n:  🎉🎉 One paper titled “Dual-Level Cross-Modality Neural Architecture Search for Guided Image Super-Resolution” has been accepted by IEEE TPAMI (\nTop journal, IF=18.2\n).\n2025.06\n:  🎉🎉 We achieved the first place at IEEE CVPR 2025 PBVS\nThermal Image Super-resolution Challenge\n.\n📝 Publications\nIEEE TIP 2022\nHigh-Resolution Depth Maps Imaging via Attention-Based Hierarchical Multi-Modal Fusion\nZhiwei Zhong\n, Xianming Liu, Junjun Jiang, Debin Zhao, Zhiwen Chen, Xiangyang Ji\nGithub\nPropose a novel attention-based hierarchical multi-modal fusion (AHMF) network for guided depth map supe-resolution.\nIEEE TNNLS 2023\nDeep Attentional Guided Image Filtering\nZhiwei Zhong\n, Xianming Liu, Junjun Jiang, Debin Zhao, Xiangyang Ji\nGithub\nPropose a learning-based multi-scale image filtering framework.\nACM CSUR 2023\nGuided Depth Map Super-Resolution: A Survey\nZhiwei Zhong\n, Xianming Liu, Junjun Jiang, Debin Zhao, Xiangyang Ji\nGithub\nA comprehensive survey and a benchmark for guided depth map super-resolution.\nIEEE TPAMI 2025\nDual-Level Cross-Modality Neural Architecture Search for Guided Image Super-Resolution\nZhiwei Zhong\n, Xianming Liu, Junjun Jiang, Debin Zhao, Shiqi Wang\nGithub\nThe first Neural Architecture Method for guided depth map super-resolution.\n🎖 Honors and Awards\n2021.12\nWinner\nof ACM ICMR 2021 Guided Depth Map Super-resolution\nChallenge\n.\n2023.06\nWinner\nof IEEE CVPR 2023 PBVS Thermal Image Super-resolution Challenge,\nTrack 1, SSIM\n.\n2023.06\nWinner\nof IEEE CVPR 2023 PBVS Thermal Image Super-resolution Challenge,\nTrack 2\n.\n2024.06\nWinner\nof IEEE CVPR 2024 PBVS Thermal Image Super-resolution Challenge,\nTrack 1\n.\n2024.06\nWinner\nof IEEE CVPR 2024 PBVS Thermal Image Super-resolution Challenge,\nTrack 2\n.\n2025.06\nWinner\nof IEEE CVPR 2025 PBVS Thermal Image Super-resolution Challenge,\nTrack 2\n.\nNational Scholarship, Heilongjiang Provincial Outstanding Student, Heilongjiang Provincial Outstanding Ph.D. Graduate, HIT Outstanding Ph.D. Graduate, Tencent Scholarship, HIT Outstanding Ph.D. Thesis Nomination ($10\\%$)\n💼 Work Experience\n2023.12 -  (now)\n, Postdoc Research, Department of Computer Science, City University of Hong Kong (CityU), HK, China.\n📖 Educations\n2017.09 - 2023.09\n, Ph. D. , School of Computer Science and Technology, Harbin Institute of Technology (HIT), Harbin, China.\n2013.09 - 2017.06\n, Bachelor, School of Computer Science and Technology, Heilongjiang University, Harbin, China.\n💬 Invited Talks\n2024.10\n, Guided Image Super-resolution: From Traditional Methods to Deep Learning (Northeastern University, Shenyang, China).\n💻 Internships\n2018.09 - 2023.12\n,\nPeng Cheng Laboratory\n, Shenzhen, China."
  },
  "serper::Chudi Zhong": {
    "homepage": "https://chudizhong.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "chudi@unc.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Chudi Zhong\nChudi Zhong\nAssistant Professor\nSchool of Data Science and Society\nDepartment of Statistics and Operations Research\nUniversity of North Carolina at Chapel Hill\nEmail\n: chudi [at] unc [dot] edu\nGoogle Scholar\nI am an assistant professor in the School of Data Science and Society and \r\n\t\t\t    Department of Statistics and Operations Research at UNC-Chapel Hill. \r\n\t\t\t\tI received my Ph.D. in Computer Science from Duke University.\nMy research focuses on understanding and controlling\nmodel diversity\nin modern machine learning, \r\n\t\t\t\tand on building\ntrustworthy and reliable AI systems\n. \r\n\t\t\t\tA central theme of my work is the\nRashomon Effect\n, the existence of many equally accurate \r\n\t\t\t\tyet behaviorally distinct models, and its implications for scientific understanding of learning \r\n\t\t\t\tand model behavior. Current key directions include:\ncharacterize the set of near-optimal models or equally valid outputs, \r\n\t\t\t\t\t\tand study the variability that arises across them\nleverage model diversity to reveal alternative explanations and failure modes \r\n\t\t\t\t\t\tand support human-aligned, interpretable, and stable reasoning in modern ML systems and LLMs.\nLab Openings:\nI am actively recruiting highly motivated\nPhD students and multiple research interns\nwith strong technical backgrounds. \r\n\t\t\t\tCandidates who are interested are strongly encouraged to contact me via email, including a resume and transcripts.\nRecent News\n[Jan 2026]\nI will co-organize a tutorial and a workshop on the Rashomon Effect at AAAI 26 in Singapore.\nSelected Awards\nSecond Place, Bell Labs Prize, 2023\nThe Bell Labs Prize is an international competition to solicit game changing and impactful ideas \r\n\t\t\t\t\tthat have the potential to change the way people live, work, and communicate with each other. \r\n\t\t\t\t\tAmong 107 proposals received in 2023, we won the second place in the Bell Labs Prize.\nRising Stars in Data Science\nUniversity of Chicago, 2023\nOutstanding Ph.D. Preliminary Research Award\nDepartment of Computer Science, Duke University, 2023\nFinalist, Best Student Paper Award\nData Mining Society, INFORMS, 2022"
  },
