  "serper::Sin Yong Tan": {
    "homepage": "https://scslab.me.iastate.edu/team--contact.html",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/tryambak-gangopadhyay-0ab13568/",
    "homepage_text": "Team & Contact - Self-aware Complex Systems Lab\nHome\nPrincipal Investigator\nTeam & Contact\nResearch\nPublications\nShowcase\nPrincipal Investigator\nContact Information\n207 Lab of Mechanics\nIowa State University\nAmes, IA 50011\n515-294-5212\nsoumiks (@) iastate.edu\nDr. Soumik Sarkar\nAssociate Professor\nPh. D. Mechanical Engineering, Penn State University, 2011\nM.S. Mechanical Engineering, Penn State University, 2009\nM.S. Mathematics, Penn State University, 2009\nB.E. Mechanical Engineering, Jadavpur University, 2006\nDr.\nSoumik Sarkar received his B. Eng. Degree in Mechanical Engineering in 2006 from Jadavpur University, Kolkata, India. He received M.S. in Mechanical Engineering and M.A. in Mathematics in 2009 from Penn State University. Dr. Sarkar received his Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering from Penn State in 2011. He joined the Department of Mechanical Engineering at Iowa State as an Assistant Professor in Fall 2014. Currently he is an Associate Professor of Mechanical Engineering and Computer Science and a Walter W Wilson Faculty Fellow in Engineering. Dr. Sarkar’s research interests include Machine Learning, Sensor Fusion, and Distributed Optimization and Control with applications to complex Cyber-Physical Systems such as aerospace, energy & smart building systems, transportation, design & manufacturing and agriculture systems. He co‐authored more than 202 peer-reviewed publications including 79journal papers and 8 book chapters. Dr. Sarkar is currently serving as an Associate Editor of Frontiers in Robotics and AI: Sensor Fusion and Machine Perception journal, Plant Phenomics, a Science Partner Journal and an NVIDIA DLI University Ambassadorship. He received about $20M federal and private funding over the past seven years at Iowa State. He was selected to be an Iowa state Plant Science Institute scholar in 2018, received the Iowa State College of Engineering Award for Early Achievement in Research in 2019 and the Iowa State University Award for Early Achievement in Research in 2020. Dr. Sarkar is a recipient of the prestigious NSF CISE Career Initiation Initiative (CRII) award in 2015, the Young Investigator award from the US Air Force Office of Scientific Research (AFOSR) in 2017 and the NSF CAREER award in 2019.\n[\nGoogle Scholars\n] [\nCV\n]\nPost-doctorate Fellow\n​Aditya Balu\nPhD, Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 2020\nB.E. Hons. Mechanical Engineering,\nBirla Institute of Technology and Sciences, Pilani, 2014\nAditya Balu is currently a postdoctoral researcher at Iowa State University, where he is working on generative designs for organic photovoltaic cells using physics-aware deep learning methodologies. He finished his Ph.D. at Iowa State University on Deep Learning and GPU computing for Design and Manufacturing before this. During his graduate studies, he also interned at ANSYS Inc., where he contributed to Deep Learning-based Topology Optimization. Before his graduate studies, he has also worked at an Oil \\& Gas industry, FMC Technologies (now known as Technip FMC), as a product design engineer where he was designing connectors and manifolds for subsea high pressure and high-temperature applications.\n​\nbaditya\n(@) iastate.edu\nPersonal Website\nDoctoral Students\nKoushik Nagasubramanian\nB.Tech, Electronics and Communication Engineering, RNSIT, Bangalore, India, 2015.\nKoushik is a PhD student\nunder joint supervision of Prof. Baskar Ganapathysubramanian and Prof. Soumik Sarkar\n. He received his undergraduate degree in 2015. After that, he worked at Nubesol Technologies, Bangalore for almost seven months. His research interests include machine Learning, deep learning, and computer vision. Currently, he is working on using deep learning for plant disease prediction.\nkoushikn (@) iastate.edu\nTryambak Gangopadhyay\nM.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 2019\nB.E. Mechanical Engineering, Jadavpur University, 2017\nTryambak is currently a Ph.D. student in the Department of Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University. He joined Dr. Soumik Sarkar’s lab in Fall 2017.  His research interests include designing of deep learning algorithms for large-scale image/video data, multivariate time series data, and text data with applications in healthcare, agriculture, vision, and different cyber-physical systems. He is doing a concurrent Masters in Computer Science. In 2019, as a summer intern at the Machine Learning group of Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory (LLNL), he developed deep learning fusion models for clinical prediction tasks using Electronic Health Record datasets. His other interests are story-writing, reading, drawing sketches and playing guitar.\n​tryambak (@) iastate.edu\nLinkedIn\nGoogle Scholar\nLuis G. Riera\n​M.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 1997\nB.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 1995\nLuis is a Ph.D. Student in Mechanical Engineering, who joined Dr. Sarkar's  lab in August 2017.  His research interests are in deep learning, machine learning, visualization, Self-Driven vehicles, including cars, trucks, tractors, and others.  Before joining the lab, Luis worked mainly in Project Management and Business Development. His hobbies are racing Go-Kart, baking, Mount Biking and computers.\nlgriera\n(@) iastate.edu\nSin Yong Tan (Evan)\nB.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 2017\nSin Yong is currently a PhD student in Mechanical Engineering and joined this lab in Fall 2017. He is currently learning the concept of Machine Learning and will be involved in the Autonomous Rover project, and Robot and Cop project to acquire some hands-on experience on Machine Learning applications.\ntsyong98 (@) iastate.edu\nBernard Lee Xian Yeow\nB.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 2016\nXian Yeow received his bachelor’s degree in Mechanical Engineering from Iowa State University in 2016. He is currently pursuing the Ph.D. degree at Iowa State University and his current research interests include applications of reinforcement learning and deep learning methods to engineering problems.\nxylee (@) iastate.edu\nRussell\nKai Liang Tan\nB.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 2017\n​\nKai Liang (Russell) is currently a PhD student in Mechanical Engineering and joined Self-aware Complex Systems Lab since Spring 2018. He received his undergraduate degree in Fall 2017 from Iowa State University. During his undergraduate degree, he was an intern for The Bobcat Company and Danfoss Power Solutions. His current research interest includes utilizing Deep Learning, Reinforcement Learning and Computer Vision to solve engineering problems, cyber-physical systems and autonomous systems. His passion for creativity also goes beyond academics, such as photography, digital arts and playing strategic video games.\nkailiang (@) iastate.edu\nYasaman Esfandiari\n​M.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa, 2017\nB.S. Mechanical Engineering, Amirkabir University of Technology, Tehran, Iran, 2015\nYasaman is a PhD student in mechanical engineering and joined the lab in Fall 2018. Her research interests are dynamics and controls, optimization, machine learning and deep learning. She is currently working on nonconvex robust optimization in deep neural networks with unknown adversaries. Her other activities include playing piano, reading and working out.\nyasesf​ (@) iastate.edu\nFateme Fotouhi Ardakani\nM.S. Mechanical engineering, Shiraz University, 2018\nB.E. Mechanical Engineering, Shiraz University, 2015\nFateme Fotouhi is a Ph.D. student in mechanical engineering and she joined this lab in Fall 2019. Her M.S. degree was in the field of control and vibration. Her main research interests include machine learning, deep learning, biomechanics, nonlinear control, robotics, mechatronics, and optimizations. She will emphasize in machine learning and deep learning in her Ph.D. thesis. She also interests in playing piano, reading books and mountain climbing.\n​fotouhif (@) iastate.edu\nJoshua Waite\nB.S. Mechanical Engineering, Iowa State University, 2019\nJoshua is a PhD student in Mechanical Engineering, who joined Dr. Sarkar’s lab in Spring 2020. He is currently learning the concepts of machine learning along with gaining hands-on experience. His research interests include machine learning, deep learning, robotics, computer vision, and human-machine interaction.\njrwaite (@) iastate.edu\n​\nEthan Herron\nB.S. Mathematics, Iowa State University, 2020\nEthan is a Ph.D. student in Mechanical Engineering and joined the Self-aware Complex Systems Laboratory in Summer 2020. He received his B.S. in Mathematics at Iowa State University in Spring 2020. His current research interests are in machine learning, deep learning, and optimization. He is currently working on applying deep learning methods for topology optimization. His other activities include high-powered rocketry, cooking, and various outdoor activities.\nedherron (@) iastate.edu\nSouradeep Chattopadhyay\nB.S. Statistics, University of Calcutta, 2014\nM.S. Statistics, University of Calcutta, 2016\nSouradeep is currently a PhD student and joined the lab in Spring 2021. He is interested in machine learning especially unsupervised learning methods and its applications to various domains. He has a very deep interest in astronomy and over the past two years have published papers which involve analyzing astronomical data using machine learning methods. His hobbies include singing and reading, his favorite author being Dan Brown. He is also an ardent fan of Sherlock Holmes.\nsoura (@) iastate.edu\nZahid Hasan\nB.S. Electrical Engineering, Bangladesh University of Engineering and Technology, 2018\nZahid is a Ph.D. student in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Iowa State University. He joined Dr. Soumik's lab in the Summer 2021 semester. His research interest includes Machine Learning, Deep Learning and Cyber-Physical System design. He is currently working on deep learning and computer vision in intelligent driving applications. Hi...[truncated]"
  },
