    "homepage_text": "Gong Research Group\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nGong Research Group\n_______________\nGong Research Group\nJoin us\nto Advance the Next Generation Bio-Electronics\nOur Mission\n.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .\nThe\nNEXT\ngeneration of electronics is going to be soft, smart, and more interactive. Their capacity of interfacing with our body enables them to collect and transmit the data of biological activity with the high performance (e.g. accuracy, speed) which are unimaginable before. This is going to change the landscape of healthcare, robotics, agriculture, and beyond.\nThe Gong Research Group at the University of Michigan is developing the soft electronic materials and devices. We utilize a transdisciplinary approach that unites physics, chemistry, and engineering. Our goal is to innovate soft electronic materials (e.g. nanomaterials, polymers, perovskites) from the molecular level, understand their fundamental properties using advanced spectroscopies, and advance the next generation of bio-electronics for the applications in biomedical sensing, soft robotics, energy harvesting and storage.\nResearch\n.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .\nNew Materials\nInnovate materials at molecular level.\nSpectroscopy\nUnderstand materials using light.\nBio-electronics\nInterface electronics with body.\nResearch\nPublications\nGet iN Touch\n.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .\nnewS\nGong Research Group\nReceives\nCoE ETR Award\nPosted on:\nJune 19th\n, 2025\nOur group received funding from the U-M CoE\nDean's Engineering Translation Research (ETR) Fund to assist with commercialization of\nperovskite solar cells\n.\nGong Research Group Receives MTRAC Award\nPosted on:\nMay 29th\n, 2025\nOur group received funding to c\nontinue the\ndevelopment of l\now-cost, large-scale perovskite solar cells with high efficiency and stability\n.\nGong Research Group Receives MMRI Award\nPosted on: March 17th, 2025\nOur group received Michigan Materials Research Institute (MMRI) Faculty Proposal Team Award to develop self-adhesive, solid-state hydrogel electrodes for transcranial electrical stimulation (tES).\nZach Pizzo awarded\nIES Fellowship\nPosted on: November 18th, 2024\nZach selected for\none-year fellowship that brings together graduate students and postdoctoral scholars at the University of Michigan from diverse disciplines who are interested in advancing future energy solutions via interdisciplinary networks\nZach Pizzo Receives Joseph J. Martin Award\nPosted on:\nSeptember 18th\n, 2024\nZach selected for departmental award that recognizes exceptional graduate students who demonstrate outstanding scholarship, athletic excellence, and a strong commitment to service.\nGong Research Group\nReceives\nMTRAC Kickstart Award\nPosted on:\nAugust 9th, 2024\nOur group received\nMTRAC funding\nto develop\nl\now-cost, large-scale perovskite solar cells with high efficiency and stability needed for potential applications in the electric and hybrid vehicle and industry.\nRead more from\nChemical Engineering News\n.\nGong Research Group awarded ACS PRF Doctoral New Investigator Grant\nPosted on:\nJune\n26\nth, 2024\nOur group received ACS grant to develop recyclable, durable electrodes. Read more from\nChemical Engineering News\n.\nXiwen Gong\nReceives NSF CAREER Award\nPosted on:\nJan 9th, 2024\nXiwen\nawarded the NSF CAREER Award entitled,\n\"\nCAREER: Controlling the Deformability of Quantum Dots Solids for Wearable NIR Optoelectronics.\n\"\nMedia\nWe are hiring\nWe\nhave openings for\npostdoctoral fellow\ns, graduate students, undergraduate interns, and visiting students.\nClick Join Us for further information.\nJoin Us\nTeam\nResearch\nPublications\nJoin Us\nNews\nContact\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
