    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Pan Chao's Academic Homepage\nCarpe Diem\nMain Menu\nHome\nWordpress\nResearch\nTeaching\nä¸­æ\nResume\nWelcome\nI am a Ph.D. graduate from\nPurdue Statistics Department\n. I received my Bachelor in Economics from\nShanghai University of Finance and Economics\nand MS in Statistics from Purdue University. My research interests include statistical methodology for high-dimensional data analysis, data visualization and statistical modeling for machine learning tasks. The application areas of my research include public health, business analytics and finance.\nContact\nEmail:\npanc AT purdue DOT edu\nAddress:\n250N, University Street\nWest Lafayette, IN 47906"
  },
  "serper::Yuanming Hu": {
    "homepage": "https://yuanming.taichi.graphics/",
    "emails": [
      "yuanmhu@gmail.com",
      "careers@meshy.ai"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/yuanming-hu",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Yuanming Hu\nSearch\nYuanming Hu\nEthan 胡渊鸣 博士\nPh.D., MIT EECS\nFounder & CEO, Meshy AI\nHi, I’m Ethan, founder and CEO of\nMeshy AI\n. Before founding Meshy, I did my Ph.D. in computer science (MIT CSAIL), advised by\nFrédo Durand\nand\nBill Freeman\n.\nWe are hiring!\nMeshy is the leading generative AI product for 3D. We’re actively hiring exceptional researchers and engineers in computer graphics, AI, and high-performance systems (compilers and GPU performance engineering) across Asia and North America—for both full-time roles and internships. If you’re interested, please visit our\ncareer site\nto explore open roles, or send your resume directly to\ncareers@meshy.ai\n.\nI graduated with honor from\nTsinghua University\n(\nYao class\n) in July 2017. I worked on deep learning and computer vision, during my internship with\nStephen Lin\nat\nMicrosoft Research Asia\n. My undergrad thesis is on\nautomatic (differentiable) photo post-processing using reinforcement learning & GANs (TOG & SIGGRAPH 2018)\n. I completed my master thesis (\nThe ChainQueen Differentiable Physical Simulator\n) with Wojciech Matusik in November 2018. My research has been partly supported by an Edwin Webster fellowship, a\nSnap Research fellowship\n, an\nAdobe Research fellowship\n, a\nFacebook Research fellowship\n, and\na GPU gift\nfrom NVIDIA.\nimport taichi as ti\nUpdate March 2021:\nI completed my Ph.D. thesis defense :-)\nUpdate July 2022:\nThe\nTaichi thesis\nis awarded\nMIT EECS 2022 George M. Sprowls Thesis Award\nand\nSIGGRAPH 2022 Outstanding Doctoral Dissertation Award Honorable Mention\n.\nMeshy news\n:\n(Sept 2025) We dropped the Meshy 6 Preview model that delivers\nsculpting-level details\n.\nTaichi news\n:\nETH Zurich adopted Taichi to teach physically-based simulation in fall 2021! The vast majority of the students used the Taichi for their\nfinal projects\n.\n[Read the full story]\n.\nMade a\nvideo collection\nof recent Taichi community contributions.\nCheck out Taichi Voxel Challenge with\n100+ submissions\n.\nTaichi v1.7.4 is released! Try the latest version:\npip install --upgrade taichi\nEmail\n: yuanmhu\nat\ngmail\ndot\ncom\nEducation\nPhD in EECS, MIT\n2019.1-2021.3\nMSc in EECS, MIT\n2017.9-2018.12\nBEng in Computer Science, Yao class, Tsinghua University\n2013.9-2017.6\nInterests\nComputer graphics\nCompilers\n(Differentiable) Physical simulation\nHigh-performance computing\nDifferentiable programming\nDeep learning\nComputational photography\nNews\nWill talk about our\nSIGGRAPH 2018 Asia high-resolution topology optimization paper\nat\nTOP Webinar\non Oct 29\n(flyer)\n. Welcome!\nWill teach a 3.5-hour course on Taichi and physical simulation at\nChinagraph 2020 on Oct 21\n.\nWill teach a course on Taichi at\nSIGGRAPH 2020\n.\nWill speak at\nDiffCVGP @ NeurIPS 2020\non differentiable physics and machine learning.\nGAMES 201 was a big success!\n~1000 students participated online to learn how to write physics engines :-)\nTeaching\n(GAMES 201) Advanced Physics Engines 2020: A Hands-on Tutorial\nSummer 2020 online course. The math and code behind physically based animation/physics engines.\nLast updated on\nDec 19, 2020\nResearch\nThe Taichi High-Performance and Differentiable Programming Language for Sparse and Quantized Visual Computing\nMIT EECS Ph.D. thesis\nEverything about the Taichi programming language.\nMIT EECS 2022 George M. Sprowls Thesis Award\nHonorable Mention, SIGGRAPH 2022 Outstanding Doctoral Dissertation Award\nYuanming Hu\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nQuanTaichi: A Compiler for Quantized Simulations\nSIGGRAPH 2021\nSimulate more with less memory, using a quantization compiler.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nJiafeng Liu\n,\nXuanda Yang\n,\nMingkuan Xu\n,\nYe Kuang\n,\nWeiwei Xu\n,\nQiang Dai\n,\nWilliam T. Freeman\n,\nFredo Durand\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nPlasticineLab: A Soft-Body Manipulation Benchmark with Differentiable Physics\nICLR 2021 (Spotlight)\nA virtual environment for\ndifferentiable elastoplastic\nsoft object manipulation.\nZhiao Huang\n,\nYuanming Hu\n,\nTao Du\n,\nSiyuan Zhou\n,\nHao Su\n,\nJoshua B. Tenenbaum\n,\nChuang Gan\nPDF\nCite\nPoster\nDiffTaichi: Differentiable Programming for Physical Simulation\nICLR 2020\nThe two-scale (tape + source-code transfrom) reverse-mode automatic differentiation engine for Taichi.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nLuke Anderson\n,\nTzu-Mao Li\n,\nQi Sun\n,\nNathan Carr\n,\nJonathan Ragan-Kelley\n,\nFredo Durand\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nLife of a Taichi Kernel\nInvited talk, 2020.\nA trip through Taichi’s internal design and implementation.\nYuanming Hu\nPDF\nCite\nThe Taichi Programming Language: A Hands-on Tutorial\nSIGGRAPH 2020 Courses\nAn introductory Taichi tutorial.\nYuanming Hu\nPDF\nCite\nVideo\nOn Hybrid Lagrangian-Eulerian Simulation Methods: Practical Notes and High-Performance Aspects\nSIGGRAPH 2019 Courses\nPerformance-engineering MPM solvers so that they run 100x faster than a basic implementation.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nXinxin Zhang\n,\nMing Gao\n,\nChenfanfu Jiang\nPDF\nCite\nTaichi: A Language for High-Performance Computation on Spatially Sparse Data Structures\nSIGGRAPH Asia 2019\nA data-oriented programming language and compiler for compute-intense visual computing workloads, especially those needing\nspatially sparse data structures\n.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nTzu-Mao Li\n,\nLuke Anderson\n,\nJonathan Ragan-Kelley\n,\nFredo Durand\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nLearning-In-The-Loop Optimization: End-To-End Control And Co-Design of Soft Robots Through Learned Deep Latent Representations\nNeurIPS 2019\nDeep learning control and state representation simultaneously for soft robots.\nAndrew Spielberg\n,\nAllan Zhao\n,\nYuanming Hu\n,\nTao Du\n,\nWojciech Matusik\n,\nDaniela Rus\nPDF\nCite\nVideo\nChainQueen: A Real-Time Differentiable Physical Simulator for Soft Robotics\nICRA 2019\nThe first differentiable MLS-MPM simulator.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nJiancheng Liu\n,\nAndrew Spielberg\n,\nJoshua B. Tenenbaum\n,\nWilliam T. Freeman\n,\nJiajun Wu\n,\nDaniela Rus\n,\nWojciech Matusik\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nThe ChainQueen Differentiable Physics Engine\nMIT EECS Master Thesis, 2018\nMIT-thesis version of\nChainQueen\nYuanming Hu\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nNarrow-Band Topology Optimization on a Sparsely Populated Grid\nSIGGRAPH Asia 2018\nHigh-performance giga-voxel FEM solver for topology optimization on a narrow-band, sparse grid data structure.\nHaixiang Liu*\n,\nYuanming Hu*\n,\nBo Zhu\n,\nWojciech Matusik\n,\nEftychios Sifakis\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nDeep Multispectral Painting Reproduction via Multi-layer, Custom-Ink Printing\nSIGGRAPH Asia 2018\nUse deep learning to generate faithful spectral color reproduction for 3D printing.\nLiang Shi\n,\nVahid Babaei\n,\nChangil Kim\n,\nMichael Foshey\n,\nYuanming Hu\n,\nPitchaya Sitthi-Amorn\n,\nSzymon Rusinkiewicz\n,\nWojciech Matusik\nPDF\nCite\nExposure: A White-Box Photo Post-Processing Framework\nSIGGRAPH 2018 (TOG), and Tsinghua undergraduate thesis\nDifferentiable photo-editing model with RL and GANs.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nHao He\n,\nChenxi Xu\n,\nBaoyuan Wang\n,\nStephen Lin\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nA Moving Least Squares Material Point Method with Displacement Discontinuity and Two-Way Rigid Body Coupling\nSIGGRAPH 2018\nThe moving least squares material point method (MLS-MPM) that runs fast fast and is easy to implement.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nYu Fang\n,\nZiheng Ge\n,\nZiyin Qu\n,\nYixin Zhu\n,\nAndre Pradhana\n,\nChenfanfu Jiang\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nA Temporally Adaptive Material Point Method with Regional Time Stepping\nSCA 2018\nThe asynchronous material point method (AsyncMPM): Use small time steps where necessary, and large time steps where possible.\nYu Fang\n,\nYuanming Hu\n,\nShi-Min Hu\n,\nChenfanfu Jiang\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nAn Asynchronous Material Point Method\nSIGGRAPH 2017 Posters\nAn early version of\nAsyncMPM\n.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nYu Fang\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nFC4: Fully Convolutional Color Constancy with Confidence-weighted Pooling\nCVPR 2017\nDeep-learning-based color consistency algorithm that adaptively selects high-confidence regions.\nYuanming Hu\n,\nBaoyuan Wang\n,\nStephen Lin\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nTalks\nSingle-computer giga-voxel topology optimization using a narrowband sparse grid\n“How to solve topology optimization with over one billion (1,040,875,347) voxels on a single computer.”\nOct 29, 2020\nPDF\nCode\nCite\n×\nCopy\nDownload"
  },
