    "homepage_text": "Carlos Fernandez-Granda\nHome\nGroup\nPublications\nTalks\nOutreach\nTeaching\nProjects\nMachine learning\nDenoising\nStroke rehabilitation\nData-driven medicine\nClimate\nInverse problems\nResources\nProbability and Stats for DS\nAI-powered Denoising\nMath Tools for DS\nCarlos Fernandez-Granda\nInterim Director, Center for Data Science\nAssociate Professor of Mathematics and Data Science\nCourant Institute of Mathematical Science\nand\nCenter for Data Science\nNew York University\nThe goal of my research group is to design and analyze data-science methodology. Currently, our main focus is machine learning, and its application to medicine, climate science and scientific imaging.\nMy book on\nProbability and Statistics for Data Science\nwas recently published. A preprint, videos, code, slides and solutions to exercises are available in the\nbook website\n.\nI am part of the\nMath and Data\ngroup and the\nM2LInES project\n.\nContact\ncfgranda@cims.nyu.edu\nPage generated 2025-06-18 17:20:58 EDT, by\njemdoc\n."
  },
  "serper::Felix Juefei-Xu": {
    "homepage": "https://xujuefei.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/tsingqguo",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/felixu/",
    "homepage_text": "Felix Juefei-Xu - Home Page\nFelix Juefei Xu\nFelix Juefei Xu\nis a\nResearch Scientist\nat\nMeta Superintelligence Labs\nin New York City, where he builds next-generation\nmultimodal foundational models\nand\nmedia generation systems\n, spanning image and video, with an increasing focus on controllable dynamics, predictive representations, and world-aware learning, alongside robustness and efficiency at scale. He is also affiliated with\nNew York University\nas an Adjunct Professor. Previously, he served as a Research Scientist at\nAlibaba Group\nin Sunnyvale, California.\nDuring his Ph.D. studies at\nCarnegie Mellon University (CMU)\n, he was working in a research group specializing in pattern recognition, machine learning, computer vision, and image processing, especially as applied to the field of biometrics, at\nCarnegie Mellon CyLab Biometrics Center\nunder the supervision of\nProf. Marios Savvides\n. CyLab is CMU's Security and Privacy Institute. He received his B.S. from\nShanghai Jiao Tong University (SJTU)\n. Felix is published under\nF. Juefei-Xu.\nEmail\n/\nGoogle Scholar\n/\nX\n/\nLinkedin\n/\nPhoto\nResearch (circa 2018-2022)\nGenerative Models, Multimodal, Deep Learning, Computer Vision, Adversarial Robustness, AI Safety.\nMy research in general is focused on a fuller understanding of deep learning where I am actively exploring new methods in deep learning that are\nstatistically efficient\nand\nadversarially robust\n. Through these efforts, I am investigating under what conditions deep learning starts to work and under what conditions deep learning starts to fail. I am now heavily involved in pushing the boundary of robust vision and safe AI by creating high-realism degradation-mimetic adversarial attacks such as adversarial motion/defocus blur, rain/haze, relighting/exposure/vignetting/shadow, etc., and then devising tools to defend against them through an attempt to situate both the\nnatural corruption robustness\nand the\nadversarial robustness\nproblems under the same dome; creating cunning DeepFakes that are detection-evasive to foster the development of next-generation robust detectors of real-world video/audio DeepFakes; seeking robust learners by creating novel neural architectures and computational modules that are statistically efficient and adversarially robust.\nMy Ph.D. thesis is primarily focused on tackling the Pose, Expression, Resolution, Illumination, and Occlusion (Perio) challenges for unconstrained periocular face recognition using both shallow and deep discriminative and generative methods, especially under the dome of self-supervised predictive learning. I also have broader interests in  pattern recognition, computer vision, machine learning, optimization, statistics, compressive sensing, and image processing.\nI am the recipient of multiple best/distinguished paper awards, including the\nBest Poster Paper Award\nof the\nIEEE/IAPR International Joint Conference on Biometrics (IJCB)\nin 2011, the\nBest Paper Award\nof the\nIEEE Seventh International Conference on Biometrics: Theory, Applications and Systems (BTAS)\nin 2015,  the\nBest Student Paper Award\nof the\nIEEE Eighth International Conference on Biometrics: Theory, Applications and Systems (BTAS)\nin 2016,  the\nACM SIGSOFT Distinguished Paper Award\nof the\nIEEE/ACM International Conference on Automated Software Engineering (ASE)\nin 2018, and the\nBest Student Paper Award\nof the\n14th Asian Conference on Computer Vision (ACCV)\nin 2018.\nCVPR'26 J2A Workshop\nCall for Papers\nCVPR'26 EDGE Workshop\nCall for Papers\nCVPR'25 EDGE Workshop\nCall for Papers\nCVPR'24 EDGE Workshop\nCall for Papers\nLatest News\n[2026-03] Two papers (UniT and StreamDiT) are accepted to the IEEE/CVF Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Conference (CVPR), 2026.\n[2026-01]\nFirst Workshop on Journey to the Awards: Generative AI for Movie-Grade Video Production (J2A) at CVPR 2026\nis now accepting submission.\n[2026-01]\nThird Workshop on Efficient and On-Device Generation (EDGE) at CVPR 2026\nis now accepting submission.\n[2025-04] A few papers are accepted to the IEEE/CVF Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Conference (CVPR), 2025.\n[2025-02]\nSecond Workshop on Efficient and On-Device Generation (EDGE) at CVPR 2025\nis now accepting submission.\n[2024-10]\nMeta Movie Gen\nis finally premiered! This is the most advanced media foundation AI models.\n[2024-09]\nTowards Transmission-Friendly and Robust CNN Models over Cloud and Device\nhas won the IEEE-CCF Cloud Computing Best Paper Award, IEEE Computer Society Technical Committee on Cloud Computing (TCCLD).\n[2024-07]\nImagine yourself\nis published online, the research behind Meta's tuning-free personalized image generation.\n[2024-02]\nFirst Workshop on Efficient and On-Device Generation (EDGE) at CVPR 2024\nis now accepting submission.\nAmong Us\nis accepted to the IEEE International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV), 2023.\nThe 4th Workshop on Adversarial Robustness in the Real World (AROW) at ICCV 2023\nis now accepting paper submission.\nTFormer\nis accepted to the IEEE Transactions on Parallel and Distributed Systems (TPDS).\nALA\nhas won the Best Paper Award in ECCV 2022 AROW Workshop.\nDARTSRepair\nis accepted to the journal of Pattern Recognition (PR) 2022.\nMonoCNN\nis accepted to the IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing (TMC) 2022.\nCountering Malicious DeepFakes\nis accepted to the International Journal of Computer Vision (IJCV) 2022.\nAdversarially Camouflaging Images from Co-salient Object Detection\nis accepted to CVPR 2022.\nResearch Highlights & Preprints\nUniT: Unified Multimodal Chain-of-Thought Test-time Scaling\nLeon Liangyu Chen\n,\nHaoyu Ma\n,\nZhipeng Fan\n,\nZiqi Huang\n,\nAnimesh Sinha\n,\nXiaoliang Dai\n,\nJialiang Wang\n,\nZecheng He\n,\nJianwei Yang\n,\nChunyuan Li\n,\nJunzhe Sun\n,\nChu Wang\n,\nSerena Yeung-Levy\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\nIEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)\n, 2026\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\nUniT establishes multimodal test-time scaling as an effective paradigm for advancing both generation and understanding in unified models.\nStreamDiT: Real-Time Streaming Text-to-Video Generation\nAkio Kodaira\n,\nTingbo Hou\n,\nJi Hou\n,\nMarkos Georgopoulos\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nMasayoshi Tomizuka\n,\nYue Zhao\nIEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)\n, 2026\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\nStreamDiT enables real-time text-to-video generation by streaming video frames with consistent quality and low latency.\nPhyGDPO: Physics-Aware Groupwise Direct Preference Optimization for Physically Consistent Text-to-Video Generation\nYuanhao Cai\n,\nKunpeng Li\n,\nMenglin Jia\n,\nJialiang Wang\n,\nJunzhe Sun\n,\nWeifeng Chen\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nChu Wang\n,\nAli Thabet\n,\nXiaoliang Dai\n,\nXuan Ju\n,\nAlan Yuille\n,\nJi Hou\narXiv Preprint\n, 2025\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\n/\nproject\nPhyGDPO brings physics awareness into preference optimization, enabling text-to-video models to generate more physically consistent and realistic motions.\nExploring MLLM-Diffusion Information Transfer with MetaCanvas\nHan Lin\n,\nXichen Pan\n,\nZiqi Huang\n,\nJi Hou\n,\nJialiang Wang\n,\nWeifeng Chen\n,\nZecheng He\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nJunzhe Sun\n,\nZhipeng Fan\n,\nAli Thabet\n,\nMohit Bansal\n,\nChu Wang\narXiv Preprint\n, 2025\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\n/\nproject\nMetaCanvas lets multimodal LLMs reason directly in spatial and temporal latent spaces, enabling more precise and controllable image and video generation.\nThreadWeaver: Adaptive Threading for Efficient Parallel Reasoning in Language Models\nLong Lian\n,\nSida Wang\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nTsu-Jui Fu\n,\nXiuyu Li\n,\nAdam Yala\n,\nTrevor Darrell\n,\nAlane Suhr\n,\nYuandong Tian\n,\nVictoria Xi Lin\narXiv Preprint\n, 2025\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\n/\nproject\nThreadWeaver enables fast parallel reasoning in LLMs while preserving the accuracy of sequential chain-of-thought.\nMoCha: Towards Movie-Grade Talking Character Synthesis\nCong Wei\n,\nBo Sun\n,\nHaoyu Ma\n,\nJi Hou\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nZecheng He\n,\nXiaoliang Dai\n,\nLuxin Zhang\n,\nKunpeng Li\n,\nTingbo Hou\n,\nAnimesh Sinha\n,\nPeter Vajda\n,\nWenhu Chen\nNeural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)\n, 2025\n[Spotlight Presentation]\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\n/\nproject\n/\nteaser video\nMoCha is a pioneering model for dialogue-driven movie shot generation.\nToken-Shuffle: Towards High-Resolution Image Generation with Autoregressive Models\nXu Ma\n,\nPeize Sun\n,\nHaoyu Ma\n,\nHao Tang\n,\nChih-Yao Ma\n,\nJialiang Wang\n,\nKunpeng Li\n,\nXiaoliang Dai\n,\nYujun Shi\n,\nXuan Ju\n,\nYushi Hu\n,\nArtsiom Sanakoyeu\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nJi Hou\n,\nJunjiao Tian\n,\nTao Xu\n,\nTingbo Hou\n,\nYen-Cheng Liu\n,\nZecheng He\n,\nZijian He\n,\nMatt Feiszli\n,\nPeizhao Zhang\n,\nPeter Vajda\n,\nSam Tsai\n,\nYun Fu\narXiv Preprint\n, 2025\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\n/\nproject\nToken-Shuffle enables efficient 2048 x 2048 autoregressive image generation by reducing visual token counts through spatial merging.\nTransfer between Modalities with MetaQueries\nXichen Pan\n,\nSatya Narayan Shukla\n,\nAashu Singh\n,\nZhuokai Zhao\n,\nShlok Kumar Mishra\n,\nJialiang Wang\n,\nZhiyang Xu\n,\nJiuhai Chen\n,\nKunpeng Li\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nJi Hou\n,\nSaining Xie\narXiv Preprint\n, 2025\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\n/\nproject\nThis paper introduces MetaQuery, a minimal recipe for building state-of-the-art unified multimodal understanding (text output) and generation (pixel output) models.\nLlama Learns to Direct: DirectorLLM for Human-Centric Video Generation\nKunpeng Song\n,\nTingbo Hou\n,\nZecheng He\n,\nHaoyu Ma\n,\nJialiang Wang\n,\nAnimesh Sinha\n,\nSam Tsai\n,\nYaqiao Luo\n,\nXiaoliang Dai\n,\nLi Chen\n,\nXide Xia\n,\nPeizhao Zhang\n,\nPeter Vajda\n,\nAhmed Elgammal\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\nBritish Machine Vision Conference (BMVC)\n, 2025\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\nThis paper extends the LLM from a text generator to a video director and human motion simulator.\nLinGen: Towards High-Resolution Minute-Length Text-to-Video Generation with Linear Computational Complexity\nHongjie Wang\n,\nChih-Yao Ma\n,\nYen-Cheng Liu\n,\nJi Hou\n,\nTao Xu\n,\nJialiang Wang\n,\nFelix Juefei-Xu\n,\nYaqiao Luo\n,\nPeizhao Zhang\n,\nTingbo Hou\n,\nPeter Vajda\n,\nNiraj K. Jha\n,\nXiaoliang Dai\nIEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)\n, 2025\narxiv\n/\nbibtex\n/\nproject\n/\nproceedings\nLinGen introduces a linear-complexity f...[truncated]"
  },
