  "serper::Anne-Marie George": {
    "homepage": "https://www.mn.uio.no/ifi/english/people/aca/annemage/",
    "emails": [
      "annemage@ifi.uio.no",
      "nettredaktor@ifi.uio.no"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Anne-Marie George\n       - Department of Informatics\nUiO\nThe Faculty of Mathematics and Natural Sciences\nDepartment of Informatics\nMenu\nAnne-Marie George\nAssociate Professor\n          -\nScientific Computing and Machine Learning\nNorwegian\nversion of this page\nEmail\nannemage@ifi.uio.no\nRoom\n8164\nUsername\nLog in\nVisiting address\nOle Johan Dahls hus\nPostal address\nPostboks 1080 Blindern\n0316 Oslo\nOther affiliations\nThe International Summer School\n(Student)\nDownload business card\nAcademic Interests\nHuman preferences are notoriously difficult to capture—yet they lie at the heart of every decision we make. Because of this, they play a central role in many areas of AI. My research explores several facets of preference handling: from modelling and inferring individual preferences, to aggregating group preferences (and addressing the fairness challenges that arise), to designing efficient methods for eliciting preferences when they are not fully known.\nInferring and predicting individual preferences is essential for recommendation systems—platforms like Spotify or Netflix rely on it to deliver personalised content. Decisions involving multiple stakeholders' preferences appear in classical social choice settings such as voting, matching, and fair division, as well as in contemporary applications like fine-tuning language models using diverse (and sometimes conflicting) human preferences. In these contexts, fairness, representation of preference clusters, and attention to minority groups are vital.\nWhen individual preferences are unknown or incomplete, elicitation becomes necessary. I am particularly interested in efficient preference elicitation for group decisions—minimising user effort while still enabling high-quality collective outcomes. Here, individual preferences need not be perfectly accurate; what matters is that the resulting group decision is sound.\nAnother line of my research examines the interplay between recourse and fairness. I study how action recommendations—such as advice on obtaining a loan, securing admission to a desired study program, or qualifying for a public benefit—affect populations when resources (e.g., budgets or available places) are limited. In particular, I am interested in how the validity of such recourse changes over time and how fair these recommendations are for the population as a whole.\nTags:\npreference learning\n,\npreference modelling\n,\ncomputational social choice\n,\nalgorithmic fairness\n,\nmulti-armed bandit problems\nPublications\nScientific articles and book chapters\nOther\nGeorge, Anne-Marie & Castillo, Ana Ozaki Rivera\n(2025).\nOn Middle Grounds for Preference Statements.\nIJCAI International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence.\nISSN 1045-0823.\ndoi:\n10.24963/ijcai.2025/503\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nShow summary\nIn group decisions or deliberations, stakeholders are often confronted with conflicting opinions. We investigate a logic-based way of expressing such opinions and a formal general notion of a middle ground between stakeholders. Inspired by the literature on preferences with hierarchical and lexicographic models, we instantiate our general framework to the case where stakeholders express their opinions using preference statements of the form ‘I prefer ‘a’ to ‘b’’, where ‘a’ and ‘b’ are alternatives expressed over some attributes, e.g., in a trolley problem, one can express I prefer to save 1 adult and 1 child to 2 adults (and 0 children). We prove theoretical results on the existence and uniqueness of middle grounds. In particular, we show that, for preference statements, middle grounds may not exist and may not be unique. We provide algorithms for deciding the existence and finding middle grounds.\nAthanasopoulos, Andreas; George, Anne-Marie & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2025).\nProbably Correct Optimal Stable Matching for Two-Sided Markets Under Uncertainty.\nIn y Das, Sanmay; Nowé, Ann & Vorobeychik, Yevgeniy (Ed.),\nAAMAS '25: Proceedings of the 24th International Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems.\nAssociation for Computing Machinery (ACM)\n.\nISSN 9798400714269.\np. 152–160.\ndoi:\nhttps:/www.ifaamas.org/Proceedings/aamas2025/pdfs/p152.pdf\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nShow summary\nWe consider a learning problem for the stable marriage model under unknown preferences for the left side of the market. We focus on the centralized case, where at each time step, an online platform matches the agents and obtains a noisy evaluation reflecting their preferences. Our aim is to quickly identify the stable matching that is left-side optimal, rendering this a pure exploration problem with bandit feedback. We specifically aim to find Probably Correct Optimal Stable Matchings and present several bandit algorithms to do so. Our findings provide a foundational understanding of how to efficiently gather and utilize preference information to identify the optimal stable matching in two-sided markets under uncertainty. An experimental analysis on synthetic data complements theoretical results on sample complexities for the proposed methods.\nGeorge, Anne-Marie & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2024).\nEliciting Kemeny Rankings.\nProceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence.\nISSN 2159-5399.\n38(11),\np. 12164–12171.\ndoi:\n10.1609/aaai.v38i11.29105\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nKindermann, Christian; George, Anne-Marie; Parsia, Bijan & Sattler, Uli\n(2024).\nMinimal Macro-Based Rewritings of Formal Languages: Theory and Applications in Ontology Engineering (and Beyond).\nProceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence.\nISSN 2159-5399.\ndoi:\n10.1609/aaai.v38i9.28928\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nSegal, Meirav; George, Anne-Marie & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2024).\nThreshold Recourse for Dynamic Allocations.\nSeptentrio Conference Series.\nFull text in Research Archive\nSegal, Meirav; George, Anne-Marie; Yu, Ingrid Chieh & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2024).\nBetter Luck Next Time: About Robust Recourse in Binary Allocation Problems.\nCommunications in Computer and Information Science (CCIS).\nISSN 1865-0929.\n2155,\np. 374–394.\ndoi:\n10.1007/978-3-031-63800-8_19\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nSegal, Meirav; George, Anne-Marie & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2023).\nPolicy Fairness and Unknown Bias Dynamics in Sequential Allocations.\nIn Patro, Gourab K. & Verastegui-Grunewald, Felipe (Ed.),\nEAAMO '23: Proceedings of the 3rd ACM Conference on Equity and Access in Algorithms, Mechanisms, and Optimization.\nAssociation for Computing Machinery (ACM)\n.\nISSN 9798400703812.\ndoi:\n10.1145/3617694.3623262\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nSegal, Meirav; George, Anne-Marie; Yu, Ingrid Chieh & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2023).\nRobust Recourse for Binary Allocation Problems.\nNeurips 2023 workshop on XAI in Action: Past, Present, and Future Applications.\nFull text in Research Archive\nBrill, Markus; Delemazure, Théo; George, Anne-Marie; Lackner, Martin & Schmidt-Kraepelin, Ulrike\n(2022).\nLiquid Democracy with Ranked Delegations.\nProceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence.\nISSN 2159-5399.\n36(5),\np. 4884–4891.\ndoi:\n10.1609/aaai.v36i5.20417\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nBredereck, Robert; George, Anne-Marie; Israel, Jonas & Kellerhals, Leon\n(2022).\nSingle-Peaked Opinion Updates.\nIJCAI International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence.\nISSN 1045-0823.\nFull text in Research Archive\nBüning, Thomas Kleine; George, Anne-Marie & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2022).\nInteractive Inverse Reinforcement Learning for Cooperative Games.\nProceedings of Machine Learning Research (PMLR).\nFull text in Research Archive\nSegal, Meirav; George, Anne-Marie & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2022).\nSimulating University Application Data for Fair Matchings.\nCommunications in Computer and Information Science (CCIS).\nISSN 1865-0929.\n1650,\np. 132–138.\ndoi:\n10.1007/978-3-031-17030-0_11\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nBrams, Steven; Brill, Markus & George, Anne-Marie\n(2021).\nThe excess method: a multiwinner approval voting procedure to allocate wasted votes.\nSocial Choice and Welfare.\nISSN 0176-1714.\ndoi:\n10.1007/s00355-021-01358-3\n.\nFull text in Research Archive\nView all works in NVA\nZobolas, John; George, Anne-Marie; López, Alberto; Fischer, Sebastian; Becker, Marc & Aittokallio, Tero Antero\n(2025).\nA Pareto-Driven Ensemble Feature Selection Approach Optimizes Biomarker Discovery in Multi-omics Pancreatic Cancer Studies.\nFull text in Research Archive\nShow summary\nPrediction of patient survival using high-dimensional multi-omics data requires systematic feature selection methods that ensure predictive performance, sparsity, and reliability for prognostic biomarker discovery. We developed a hybrid ensemble feature selection (hEFS) approach that combines data subsampling with multiple prognostic models, integrating both embedded and wrapper-based strategies for survival prediction. Omics features are ranked using a voting-theory-inspired aggregation mechanism across models and subsamples, while the optimal number of features is selected via a Pareto front, balancing predictive accuracy and model sparsity without any user-defined thresholds. When applied to multi-omics datasets from three pancreatic cancer cohorts, hEFS identifies significantly fewer and more stable biomarkers compared to conventional, late-fusion CoxLasso models, while maintaining comparable discrimination performance. Implemented within the open-source mlr3fselect R package, hEFS offers a robust, interpretable, and clinically valuable tool for prognostic modelling and biomarker discovery in high-dimensional survival settings.\nSegal, Meirav; Leqi, Liu; George, Anne-Marie; Dimitrakakis, Christos & Heidari, Hoda\n(2023).\nAllocating Bonus Points in Sequential Matchings with Preference Dynamics.\nNeurIPS 2023 workshop: Algorithmic Fairness through the Lens of Time.\nFull text in Research Archive\nBüning, Thomas Kleine; Segal, Meirav; George, Anne-Marie; Basu, Debabrota & Dimitrakakis, Christos\n(2022).\nOn Meritocracy in Optimal Set Selection.\nFull text in Rese...[truncated]"
  },
