    "github": "https://github.com/leozoroaster",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "About me - Zhou Lu (陆洲)’s Homepage\nZhou Lu\nResearch scientist at Meta\nFollow\nNJ, USA\nGithub\nGoogle Scholar\nAbout me\nI am a research scientist at Meta.\nI received my Ph.D. in CS at Princeton University, advised by Professor\nElad Hazan\n. Previously, I received a B.S. degree in Math at Peking University, advised by Professor\nLiwei Wang\n.\nPublications\nSparsity-Based Interpolation of External, Internal and Swap Regret\n.\nZhou Lu, Y. Jennifer Sun, Zhiyu Zhang. COLT 2025.\nWhen is Inductive Inference Possible?\n.\nZhou Lu. Neurips 2024 (\nspotlight\n).\nOnline Control in Population Dynamics\n.\nNoah Golowich, Elad Hazan, Zhou Lu, Dhruv Rohatgi, Y. Jennifer Sun. Neurips 2024.\nTight Rates for Bandit Control Beyond Quadratics\n.\nY. Jennifer Sun, Zhou Lu. Neurips 2024.\nAdaptive Online Learning of Quantum States\n.\nXinyi Chen, Elad Hazan, Tongyang Li, Zhou Lu, Xinzhao Wang, Rui Yang. Quantum.\nAdaptive Regret for Bandits Made Possible: Two Queries Suffice\n.\nZhou Lu, Qiuyi Zhang, Xinyi Chen, Fred Zhang, David Woodruff, Elad Hazan. ICLR 2024.\nOn the Computational Benefit of Multimodal Learning\n.\nZhou Lu. ALT 2024.\nA Theory of Multimodal Learning\n.\nZhou Lu. Neurips 2023.\nProjection-free adaptive regret with membership oracles\n.\nZhou Lu, Nataly Brukhim, Paula Gradu, Elad Hazan. ALT 2023.\nNon-convex online learning via algorithmic equivalence\n.\nUdaya Ghai, Zhou Lu, Elad Hazan. Neurips 2022.\nTowards Certifying L-infinity Robustness using Neural Networks with L-inf-dist Neurons\n.\nBohang Zhang, Tianle Cai, Zhou Lu, Di He, Liwei Wang. ICML 2021.\nBoosting for control of dynamical systems\n.\nNaman Agarwal, Nataly Brukhim, Elad Hazan, Zhou Lu. ICML 2020.\nThe expressive power of neural networks: A view from the width\n.\nZhou Lu, Hongming Pu, Feicheng Wang, Zhiqiang Hu, Liwei Wang. NIPS 2017.\nServices\nReviewers for Neurips, ICML, ICLR, COLT, STOC, FOCS, ICALP, Mathematical Programming, IEEE-TPAMI."
  },
  "serper::Pan Kessel": {
    "homepage": "https://www.gene.com/scientists/our-scientists/pan-kessel",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Genentech: Pan Kessel | Machine Learning Scientist, Prescient Design, Computational Sciences\nAttention: This page may not render properly. The browser you are using, Internet Explorer 10, is no longer supported.\nScientists\n/\nOur Scientists\nPan Kessel\nMachine Learning Scientist, Prescient Design, Computational Sciences\n3\nYears at Genentech\nI obtained my PhD in String Theory from the Max Planck Institute for Gravitational Physics. I then\nswitched to Machine Learning and became a member of the Machine Learning Group at TU\nBerlin.\nFeatured Publication\nAsymptotically unbiased estimation of physical observables with neural samplers\nPhys. Rev. E 101, 023304\nKim A. Nicoli, Shinichi Nakajima, Nils Strodthoff, Wojciech Samek, Klaus-Robert Müller, and Pan Kessel\nView Abstract\nEstimation of Thermodynamic Observables in Lattice Field Theories with Deep Generative Models\nPhys. Rev. Lett. 126, 032001\nKim A. Nicoli, Christopher J. Anders, Lena Funcke, Tobias Hartung, Karl Jansen, Pan Kessel, Shinichi Nakajima, and Paolo Stornati\nView Abstract\nExplanations can be manipulated and geometry is to blame\nAnn-Kathrin Dombrowski, Maximillian Alber, Christopher Anders, Marcel Ackermann, Klaus-Robert Müller, Pan Kessel\nView Abstract\nPath-Gradient Estimators for Continuous Normalizing Flows\nPMLR 162:21945-21959\nLorenz Vaitl, Kim Andrea Nicoli, Shinichi Nakajima, Pan Kessel\nView Abstract\nMy Focus\nMy research focus is mainly on generative models and their applications to physics. In particular, I am interested in using generative models to speed up sampling problems. I have also worked on interpretability and its connection to generative models. Recently, I have been also thinking about NTK theory and Bayesian optimization.\nEducation\nMax Planck Institute, PhD – 2017\n© 2026 Genentech, Inc. All rights reserved. This site is intended for US residents only."
  },
