    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Jongyeong Lee\nJongyeong Lee\nPostdoctoral Researcher (Technical Research Personnel, 전문연) in the Computational Science Research Center at the Korea Institute of Science and Technology (KIST) hosted by Prof. Chansoo Kim.\nI am currently looking for next academic positions at universities or research institutes starting after December (when my military duty ends).\nIf you know of any relevant opportunities, please let me know (not restricted to Korea)!\n[Google Scholar]\n[CV]\nContact: jongyeong [at] kist.re.kr / jongyeong.lee95 [at] gmail.com  (in general, gmail is faster)\nResearch Interests\nMachine Learning\nBandit problems: usually interested in simple algorithms and simple settings\nOptimization\nInformation Geometry\nCan the geometry of statistical models provide meaningful description that can be utilized in designing algorithms in bandit problems? (such as geodesic, divergence, alpha prior)\nNews\nNov 17, 2025: Our paper “Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader for Decoupled Bandits: Best-of-Both-Worlds and Practicality” received a Best Paper Award at the KAIA Fall Conference 2025!\nOct 17, 2025: I was selected as a top reviewer at NeurIPS 2025!\nOct 13, 2025: I moved to the Computational Science Research Center at the Korea Institute of Science and Technology.\nOct 02, 2025: Our paper \"Revisiting Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader with Unbounded Perturbations in Bandit Problems,\" has been accepted to\nNeurIPS 2025\n! This paper, along with our paper “Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader for Decoupled Bandits: Best-of-Both-Worlds and Practicality”, was also accepted to the\nMLxOR workshop\nat NeurIPS2025.\nAug 06, 2025: There was a technical flaw in the proof of our paper \"Thompson Exploration with Best Challenger Rule in Best Arm Identification\" published at ACML 2023. I fortunately corrected the proof without changing the overall result, which is available on\narXiv\n.\nMay 01, 2025: Our paper \"Geometric Resampling in Nearly Linear Time for Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader with Best-of-Both-Worlds Guarantee in Bandit Problems,\" has been accepted to\nICML 2025\n!\nNov 05, 2024: I was selected as a top reviewer at NeurIPS 2024!\nMay 18, 2024: My research proposal has been accepted for the Sejong Science Fellowship by the National Research Foundation of Korea!\nMay 13, 2024: Our paper \"Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader with Fréchet-type Tail Distributions: Optimality in Adversarial Bandits and Best-of-Both-Worlds,\" has been accepted to\nCOLT 2024\n!\nDec 09, 2023: Our paper \"The Choice of Noninformative Priors for Thompson Sampling in Multiparameter Bandit Models\" has been accepted to\nAAAI 2024\nas an oral paper!\nPublications\nConference Proceedings (peer-reviewed, *: equal contribution)\nJongyeong Lee\n, Junya Honda, Shinji Ito, and Min-hwan Oh,\n\"Revisiting Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader with Unbounded Perturbations in Bandit Problems,\"\nThe 39th Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025), pp. xx-xx, San Diego, USA, Dec. 2-7, 2025.\n[\nopenreview\n] \n      [\nposter\n]\nBotao Chen\n*,\nJongyeong Lee\n*, and Junya Honda,\n\"Geometric Resampling in Nearly Linear Time for Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader with Best-of-Both-Worlds Guarantee in Bandit Problems,\"\nThe 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025), pp. 8403-8426, Vancouver, Canada, Jul. 13-Jul. 19, 2025.\n[\nproceeding\n]\n      [\nposter by Botao\n]\nJongyeong Lee\n, Junya Honda,\nShinji Ito\n, and\nMin-hwan Oh\n,\n\"Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader with Fréchet-type Tail Distributions: Optimality in Adversarial Bandits and Best-of-Both-Worlds,\"\nThe 37th Annual Conference on Learning Theory (COLT 2024), pp. 3375-3430, Edmonton, Canada, Jun. 30-Jul. 3, 2024.\n[\nproceeding\n]\n      [\nposter\n]\n      [\nslides\n]\nJongyeong Lee\n, Chao-Kai Chiang, and Masashi Sugiyama,\n\"The Choice of Noninformative Priors for Thompson Sampling in Multiparameter Bandit Models\"\nThe 38th AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2024), pp. 13383-13390, Vancouver, Canada, Feb. 20-27, 2024.\nOral\n[\narXiv (full)\n]\n      [\nproceeding\n]\n      [\nposter\n]\n      [\nslides\n]\nJongyeong Lee\n, Junya Honda, and Masashi Sugiyama,\n\"Thompson Exploration with Best Challenger Rule in Best Arm Identification\"\nThe 15th Asian Conference on Machine Learning (ACML 2023), pp. 646-661, Istanbul, Turkey, Nov. 11-14, 2023.\nLong Oral\n[\nproceeding\n]\n      [\narXiv (Corrigendum)\n]\n      [\nposter\n]\n      [\nslides\n]\nJongyeong Lee\n,\nJunya Honda\n, Chao-Kai Chiang, and Masashi Sugiyama,\n\"Optimality of Thompson Sampling with Noninformative Priors for Pareto Bandits\"\nThe 40th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML2023), pp. 18810-18851, Hawaii, USA, Jul. 23-29, 2023.\n[\nproceeding\n]\n      [\nposter\n]\nNontawat Charoenphakdee,\nJongyeong Lee\n, Yiping Jin, Dittaya Wanvarie, and Masashi Sugiyama,\n\"Learning Only from Relevant Keywords and Unlabeled Documents\"\nThe 2019 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing and 9th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing (EMNLP-IJCNLP2019), pp. 3993–4002, Hong Kong, Nov. 3-7, 2019.\n[\nproceeding\n]\n      [\nmore information by Nutt\n]\nNontawat Charoenphakdee\n,\nJongyeong Lee\n, and\nMasashi Sugiyama\n,\n\"On Symmetric Losses for Learning from Corrupted Labels\"\nThe 36th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML2019), pp. 961-970, Long Beach, California, USA, Jun. 9-15, 2019.\nLong Oral\n[\nproceeding\n]\n      [\nmore information by Nutt\n]\nOthers (preprints / workshops)\nChaiwon Kim\n*,\nJongyeong Lee\n*, and Min-hwan Oh,\n“Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader for Decoupled Bandits: Best-of-Both-Worlds and Practicality”\nNeurIPS 2025 Workshop MLxOR: Mathematical Foundations and Operational Integration of Machine Learning for Uncertainty-Aware Decision-Making.\nBest Paper Award\nat KAIA (Korean Artificial Intelligence Association) Fall Conference 2025 (JKAIA 2025).\n[\narXiv\n]\nNontawat Charoenphakdee,\nJongyeong Lee\n, and Masashi Sugiyama,\n“A Symmetric Loss Perspective of Reliable Machine Learning”\nThe Fields Institute Communications Series on\nData Science and Optimization\n, 2021.\nAn invited article preprint [\narXiv\n]\nJongyeong Lee\n, Nontawat Charoenphakdee, and Masashi Sugiyama,\n“Domain Discrepancy Measure Using Complex Models in Unsupervised Domain Adaptation”\nSymbolic-Neural Learning Workshop (SNL 2019), 2019.\n[\narXiv\n]\nGrants\nSejong Science Fellowship (domestic)\n, National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF), May. 2024 - Apr. 2029 (expected), appx. 130M KRW per year\n세종과학펠로우쉽 (국내)\nSPRING GX\n, Japan Science and Technology Agency (JST), Oct. 2021 - Mar. 2023, appx. 2.8M JPY per year\nEducation\nSep. 2023 : Ph.D. in Department of Computer Science, The University of Tokyo (supervisor: Prof.\nMasashi Sugiyama\n, committee chair: Prof.\nKen-ichi Kawarabayashi\n)\nThesis: \"On Thompson Sampling with Noninformative Priors in Stochastic Multi-Armed Bandits\" [\nlink\n] [\npdf\n]\n     [\ndefense\n]\nApr. 2020 : M.S. in Department of Computer Science, The University of Tokyo (supervisor: Prof.\nMasashi Sugiyama\n)\nFeb. 2017 : B.E. in Electrical Engineering, Korea University (one-year early graduation)\nWork Experiences\nOct. 2025 – present : Postdoctoral researcher (Technical Research Personnel, 전문연), Korea Institute of Science and Technology hosted by Prof. Chansoo Kim\nDec. 2023 – Oct. 2025 : Postdoctoral researcher (Technical Research Personnel, 전문연), Seoul National University hosted by Prof. Min-hwan Oh\nOct. 2018 – Sep. 2023 : Research part-time worker, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project\nReivewer\nCOLT(2026-), ICML(2024-), NeurIPS(2024-,Top reviewer; 2024, 2025), TMLR(2025-).\nACML(2023-2024), AISTATS(2024), ICLR(2026).\n© Jongyeong Lee, Jan 25, 2026"
