    "homepage_text": "Linyuan Gong\nSearch\nLinyuan Gong\nPhD Student in Artificial Intelligence\nEECS Department, UC Berkeley\nI am a PhD student in Computer Science at University of California, Berkeley, advised by\nProf. Alvin Cheung\n. I was also advised by\nProf. Dawn Song\nat UC Berkeley. Before that, I received my Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science from Peking University, China, advised by\nProf. Liwei Wang\nand\nProf. Di He\n.\nI specialize in Artificial Intelligence (AI) with a focus on Large Language Models (LLMs). My research includes pretraining, prompting, and evaluation methodologies for a variety of language models, including BERT, T5, and GPT-like LLMs. My recent research delves into leveraging LLMs for code generation, infilling, transpilation, and understanding, pushing the boundaries of how AI interacts with programming languages.\nInterests\nArtificial Intelligence\nNatural Language Processing\nLarge Language Models\nEducation\nPh.D. in Computer Science, 2020 - Present\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nB.S. in Computer Science, 2016 - 2020\nPeking University, Beijing, China\nFeatured Publications\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nSida Wang\n,\nMostafa Elhoushi\n,\nAlvin Cheung\nMarch, 2024\nIn ICML 2024\nEvaluation of LLMs on Syntax-Aware Code Fill-in-the-Middle Tasks\nWe introduce Syntax-Aware Fill-In-the-Middle (SAFIM), a new benchmark for evaluating Large Language Models (LLMs) on the code Fill-in-the-Middle (FIM) task.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nDataset\nProject\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nMostafa Elhoushi\n,\nAlvin Cheung\nJanuary, 2024\nIn ICML 2024\nAST-T5: Structure-Aware Pretraining for Code Generation and Understanding\nWe introduce AST-T5, a novel pretraining paradigm that leverages the Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) for enhanced code generation, transpilation, and understanding.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nChenyan Xiong\n,\nXiaodong Liu\n,\nPayal Bajaj\n,\nYiqing Xie\n,\nAlvin Cheung\n,\nJianfeng Gao\n,\nXia Song\nJuly, 2023\nIn ACL 2023\nModel-Generated Pretraining Signals Improves Zero-Shot Generalization of Text-to-Text Transformers\nThis paper explores the effectiveness of model-generated signals in improving zero-shot generalization of text-to-text Transformers such as T5.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nDOI\nRecent Publications\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nJiayi Wang\n,\nAlvin Cheung\n(2023).\nADELT: Transpilation Between Deep Learning Frameworks\n.\nIn IJCAI 2024.\nPDF\nCite\nJianhao Shen\n,\nChenguang Wang\n,\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nDawn Song\n(2022).\nJoint Language Semantic and Structure Embedding for Knowledge Graph Completion\n.\nIn COLING 2022.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nXinyun Chen\n,\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nAlvin Cheung\n,\nDawn Song\n(2021).\nPlotCoder: Hierarchical Decoding for Synthesizing Visualization Code in Programmatic Context\n.\nIn ACL 2021.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nVideo\nDOI\nYilun Xu\n,\nYang Song\n,\nSahaj Garg\n,\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nRui Shu\n,\nAditya Grover\n,\nStefano Ermon\n(2021).\nAnytime Sampling for Autoregressive Models via Ordered Autoencoding\n.\nIn ICLR 2021.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nSlides\nJuyong Kim\n,\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nJustin Khim\n,\nJeremy C. Weiss\n,\nPradeep Ravikumar\n(2020).\nImproved Clinical Abbreviation Expansion via Non-Sense-Based Approaches\n.\nIn ML4H (NeurIPS Workshop) 2020.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nZhenhui Xu\n,\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nGuolin Ke\n,\nDi He\n,\nShuxin Zheng\n,\nLiwei Wang\n,\nJiang Bian\n,\nTie-Yan Liu\n(2020).\nMC-BERT: Efficient Language Pre-Training via a Meta Controller\n.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nYingce Xia\n,\nXu Tan\n,\nFei Tian\n,\nFei Gao\n,\nWeicong Chen\n,\nYang Fan\n,\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nYichong Leng\n,\nRenqian Luo\n,\nYiren Wang\n,\nLijun Wu\n,\nJinhua Zhu\n,\nTao Qin\n,\nTie-Yan Liu\n(2019).\nMicrosoft Research Asia's Systems for WMT19\n.\nIn WMT19 (ACL 2019 Workshop).\nPDF\nCite\nDOI\nLinyuan Gong\n,\nDi He\n,\nZhuohan Li\n,\nTao Qin\n,\nLiwei Wang\n,\nTieyan Liu\n(2019).\nEfficient training of BERT by progressively stacking\n.\nIn ICML 2019.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nCite\n×\nCopy\nDownload"
