    "homepage_text": "Yunzong Xu\nYunzong Xu\nCurrent Version: March 2025\nAbout Me\nI recently started my position as an assistant professor in the Department of\nISE\nand the\nCSL\nat the University of Illinois, Urbana-Champaign. I am also affiliated with the Departments of\nCS\nand\nECE\n.\nThis site is a work in progress, much like this stage of my life. I hope to find time for a meaningful update soon.\nResearch\nI do research in machine learning theory, foundations of AI, operations research, and management science. Specific topics of interest include online learning, deep learning, sequential decision making, and mathematical problems related to markets, incentives, and social good.\nBelow is a list of my publications.\n1\nJournal Publications\nBypassing the Monster: A Faster and Simpler Optimal Algorithm for Contextual Bandits Under Realizability\n(with David Simchi-Levi)\nMathematics of Operations Research\n(2022)\nHonorable Mention,\nINFORMS George Nicholson Student Paper Competition\n, 2020\nOnline Pricing with Offline Data: Phase Transition and Inverse Square Law\n(with Jinzhi Bu and David Simchi-Levi)\nManagement Science\n(2022)\nPreliminary version appeared in\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning\n(2020)\nWinner,\nINFORMS Data Mining Best Theoretical Paper Award\n, 2020\nPhase Transitions in Bandits with Switching Constraints\n(with David Simchi-Levi)\nManagement Science\n(2023)\nPreliminary version appeared in\nNeural Information Processing Systems\n(2019)\nFinalist,\nApplied Probability Society Best Student Paper Award\n, 2019\nAssortment Optimization for a Multi-Stage Choice Model\n(with Zizhuo Wang)\nManufacturing & Service Operations Management\n(2023)\nFinalist,\nINFORMS Undergraduate Operations Research Prize\n, 2018\nBlind Network Revenue Management and Bandits with Knapsacks Under Limited Switches\n(with David Simchi-Levi and Jinglong Zhao)\nOperations Research\n(2025)\nFinalist, IBM Service Science Best Student Paper Award, 2021\nConference Publications\nInstance-Dependent Complexity of Contextual Bandits and Reinforcement Learning: A Disagreement-Based Perspective\n(with Dylan Foster, Alexander Rakhlin, David Simchi-Levi)\nJournal version to be submitted\nConference version accepted to\nConference on Learning Theory\n(2021)\nOffline Reinforcement Learning: Fundamental Barriers for Value Function Approximation\n(with Dylan Foster, Akshay Krishnamurthy, David Simchi-Levi)\nJournal version to be submitted\nConference version accepted to\nConference on Learning Theory\n(2022)\nOral Presentation, NeurIPS 2021 Offline Reinforcement Learning Workshop\nGreedy Algorithm for Structured Bandits: A Sharp Characterization of Asymptotic Success/Failure\n(with Alex Slivkins and Shiliang Zuo)\nJournal version to be submitted\nConference version accepted to\nNeural Information Processing Systems\n(2025)\nSelected Working Papers\nFeature-Based Dynamic Pricing with Online Learning and Offline Data\n(with Jinzhi Bu, David Simchi-Levi, and Sabrina Zhai)\nSpatial Matching of Random Samples on a Circle: Theoretic Analysis and Applications on Ride-Hailing Systems\n(with Hao Liang and Zizhuo Wang)\nMeta Dynamic Pricing with Nonparametric Empirical Bayes\n(with Jinhui Han, Ming Hu, and Xilin Zhang)\nDoctoral Thesis\nData-Driven Dynamic Decision Making: Algorithms, Structures, and Complexity Analysis\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology (2023)\nTeaching\nIE598 Foundations of Modern Machine Learning (Fall 2024)\nA new graduate course I introduced at UIUC, covering theoretical foundations of statistical learning, online learning (full and partial information), and deep learning, with applications in modern AI and data-driven decision making\nFeatured in the\nList of Teachers Ranked as Excellent by Their Students\nContact\nEmail: xyz at illinois dot edu\nFor PhD applicants: Thank you for your interest. Please apply directly through the ISE, CS, or ECE departments. Due to the high volume of inquiries, I’m unable to respond to individual questions regarding PhD admissions. I appreciate your understanding.\n1. Authorship is ordered alphabetically by default.\n↩"
  },
  "serper::Hengrui Cai": {
