    "github": "https://github.com/TedSIWEILIU",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Welcome to Siwei’s homepage - Homepage of Siwei\nSiwei Liu\nAssistant Professor of University of Aberdeen\nFollow\nScotland\nResearchGate\nTwitter\nGithub\nGoogle Scholar\nORCID\nWelcome to Siwei’s homepage\nI am an Assistant Professor at the School of Natural and Computing Science, University of Aberdeen, UK. Before this position, I was a Postdoctoral researcher at MBZUAI. Currently, I work on the following topics with my collaborators all over the world:\nLLM Multi-agent Collaboration\nGNN for biomedical network interaction (e.g., DNA, RNA, Protein, Diseases, Bacteria)\nMulti-modal Single-cell Analysis\nBiomedical Image Analysis\nI used to be a PhD student of\nTerrier team\n, supervised by Prof.\nIadh Ounis\nand Prof.\nCraig Macdonald\n.\nI am one of the co-founders and main contributors of the\nBeta-Recsys\nproject, an open-source project for Building, Evaluating and Tuning Automated Recommender Systems.\nNews\nCheck out our open-source project\nEvoAgentX\n.\nSept, 2025.          Our paper entitled\nEvoAgentX: An automated framework for evolving agentic workflows\nis accepted as a Demo Paper by EMNLP 2025.\nAug, 2025.          We have established the latest survey for self-evolving agent on arxiv:\n*A comprehensive survey of self-evolving ai agents: A new paradigm bridging foundation models and lifelong agentic systems *\n.\nJune, 2025.          Our paper entitled\nRRGMambaFormer: A hybrid Transformer-Mamba architecture for radiology report generation\nis accepted by Expert Systems with Applications.\nOct, 2024.          Our paper entitled\nActive Learning for Multi-Class Drug-Drug Interactions Prediction\nis accepted by IEEE, International Conference on Bioinformatics and Biomedicine.\nOct, 2024.          Our paper entitled\nPASCL: supervised contrastive learning with perturbative augmentation for particle decay reconstruction\nis accepted by Machine Learning: Science and Technology.\nOct, 2024.          Our paper entitled\nEnhancing Graph Neural Networks via Memorized Global Information\nis accepted by ACM Transactions on the Web.\nAug, 2024.          Our paper entitled\nHeterogeneous biomedical entity representation learning for gene–disease association prediction\nis accepted by Briefings in Bioinformatics.\nJune, 2024.          Our paper entitled\nBPP: A Platform for Automatic Biochemical Pathway Prediction\nis accepted by Briefings in Bioinformatics.\nNov, 2023.          Our paper entitled\nA Social-aware Gaussian Pre-trained Model for Effective Cold-start Recommendation\nis accepted by Information Processing and Management.\nNov, 2023.          Our paper entitled\nMessages Are Never Propagated Alone: Collaborative Hypergraph Neural Network for Time-series Forecasting\nis accepted by TPAMI.\nAug, 2023.          Our paper entitled\nSequence pre-training-based graph neural network for predicting lncRNA-miRNA associations\nis accepted by Briefings in Bioinformatics (IF=9.5).\nAug, 2023.          Our paper entitled\nHMCDA: a novel method based on the heterogeneous graph neural network and metapath for circRNA-disease associations prediction\nis accepted by BMC Bioinformatics (IF=3.327).\nMay, 2023.          Our paper entitled\nLeveraging Relational Graph Neural Network for Transductive Model Ensemble\nis accepted by SIGKDD (CCF A).\nNov, 2022.          I gave a talk on\nGraph Neural Networks\nat\nUN Big Data Hackathon 2022\n. The powerpoint is available\nhere\n.\nOct, 2022.         I start my Postdoc at\nMBZUAI\n(Abu Dhabi) with Dr Shangsong Liang.\nOct. 2022.         Our paper entitled\nGraph neural pre-training for recommendation with side information\nis accepted by ACM TOIS (CCF A).\nMar. 2022.          Our paper entitled\nAn MLP-based Algorithm for Efficient Contrastive Graph Recommendations\nis accepted by ACM SIGIR (CCF A)."
  },
  "serper::Mingtian Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://mingtian.ai/",
    "emails": [
      "mail@mingtian.ai"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Mingtian Zhang\nMingtian Zhang\nHome\nPublications\nSlides\nBlog\nMingtian Zhang\nResearch Fellow in Machine Learning\nUniversity College London\nHi, I am Mingtian.\nI'm currently a Research Fellow at the\nCentre for Artificial Intelligence at UCL\n. I just finished my PhD in machine learning from UCL, supervised by\nProf. David Barber\nand\nProf. Brooks Paige\n. My examiners are\nProf Carlo Ciliberto\nand\nProf Yee Whye Teh\n.\nMy research revolves around the methodologies and applications of probabilistic machine learning, especially on generative models. Check out the\nPublications\nand\nBlog\nfor my research and thoughts on this topic.\nBeyond academia, I am the founder of\nPageIndex\n, a startup which focuses on building the next generation Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system."
  },
  "serper::Qiang Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://qiangairesearcher.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "qiang.zhang.cs@zju.edu.cn"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/qiangairesearcher",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "About me - Qiang Zhang’s Homepage\nQiang Zhang\nAI Researcher & Principal Investigator in Zhejiang University\nFollow\nHangzhou, China\nEmail\nTwitter\nGithub\nGoogle Scholar\nAbout me\nHey I’m Qiang, welcome to my homepage!\nI’m a Principal Investigator under the Hundred Talents Program at Zhejiang University.\nBefore joining Zhejiang University, I was a postdoctoral research fellow in machine learning working with\nProf. Emine Yilmaz\n, affiliated with the\nCentre for Artificial Intelligence\nin\nUniversity College London (UCL)\n. I studied for the Ph.D. in UCL as well. Prior to that, I obtained M.Sc. from Chinese Academy of Sciences and B.Sc. from Shandong University.\nI have wide connections with academic and industrial collaborators. I received grants from Google, Nvidia and Microsoft Research, and had internships in Amazon and Facebook AI Research. I have been closely collaborating with the\nInstitute for Medical Engineering & Science\nin MIT and\nMila\nin Montreal.\nMy research interests lie in the area of machine learning and span the entire theory-to-application spectrum from fundamental advances (e.g., Bayesian inference and meta learning) to practical progress (e.g., text mining, sequential data modeling). My research has been applied to text classification, knowledge graphs, information retrieval, recommender systems, and misinformation detection. Recently, I am mainly focusing on AI applications to scientific discovery.\nI am looking for undergraduate/graduate students, interns, postdocs and research engineers. Please drop me an email if you are interested in working with me.\nLatest News\n03.12.2021 - Three research papers accepted by AAAI 2022.\n21.11.2021 - Invited to give a talk on Bayesian meta-learning at University of Dallas.\n29.09.2021 - Two research papers accepted by NeurIPS 2021.\n15.01.2021 - One first-author research paper accepted by WWW 2021.\n26.09.2020 - One first co-author research paper accepted by NeurIPS 2020.\n30.07.2020 - Invited to give a talk on Self-Attentive Hawkes Processes at Yahoo Research, Verizon Media.\n07.06.2020 - Release\nBeta-RecSys\n, an open souce benchmark for neural recommendation systems. Tutorial is\nhere\n.\n01.06.2020 - One first-author research paper accepted by ICML 2020.\n15.03.2020 - Invited to serve as the reviewer of EMNLP 2020.\n22.12.2019 - Invited to serve as the reviewer of ACL 2020.\n20.09.2019 - Finish the 14-week summer internship in Amazon.\n24.06.2019 - Invited to attend the Google NLP summit in Zurich, Switzerland.\n19.05.2019 - Attend the Web Conference 2019 in San Francisco, USA.\n20.01.2019 - Two first-author research papers accepted by WWW 2019."
  },
  "serper::Meng Wang": {
