    "homepage_text": "Shiqian's homepage\nSkip to content\nShiqian's homepage\nShiqian Ma\nShiqian Ma\nProfessor\nDepartment of Computational Applied Mathematics and Operations Research\nDepartment of Electrical and Computer Engineering\nRice University\nAlso affiliated to:\nKen Kennedy Institute\nCenter for Computational Finance & Economic Systems\nNews:\nJan 2026, our group has two papers accepted to ICLR 2026:\nMirror Flow Matching with Heavy-Tailed Priors for Generative Modeling on Convex Domains\n, and Adaptive Gradient Descent on Riemannian Manifolds and Its Applications to Gaussian Variational Inference. Congratulations to Yunrui and Jaewook, as well as all other coauthors!\nNov 2025, our paper\nOn the Convergence of Constrained Gradient Method\nis on arxiv.\nNov 2025, our paper\nRelaxed Proximal Point Algorithm: Tight Complexity Bounds and Acceleration without Momentum\nis accepted in INFORMS Journal on Optimization.\nI am an SAC of NeurIPS 2025, ICML 2026, AISTATS 2026, an AC of ICLR 2026.\nOct 2025, our paper\nA Simple Adaptive Proximal Gradient Method for Nonconvex Optimization\nis on arxiv.\nOct 2025, our paper\nAutoBalance: An Automatic Balancing Framework for Training Physics-Informed Neural Networks\nis on arxiv.\nOct 2025, our paper\nMirror Flow Matching with Heavy-Tailed Priors for Generative Modeling on Convex Domains\nis on arxiv.\nSep 2025, two papers are accepted in NeurIPS 2025:\nRiemannian Proximal Sampler for High-accuracy Sampling on Manifolds\n, and\nASGO: Adaptive Structured Gradient Optimization\n. Congratulations to Yunrui and Kang, as well as all other coauthors!\nSep 2025, our paper\nBregman Douglas-Rachford Splitting Method\nis on arxiv.\nAug 2025, our paper\nA Single-Loop Algorithm for Decentralized Bilevel Optimization\nis accepted in Mathematics of Operations Research. Congratulations to all coauthors!\nAug 2025, our paper\nFully First-Order Methods for Decentralized Bilevel Optimization\nis accepted in IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing.\nAug 2025, our paper\nOn Relatively Smooth Optimization over Riemannian Manifolds\nis on arxiv.\nJun 2025, our paper\nExact worst-case convergence rates for Douglas-Rachford and Davis-Yin splitting methods\nis on arxiv.\nMay 2025, honored to receive the\nEditorial Contribution Award\nfrom Springer Nature for my editorial work with\nJournal of Scientific Computing\n. According to Springer Nature, “This award recognises your meticulous assessment of submissions and rigorous management of the peer review process, safeguarding the scientific accuracy of the published record”.\nMay 2025, our paper\nAn Adaptive and Parameter-Free Nesterov’s Accelerated Gradient Method for Convex Optimization\nis on arxiv.\nMay 2025, our paper\nSingle-Timescale Multi-Sequence Stochastic Approximation without Fixed Point Smoothness: Theories and Applications\nis accepted in IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing. 2025.\nMar 2025, our paper\nASGO: Adaptive Structured Gradient Optimization\nis on arxiv.\nFeb 2025, our paper\nFirst-Order Federated Bilevel Learning\nis published in AAAI.\nFeb 2025, our paper\nRiemannian Proximal Sampler for High-accuracy Sampling on Manifolds\nis on arxiv.\nFeb 2025, our paper\nAdaBB: Adaptive Barzilai-Borwein Method for Convex Optimization\nis accepted in Mathematics of Operations Research. Congratulations to Danqing and Junfeng!\nI am a Senior Area Chair of NeurIPS 2025, an Area Chair of ICML 2025, ICLR 2025 and AISTATS 2025, and a Senior Program Committee member of AAAI 2025.\nJan 2025, our paper\nTuning-Free Bilevel Optimization: New Algorithms and Convergence Analysis\nis accepted in ICLR. Congratulations to all coauthors!\nJan 2025, our paper\nEfficiently Escaping Saddle Points in Bilevel Optimization\nis accepted in Journal of Machine Learning Research. Congratulations to all coauthors!\nJan 2025, our paper\nRiemannian Bilevel Optimization\nis accepted in Journal of Machine Learning Research. Congratulations to\nJiaxiang\n!\nJan 2025, I joined the editorial board of\nSIAM Journal on Optimization\n.\nNov 2024, our paper\nA Riemannian ADMM\nis accepted in Mathematics of Operations Research. Congratulations to Jiaxiang and Tejes!\nOct 2024, together with\nKrishna Balasubramanian\n,\nShixiang Chen\n,\nJiaxiang Li\n,\nAnthony Man-Cho So\n, and\nTong Zhang\n, we received the prestigious\nINFORMS Computing Society Prize\nfor our work on Riemannian optimization! Thanks the committee for the recognition of our work! Congratulations to my amazing collaborators!\nCitation\n.\nRice News\n.\nOct 2024, our paper\nFully First-Order Methods for Decentralized Bilevel Optimization\nis on arxiv.\nOct 2024, our paper\nSingle-Timescale Multi-Sequence Stochastic Approximation without Fixed Point Smoothness: Theories and Applications\nis on arxiv.\nOct 2024, our paper\nOn the Convergence of the Projected Alternating Maximization Algorithm for Equitable and Optimal Transport\nis accepted in Journal of Machine Learning Research.\nOct 2024, our paper\nRelaxed Proximal Point Algorithm: Tight Complexity Bounds and Acceleration without Momentum\nis on arxiv.\nOct 2024, our paper\nTuning-Free Bilevel Optimization: New Algorithms and Convergence Analysis\nis on arxiv.\nSeptember 2024, our paper\nAdaptive Algorithms for Robust Phase Retrieval\nis on arxiv.\nAugust 2024, my lab is awarded an ONR grant to study Riemannian optimization. Thank you ONR!\nI am a cluster chair for the\n2025 ICCOPT\ncluster “Optimization on Manifolds”. Reach out if you are interested in organizing sessions or contributing talks.\nTogether with\nTong Zhang\n, we are organizing a session at\n2025 ICCOPT\non “Recent Progress on Bilevel Optimization for Machine Learning”.\nAugust 2024, I joined the editorial board of\nPacific Journal of Optimization\n.\nJuly 2024, our paper\nZeroth-order Riemannian Averaging Stochastic Approximation Algorithms\nis accepted in SIAM J. Optimization.\nJuly 2024, I am promoted to Full Professor at Rice University. Thanks my mentors, colleagues, collaborators, and students for all the help in this journey!\nMay 2024, I joined the editorial board of\nIISE Transactions\n.\nMay 2024, our SIAM Review paper\nNonsmooth Optimization over the Stiefel Manifold and Beyond: Proximal Gradient Method and Recent Variants\nis available online now. This is a SIAM Review SIGEST Award paper.\nEditor’s note\n.\nFeb 2024, our paper\nProblem-Parameter-Free Decentralized Nonconvex Stochastic Optimization\nis available online.\nFeb 12, 2024, I gave a talk on Decentralized Bilevel Optimization in the\nOnline Seminar Series Machine Learning NeEDS Mathematical Optimization\n. Here is the\nYoutube recording\n.\nJan 2024, our paper\nDecentralized Bilevel Optimization\nhas been accepted in Optimization Letters (special issue on Recent Advances in Bilevel Optimization and Its Applications). This is the first paper on this topic.\nJan 2024, our paper\nA New Inexact Proximal Linear Algorithm with Adaptive Stopping Criteria for Robust Phase Retrieval\nhas been accepted in IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing.\nJan 2024, we have a new paper:\nAdaBB: Adaptive Barzilai-Borwein Method for Convex Optimization\n. This is an adaptive gradient method based on BB stepsize. It is parameter-free and line-search-free. It allows large stepsize and the average of the stepsizes is lower bounded by 1/L. Essentially this provides a convergent variant of the BB method for general smooth convex optimization.\nNov 2023, we finished a new paper on decentralized bilevel optimization. We proposed a single-loop algorithm and proved its convergence rate without any heterogeneity assumptions. The paper is\nhere\n.\nOct 2023, I am now an Action Editor of Journal of Machine Learning Research (JMLR).\nOct 2023, My paper\nProximal Gradient Method for Nonsmooth Optimization over the Stiefel Manifold\nreceived the\nSIAM Review SIGEST Award!\nCoauthored with\nShixiang Chen\n,\nAnthony Man-Cho So\n, and\nTong Zhang\n. This is a great honor — every four years one paper from SIAM J. Optimization is selected for this award!\nWe are hosting the 2024 INFORMS Optimization Society Conference. Click\nhere\nfor more details.\nOct 2023, I am elected the Secretary/Treasurer of INFORMS Optimization Society.\nFully funded PhD positions are available for Fall 2024 enrollment. If you are interested in joining my group, please submit your application\nhere\nand mention my name in your application. I don’t have time to reply to inquiries, please don’t feel discouraged if you don’t receive a reply from me.\nI gave a plenary talk at\nTexas Colloquium on Distributed Learning\n. September 28-29, 2023\nI joined the editorial board of Journal of Optimization Theory and Applications.\nJuly 2023, our paper titled “\nA Riemannian smoothing steepest descent method for non-Lipschitz optimization on submanifolds\n” has been accepted in Mathematics of Operations Research. This is the first work considering algorithms for solving this type of problems. It finds important applications in robust subspace recovery, dictionary learning, and sparse PCA.\nJuly 2023, I gave a semi-plenary talk at the\nXVI International Conference on Stochastic Programming (ICSP)\nJuly 2023, my lab is awarded two NSF grants (one from CCF, the other from ECCS) to study new algorithms and theory for large-scale bilevel optimization, and decision making problems in multi-agent distributed systems with applications in energy, power, control and networks. These are  collaborative projects with Dr.\nKaiyi Ji\nat University at Buffalo. Thank you NSF!\nJuly 2023, our paper entitled “\nFederated Learning on Riemannian Manifolds\n” has been published in\nApplied Set-Valued Analysis and Optimization, special issue on Recent Trends in Optimization Methods and Their Applications.\nThis is dedicated to Professor Henry Wolkowicz on the occasion of his 75th birthday.\nI am an Area Chair of ICML 2023 and NeurIPS 2023."
  },
