    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Pinyan Lu's Homepage\nPinyan Lu（陆品燕）\nProfessor, ITCS, SUFE\nMain\nChinese Articles\nContact\nI am a professor and the founding director of\nInstitute for Theoretical Computer\n                        Science\nat\nShanghai University of Finance and Economics\n(ITCS@SUFE). I am also the founding director of\nHuawei TCS Lab\n.\n[Short Bio]\n[My Curriculum Vitae]\nDr.Pinyan Lu is a professor and the founding director of Institute for Theoretical\n                                Computer Science at Shanghai University of Finance and Economics (ITCS@SUFE). Before\n                                joining SUFE, he spent seven years as a researcher at Microsoft Research. He studied in\n                                Tsinghua University (BS (2005) and PhD (2009) both in Computer Science). He is\n                                interested in theoretical computer science, including complexity theory, algorithms\n                                design and algorithmic game theory. Currently, his research is mainly focused on\n                                complexity and approximability of counting problems, and algorithmic mechanism design.\n                                Pinyan Lu is the recipient of a number of awards including ICCM Silver Medal of\n                                Mathematics, Distinguished Membership of ACM and CCF, CCF Young Scientist award, Best\n                                Paper Award from ICALP 2007, FAW 2010, ISAAC 2010 and so on. He is the PC chairs for\n                                FAW-AAIM 2012, WINE 2017, FAW 2018, ISAAC 2019 and so on, and PC members for STOC, FOCS,\n                                SODA and a dozen of international conferences. He is an Editorial Board Member of the\n                                “Information and Computation” and \"Theoretical Computer Science\".\n陆品燕，上海财经大学信息学院教授，理论计算机科学研究中心主任。2009年1月于清华大学计算机系获博士学位后加入微软亚洲研究院，历任理论组副研究员，研究员，主管研究员。2015年12月全职加盟上海财经大学，领衔组建理论计算机科学研究中心（ITCS），他的研究中心在CSRankings的“算法与复杂性”和“计算经济学”两个方向已经排名亚洲第一、全球前十。他的主要研究方向是理论计算机，并注重与其它学科的交叉，在理论计算机的三大会议STOC/FOCS/SODA共发表论文29篇。荣获ICALP2007、FAW2010、ISAAC2010\n                                等重要国际会议最佳论文奖。担任FAW-AAIM 2012、WINE 2017、FAW 2018、ISAAC\n                                2019等国际会议程序委员会联合主席，以及多次担任STOC，FOCS，SODA等顶级国际会议的程序委员会委员。曾荣获ACM杰出科学家奖（2019）、第八届世界华人数学家大会ICCM数学奖（原晨兴数学奖）银奖（2019）、中国计算机学会青年科学家（2014）、微软学者（2008）、清华大学特等奖学金（2007）等荣誉。\nN\nResearch Interests:\nI have a broad interest in theoretical computer science, with a\n                        focus on algorithmic mechanism design, complexity and approximability of counting problems. I’m\n                        also interested in the practical side of efficient algorithm design for constraint solving and\n                        optimization. In particular, I’m trying to combine tools from TCS, heuristic algorithm,\n                        operational research and machine learning to design efficient algorithms with real industry\n                        applications.\nStudents\nCurrent and Past PhD Students:\nChao Liao (Shanghai Jiao Tong University)\nTao Xiao (Researcher, Huawei TCS Lab)\nChihao Zhang\n(Assistant Professor,\n                                        Shanghai Jiao Tong University)\nVisiting students at ITCS@SUFE:\nYaonan Jin(Columbia University)\nXin Huang (Technion - Israel Institute of Technology)\nYingkai Li\n(Northwestern\n                                        University)\nBo Li\n(Assistant\n                                        Professor, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University)\nZhihao Tang\n(Associate Professor, Shanghai University of Finance and Economics)\nIntern students at Huawei TCS Lab:\nChengyang Xu (Zhejiang University)\nXuandi Ren (Peking University)\nEnze Sun (Hongkong University)\nKewen Wu\n(UC Berkeley)\nJiaheng Wang\n(University of Edinburgh)\nCe Jin\n(MIT)\nIntern Students at MSRA:\nLingxiao Huang (Researcher, Huawei TCS Lab)\nKuan Yang\n(Assistant Professor,\n                                        Shanghai Jiao Tong University)\nZhengyang Liu\n(Assistant Professor,\n                                        Beijing Institute of Technology)\nJingcheng Liu\n(Associate Professor,\n                                        Nanjing University)\nLiang Li (Professor, Shandong University)\nXue Chen\n(Professor,\n                                        University of Science and Technology of China)\nNick Gravin\n(Professor, Shanghai University of Finance and Economics)\nZeyuan Zhu\n(Researcher, Microsoft Research)\nYuan Zhou\n(Associate Professor,\n                                        Tsinghua University)\nGuangda Huzhang (Senior Expert Engineer,\n                                        Shopee)\nService\nEditorial Board\nInformation\n                                            and Computation\nTheoretical\n                                            Computer Science\nPC Chair\nISAAC 2019\nFAW 2018\nWINE 2017\nFAW-AAIM 2012\nPC Member\nSTOC 2024, SODA 2024, EC 2022, IJCAI 2022, WWW 2021, SODA 2021, SAGT 2021, ICALP 2020, SAGT 2019, EC 2018,\n                                AAMAS 2017, ICALP 2017, FAW 2016, IJCAI 2016, WINE 2016, ESA 2016, ICALP 2015, FOCS\n                                2015, ISAAC 2014, TAMC 2013, STOC 2013,CATS 2012, ICALP 2012, FAW-AAIM 2011, COCOON\n                                2011, WINE 2011,FAW 2010\nMy dblp site\nComplete Publications\nTwo-State Spin Systems with Negative Interactions. with Yumou Fei, Leslie Ann Goldberg, Pinyan Lu, ITCS 2024\npdf\nRevocable Deep Reinforcement Learning with Affinity Regularization for Outlier-Robust Graph Matching. with Chang Liu, Zetian Jiang, Runzhong Wang, Lingxiao Huang, Pinyan Lu, Junchi Yan, ICLR 2023.\npdf\nAuction Design for Value Maximizers with Budget and Return-on-Spend Constraints. with \tPinyan Lu, Chenyang Xu, Ruilong Zhang, WINE 2023.\npdf\nLearning Reserve Prices in Second-Price Auctions. with Yaonan Jin, Pinyan Lu, Tao Xiao, ITCS 2023.\npdf\nThe Price of Stability for First Price Auction. with Yaonan Jin, SODA 2023.\npdf\nBidder Subset Selection Problem in Auction Design. with Xiaohui Bei, Nick Gravin, Zhihao Gavin Tang, SODA 2023.\npdf\nAn FPTAS for the hardcore model on random regular bipartite graphs. with Chao Liao, Jiabao Lin, Zhenyu Mao, Theor. Comput. Sci.\npdf\nBetter Approximation for Interdependent SOS Valuations. with Enze Sun, Chenghan Zhou, WINE 2022.\npdf\nMechanism Design with Predictions. with Chenyang Xu, IJCAI 2022.\npdf\nFirst Price Auction is 1 - 1/e^2 Efficient. with Yaonan Jin,  FOCS 2022.\narxiv\nBayesian auctions with efficient queries. with Jing Chen, Bo Li, Yingkai Li, Artificial Intelligence.\npdf\nOblivious Online Contention Resolution Schemes. with Hu Fu, Zhihao Gavin Tang, Abner Turkieltaub, Hongxun Wu, Jinzhao Wu, Qianfan Zhang,  SOSA 2022.\npdf\nPACE Solver Description: Hust-Solver - A Heuristic Algorithm of Directed Feedback Vertex Set Problem. with Yuming Du, Qingyun Zhang, Junzhou Xu, Shungen Zhang, Chao Liao, Zhihuai Chen, Zhibo Sun, Zhouxing Su, Junwen Ding, Chen Wu, Zhi-Peng Lv, IPEC 2022.\npdf\nM-Mix: Generating Hard Negatives via Multi-sample Mixing for Contrastive Learning. with Shaofeng Zhang, Meng Liu, Junchi Yan, Hengrui Zhang, Lingxiao Huang, Xiaokang Yang, KDD 2022.\npdf\nZeros of Holant Problems: Locations and Algorithms. with Heng Guo, Chao Liao, Chihao Zhang, ACM Transactions on Algorithms.\npdf\nApproximating Permanent of Random Matrices with Vanishing Mean: Made Better and Simpler. with Zhengfeng Ji, Zhihan Jin, SODA 2021.\npdf\nAn Algorithmic Framework for Approximating Maximin Share Allocation of Chores. with Xin Huang,  EC 2021.\npdf\nTight Revenue Gaps among Multi-Unit Mechanisms. with Yaonan Jin, Shunhua Jiang, Hengjie Zhang,  EC 2021.\npdf\nConcentration bounds for almost k-wise independence. with applications to non-uniform security. with Nick Gravin, Siyao Guo, Tsz Chiu Kwok, SODA 2021.\npdf\nGeneralized Sorting with Predictions. with Xuandi Ren, Enze Sun, Yubo Zhang. SOSA 2021.\npdf\nRelaxing the Independence Assumption in Sequential Posted Pricing, Prophet Inequality, and Random Bipartite Matching. with Ioannis Caragiannis, Nick Gravin, Zihe Wang. WINE 2021.\npdf\nOnline Selection Problems against Constrained Adversary. with Zhihao Jiang, Zhihao Gavin Tang, Yuhao Zhang, ICML 2021.\npdf\nVariance-dependent best arm identification. with Chao Tao, Xiaojin Zhang. UAI 2021.\npdf\nApproximability of the eight-vertex model. with Jin-Yi Cai Tianyu Liu,and Jing Yu, CCC 2020.\npdf\nZeros of ferromagnetic 2-spin systems. with Heng Guo, Jingcheng Liu, SODA 2020.\npdf\nTight Revenue Gaps Among Simple Mechanisms. with Yaonan Jin, Zhihao Gavin Tang, Tao Xiao,SIAM Journal on Computing.\npdf\nOptimal Budget-Feasible Mechanisms for Additive Valuations, with Nick Gravin, Yaonan Jin, Chenhao Zhang, ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation.\npdf\nStrategyproof Mechanism for Two Heterogeneous Facilities with Constant Approximation Ratio. with Minming Li, Yuhao Yao, and Jialin Zhang, IJCAI 2020.\npdf\nDichotomy for Holant∗ Problems on the Boolean Domain, with Jin-Yi Cai, Mingji Xia, Theory of Computing Systems\npdf\nCounting Hypergraph Colorings in the Local Lemma Regime. with Heng Guo, Chao Liao, Chihao Zhang, SIAM Journal on Computing.\npdf\nCounting Independent Sets and Colorings on Random Regular Bipartite Graphs, with Chao Liao, Jiabao Lin, Zhenyu Mao, APPROX-RANDOM 2019.\npdf\nApproximability of the Six-vertex Model. with Jin-Yi Cai and Tianyu Liu, SODA 2019.\npdf\nZeros of Holant problems: locations and algorithms. with Heng Guo, Chao Liao and Chihao Zhang, SODA 2019.\npdf\nTight Approximation Ratio of Anonymous Pricing. with Yaonan Jin, Qi Qi, Zhihao Gavin Tang and Tao Xiao, STOC 2019.\npdf\nRevenue Maximization with Imprecise Distribution. with Yingkai Li, and Haoran Ye, AAMAS 2019.\npdf\nCorrelation-Robust Analysis of Single Item Auction. with Xiaohui Bei, Nick Gravin and Zhihao Gavin Tang, SODA 2019.\npdf\nTight Revenue Gaps among Simple Mechanisms. with Yaonan Jin, Zhihao Gavin Tang and Tao Xiao, SODA 2019.\npdf\nLearning Plackett-Luce Mixtures from Partial Preferences. with Ao Liu, Zhibing Zhao, Chao Liao, Lirong Xia, AAAI 2019.\npdf\nUniqueness, Spatial Mixing, and ...[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Peizhong Ju": {
    "homepage": "https://functionadvanced.github.io/PeizhongJu/",
    "emails": [
      "me@peizhong.ju",
      "peizhong.ju@uky.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Peizhong's Homepage\nAbout Me\nHello, I'm\nPeizhong Ju\n, an Assistant Professor in the Department of Computer Science of\n              University of Kentucky.\n              I earned my Ph.D. degree from Purdue University in 2021 and my B.S. degree from Peking University in 2016.\nMy research area includes machine learning, smart grid, optimization, and wireless communication. My\n              recent research focuses on theories for explaining the performance of machine learning models. Before\n              that, I worked on power systems and wireless communication. Generally speaking, the goal of my research is\n              to use rigorous mathematical analysis (including probability theory, optimization, game theory, and random\n              matrix theory) to understand the fundamental limits of a complex system under uncertainty and/or\n              disturbance.\nYou can contact me at\npeizhong.ju@uky.edu\nCopy\nMy office location and phone number are available on my\nfaculty webpage\nat University of Kentucky.\nMy CV (as a pdf file) is\nhere\n(Last updated: Mar 20, 2026).\nCheck out my\nGoogle Scholar\n                profile\nfor a list of my publications.\nNews\nOn Mar 16, 2026, two of our papers were accepted by WiOpt'26. One is on multi-armed bandits (MABs), and\n            the other is on encoding for classification in 5G networks. 🎉🎉\nOn Mar 11, 2026, our work on interpreting the internal mechanisms of large language model (LLM) was\n            accepted to TMLR. 🎉🎉\nOn Jan 4, 2026, one of our papers on interpreting large language models (LLMs) was accepted to the\n            Findings of\n            EACL'26. 🎉🎉\nOn July 25, 2025, one of our papers on classification on edge devices was accepted by ACM MobiHoc'25. 🎉🎉\nOn May 8-9, 2025, I participated in AIMACCS'25 and served as a panelist for the session \"Thriving in\n            Today's Job Market: Finding, Excelling, and Evolving in Your Career\" at the Ohio State University, Columbus,\n            OH.\nOn May 1, 2025, two of our papers were accepted by ICML'25: one on federated split learning and another on\n            continual learning. 🎉🎉\nOn Feb 28, 2025, I presented our PSMGD work and chaired an oral session at AAAI'25 at Pennsylvania\n            Convention Center, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.\nOn Feb 11, 2025, our paper \"How to Find the Exact Pareto Front for Multi-Objective MDPs?\" is selected\n            to be presented as a Spotlight (5.1% of the submitted papers) by ICLR'25.\nOn Jan 22, 2025, three of our papers were accepted by ICLR'25: two focused on diffusion models and one\n            exploring multi-objective MDPs.\nOn Jan 15-16, 2025, I attended the\nNeTS Early Career Workshop 2025\nat NSF in Alexandria, VA\nOn Dec 9, 2024, our research paper on Fast Multi-Objective Optimization was accepted to AAAI'25.\nI joined the Department of Computer Science of University of Kentucky in August, 2024.\nPhoto\nOn Jul 28, 2024, two of our papers were accepted by MobiHoc'24. One is about Federated Learning, and\n            another is on network-edge classification.\nOn Jan 16, 2024, two of my papers on Reinforcement Learning were accepted by ICLR'24.\nOn Jul 27, 2023, I and Sen Lin presented our continual learning work at ICML'23 at the Hawaii Convention\n            Center, Honolulu.\nOn Jul 20, 2023,\nThe Ohio State University News\nreported our continual learning research in a\n            press release:\n\"Future\n              AI algorithms have potential to learn like humans, say researchers.\"\nExperience\nAssistant Professor\n2024 - Present\nDepartment of Computer Science at\nUniversity of Kentucky\n, Lexington, KY,\n                    USA\nPostdoctoral Scholar\n2021 - 2024\nECE department at\nThe Ohio State University\n, Columbus, OH, USA\nNSF AI Institute on Future Edge Networks and Distributed Intelligence\n                    (\nAI-EDGE\n)\nPh.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering\n2016 - 2021\nPurdue University\n, West Lafayette, IN, USA\nB.Sc. in Electrical Engineering\n2012 - 2016\nPeking University\n, Beijing, China\nHonors & Awards\nBest Paper Award, 13\nth\nACM International Conference on Future Energy Systems (ACM e-Energy\n              2022)\nThe Bilsland Dissertation Fellowship, Purdue University, 2021\nBest Paper Award Finalist of 9\nth\nACM International Conference on Future Energy Systems (ACM\n              e-Energy 2018)\nOutstanding Graduate Award of EE department of Peking University, 2016 (the only one that year)\nOutstanding Graduate Award of Peking University, 2016\nIEEE Student Grant, 2015\nResearch Excellence Award, Peking University, 2015\nChina National Scholarship, 2015\nSamsung Scholarship, 2014\nAward of excellence, OpenHW2014 open source hardware and embedded computing contest (awarded by ARM and\n              XILINX), 2014\nEMC Scholarship, 2013\nResearch\nPostdoctoral Scholar, the Ohio State University\nSupervisor: Prof.\nNess B.\n              Shroff\n.\nMulti-agent Reinforcement Learning, Multi-task supervised learning, Generalization Performance Analysis.\n              (Oct. 2021 - Aug. 2024)\nResearch Assistant, Purdue University\nAdvisor: Prof.\nXiaojun Lin\n.\nOverfitting in Machine Learning (Oct. 2019 - Sept. 2021)\nMarket Design for Renewable in Power Distribution Level (Aug. 2018 - Oct. 2019)\nVoltage Control in Power Grid with Renewable (Aug. 2016 - Aug. 2018)\nUndergraduate Research Assistant, Peking University, China\nAdvisor: Prof.\nXiang\n              Cheng\n.\nAntenna Grouping for Massive MIMO (Sep. 2015 - June 2016)\nSelf-Interference Cancellation for Full-Duplex (Oct. 2013 - Sep. 2015)\nPublications\nPeer-Reviewed Conference Papers\nChengzhang Li,\nPeizhong Ju\n, Atilla Eryilmaz, and Ness B. Shroff,\n              \"Priority-Aware Encoding for Bandwidth-Efficient Real-Time Classification in 5G Networks,\"\n24\nth\nInternational Symposium on Modeling and Optimization in Mobile, Ad hoc, and Wireless\n                Networks (WiOpt'26), 2026\nBibTeX\n@inproceedings{li2026priority,\n  title={Priority-Aware Encoding for Bandwidth-Efficient Real-Time Classification in 5G Networks},\n  author={Li, Chengzhang and Ju, Peizhong and Eryilmaz, Atilla and Shroff, Ness B.},\n  booktitle={International Symposium on Modeling and Optimization in Mobile, Ad hoc, and Wireless Networks (WiOpt)},\n  year={2026}\n}\nAshutosh Soni,\nPeizhong Ju\n, Atilla Eryilmaz, and Ness B. Shroff,\n              \"An LP-based Sampling Policy for Multi-Armed Bandits with Side-Observations and Stochastic Availability,\"\n24\nth\nInternational Symposium on Modeling and Optimization in Mobile, Ad hoc, and Wireless\n                Networks (WiOpt'26), 2026\nBibTeX\n@inproceedings{soni2026lp,\n  title={An LP-based Sampling Policy for Multi-Armed Bandits with Side-Observations and Stochastic Availability},\n  author={Soni, Ashutosh and Ju, Peizhong and Eryilmaz, Atilla and Shroff, Ness B.},\n  booktitle={International Symposium on Modeling and Optimization in Mobile, Ad hoc, and Wireless Networks (WiOpt)},\n  year={2026}\n}\nMoghis Fereidouni, Muhammad Umair Haider,\nPeizhong Ju\n, and A.B. Siddique, \"Evaluating\n              Sparse Autoencoders for Monosemantic Representation,\"\n              Findings of\n19\nth\nConference of the European\n                Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (EACL'26), 2026\narXiv version\nBibTeX\n@article{fereidouni2025evaluating,\n  title={Evaluating Sparse Autoencoders for Monosemantic Representation},\n  author={Fereidouni, Moghis and Haider, Muhammad Umair and Ju, Peizhong and Siddique, AB},\n  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.15094},\n  year={2025}\n}\nChengzhang Li,\nPeizhong Ju\n, Atilla Eryilmaz, and Ness B. Shroff,\n              \"Two Levels Are All You Need: Simplifying Data Compression for Timely Edge Classification,\"\n26\nth\nInternational Symposium on Theory, Algorithmic Foundations, and Protocol\n                Design for Mobile Networks and Mobile Computing (MobiHoc'25), 2025\n.\npaper\n                (open access)\nBibTeX\n@inproceedings{li2025two,\n  title={Two Levels Are All You Need: Simplifying Data Compression for Timely Edge Classification},\n  author={Li, Chengzhang and Ju, Peizhong and Eryilmaz, Atilla and Shroff, Ness},\n  booktitle={Proceedings of the Twenty-sixth International Symposium on Theory, Algorithmic Foundations, and Protocol Design for Mobile Networks and Mobile Computing},\n  pages={301--310},\n  year={2025}\n}\nSrijith Nair, Michael Lin,\nPeizhong Ju\n, Amirreza Talebi, Elizabeth Serena Bentley, and\n              Jia Liu,\n              \"FSL-SAGE: Accelerating Federated Split Learning via Smashed Activation Gradient Estimation,\"\n42\nnd\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (ICML'25)\n.\narXiv version\nBibTeX\n@inproceedings{nair2025fsl,\n  title={FSL-SAGE: Accelerating Federated Split Learning via Smashed Activation Gradient Estimation},\n  author={Nair, Srijith and Lin, Michael and Ju, Peizhong and Talebi, Amirreza and Bentley, Elizabeth and Liu, Jia},\n  booktitle={International Conference on Machine Learning},\n  year={2025},\n  organization={PMLR}\n}\nJunze Deng, Qinhang Wu,\nPeizhong Ju\n, Sen Lin, Yingbin Liang, and Ness Shroff,\n              \"Unlocking the Power of Rehearsal in Continual Learning: A Theoretical Perspective,\"\n42\nnd\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (ICML'25)\n.\narXiv version\nBibTeX\n@inproceedings{deng2025unlocking,\n  title={Unlocking the Power of Rehearsal in Continual Learning: A Theoretical Perspective},\n  author={Deng, Junze and Wu, Qinhang and Ju, Peizhong and Lin, Sen and Liang, Yingbin and Shroff, Ness},\n  booktitle={International Conference on Machine Learning},\n  year={2025},\n  organization={PMLR}\n}\nYining Li,\nPeizhong Ju\n, and Ness B. Shroff, \"How to Find the Exact Pareto Front for\n              Multi-Objective MDPs?\"\n13\nth\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR'25)\n.\nspotlight\narX