    "homepage_text": "Yao-Ting (Tony) Lin\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nYao-Ting (Tony) Lin\nPhD student at UCSB,\nadvised by\nPrabhanjan Ananth\n.\nFormer\nresearch assistant at\nAcademia Sinica\nwith\nKai-Min Chung\n.\nBS\nand\nMS\nfrom\nNational Taiwan University.\nEmail:\nyao-ting_lin[at]ucsb[dot]edu\nGoogle Scholar\nDBLP\nPublications\nOn the Limitations of Pseudorandom Unitaries\nwith Prabhanjan Ananth and Aditya Gulati\n.\nTCC 202\n5.\nPseudorandom Unitaries in the Haar Random Oracle Model\nwith Prabhanjan Ananth, John Bostanci, and Aditya Gulati\n.\nCRYPTO\n20\n25.\nFull version:\nPart I\nPart II\n.\nPart II accepted to\nQIP 2026\nunder the title\n\"\nGluing Random Unitaries with Inverses and Applications to Strong Pseudorandom Unitaries\n\"\n.\nPseudorandomness in the (Inverseless) Haar Random Oracle Model\nwith Prabhanjan Ananth, John Bostanci, and Aditya Gulati\n.\nEUROCRYPT\n2025.\nCryptography in the Common Haar State Model: Feasibility Results and Separations\nwith Prabhanjan Ananth\nand Aditya Gulati\n.\nTCC 2024, QCRYPT 2024\n,\nQIP 2025.\n(merged talks with\narXiv:2404.03295\n)\nPseudorandom Isometries\nwith Prabhanjan Ananth, Aditya Gulati, and Fatih Kaleoglu\n.\nEUROCRYPT\n2024.\nPseudorandom Strings from Pseudorandom Quantum States\nwith Prabhanjan Ananth\nand Henry Yuen\n.\nITCS 2024.\nOn the (Im)possibility of Time-Lock Puzzles in the Quantum Random Oracle Model\nwith Abtin Afshar, Kai-Min Chung, Yao-Ching Hsieh, and Mohammad Mahmoody\n.\nASIACRYPT 2023.\nOn the Impossibility of General Parallel Fast-forwarding of Hamiltonian Simulation\nwith\nNai-Hui Chia, Kai-Min Chung, Yao-Ching Hsieh, Han-Hsuan Lin, and Yu-Ching Shen.\nCCC 2023.\nBlack-Box Separations for Non-Interactive Commitments in a Quantum World\nwith Kai-Min Chung and Mohammad Mahmoody\n.\nEUROCRYPT 2023.\nOn the Impossibility of Key Agreements from Quantum Random Oracles\nwith Per Austrin, Hao Chung, Kai-Min Chung, Shiuan Fu, and Mohammad Mahmoody.\nCRYPTO 2022.\nManuscripts\nBlack-Box Separation Between Pseudorandom Unitaries, Pseudorandom Isometries, and Pseudorandom Function-Like States\nwith Aditya Gulati, Tomoyuki Morimae, and Shogo Yamada\nA Note on Quantum Phase Estimation\nPoster @\nTQC 2023.\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "serper::Andrea J. Liu": {
    "homepage": "https://live-sas-physics.pantheon.sas.upenn.edu/people/standing-faculty/andrea-liu",
    "emails": [
      "ajliu@physics.upenn.edu",
      "physics-info@physics.upenn.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Andrea Liu | Department of Physics and Astronomy\nSkip to main content\nUniversity of Pennsylvania School of Arts & Sciences\nCollege of Arts & Sciences\nGraduate Division\nCollege of Liberal and Professional Studies\nUniversity of Pennsylvania School of Arts and Sciences Department of Physics and Astronomy\nDepartment of Physics and Astronomy\nToggle navigation\nSearch\nAndrea Liu\nStanding Faculty\nHepburn Professor of Physics\nFounder & Director of The University of Pennsylvania Center for Soft and Living Matter\nshe/her\nResearch Areas:\nTheoretical Soft and Living Matter\najliu@physics.upenn.edu\n(215) 573-7374\nDRL 2N1A\nWebsite\nArrival as faculty at Penn (2004)\nFaculty at UCLA in Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry (1994-2004)\nPostdoctoral Research Associate, University of California, Santa Barbara (1991-94)\nPostdoctoral Fellow, Exxon Research and Engineering Company (1989-91)\nHonors include:\nAmerican Physical Society Leo P. Kadanoff Prize (2025)\nDirector Center for Soft and Living Matter (2021-)\nMember of National Academy of Sciences (2017)\nSimons Investigator in Theoretical Physics (2015-2025)\nFellow of American Association for the Advancement of Science (2012)\nHepburn Professor of Physics (2011-)\nMember of American Academy of Arts and Sciences (2010)\nEdmund J. and Louise W. Kahn Term Professor in the Natural Sciences (2009-2011)\nFellow of American Physical Society (2004)\nEducation\nPh.D., Cornell University (1989)\nB.A., University of California at Berkeley (1984)\nResearch Interests\nIn my research group, we use a combination of analytical theory and computation to study soft and living matter.  In living matter, we are interested in how new and general collective phenomena, often beyond those typically observed in inanimate soft matter, can emerge at the subcellular, cellular and tissue levels.  In soft matter, we are interested in the glass problem, in the learning problem, and in using data science methods to understand the microscopic origins of collective many-body behavior in strongly correlated systems. My collaborators and I worked for several years on the problem of jamming, where we showed that jamming produces solids at an opposite pole from perfect crystals, providing a new way of thinking about the nature of rigidity in disordered solids. The nonequilibrium jamming transition and jammed state thus serve as useful starting points for understanding a broad class of materials.\nSelected Publications\nhttps://scholar.google.com/citations?user=x0NU7KEAAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao\nCV (file)\nLiuCV2023.pdf\n398.78 KB\n© 2026 The Trustees of the University of Pennsylvania\nPenn Weblogin"
  },
