    "homepage_text": "David Wu\nDavid Wu\nAbout\nCV\nAcademic\nResearch\nPublications\nTalks\nStudents\nTeaching\nService\nAwards\nNotes\nOther\nPhotos\nDavid Wu\nDavid Wu\nDepartment of Computer Science\nUniversity of Texas at Austin\nEmail:\ndwu4@cs.utexas.edu\nResearch Interests:\nCryptography, Computer Security\nGoogle Scholar\nDBLP\nAbout Me\nI am an assistant professor in the\nComputer Science Department\nat\nthe\nUniversity of Texas at Austin\nand a member of the\nCryptography Group\n.\nPreviously, I was an assistant professor in the\nComputer\nScience Department\nat the\nUniversity of Virginia\n(UVA)\n. I received my PhD in computer science at\nStanford University\nin 2018, where I was advised by\nDan Boneh\n. I received my MS and BS in\ncomputer science from\nStanford University\nin 2013.\nMy research interests are in cryptography and computer security. \nMy work has been generously supported by a\nSloan Research Fellowship\n(2025),\nan\nAmazon Research Award\n(2025),\na\nGoogle Research Scholar Award\n(2022),\na\nMicrosoft Research Faculty Fellowship\n(2021),\nand an\nNSF CAREER Award\n(2021).\nPlease see my\npublications\n,\ntalks\n, and\nprojects\npages for a more comprehensive overview of my work.\nI am a co-organizer of the\nCryptography and Security Seminar\nand the\nTexas Crypto Day\n.\nProspective students:\nIf you are interested in working with me, please see\nthis page\nfor details. If you\nare a current UT student interested in working with me, please email me.\nContact Information\nDepartment of Computer Science, GDC 6.808\nThe University of Texas at Austin\n2317 Speedway, D9500\nAustin, TX 78712\nEmail:\ndwu4@cs.utexas.edu"
  },
  "serper::Tianyu Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://tianyuzhang.org/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/ChuanyuXue",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Tianyu Zhang\nTianyu Zhang\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Computer Science\nThe University of Iowa\nOffice: 201F MacLean Hall (MLH), lowa City, lA 52242\nEmail:\n[email protected]\nI am an Assistant Professor with the\nDepartment of Computer Science\nat The University of Iowa. Before that,\nI was a postdoctoral fellow working with\nSong Han\nat the\nSchool of Computing\nat the University of Connecticut. In 2022, I worked with\nNan Guan\nas a postdoc at The Hong Kong Polytechnic University. I obtained my Ph.D. from Northeastern University, China, and was supervised by\nX. Sharon Hu\nduring my visit to Notre Dame.\nMy research interest centers around\nreal-time cyber-physical systems\nsubject to stringent timing and reliability requirements. Examples of applications include\nindustrial internet-of-things (IIoT)\n,\nautonomous vehicles\n,\navionics\n, etc. My current research focuses mainly on the resource management and scheduling framework design/analysis/implementation for emerging IIoT systems, including\nindustrial 5G\n, time-sensitive networking (\nTSN\n), and wireless sensor-actuator networks (WSANs). My general research goal is to make real-time cyber-physical systems\ndeterministic\n,\nreliable,\nand\nsecure\n.\nWe are actively looking for self-motivated master/PhD students (\nFall 2026\n) and interns to work on real-time cyber-physical systems. If you are interested, see more details\nhere\n.\nNews\n[Talk]\nInvited talk on \"\nTowards Deterministic Networking for Real-Time Mission-Critical Systems\"\nat the\nWiCI Webinar\nat Iowa State University\n.\n[Service]\nServe as the TPC member for\nEMSOFT\n2026,\nICDCS\n2026,\nRTAS'BP\n2026, and\nRTCSA\n, 2026\n.\n[Paper]\nOur paper \"KeepON: Supporting Deterministic Traffic on Standard NICs\" is accepted in the\n23rd USENIX Symposium on Networked Systems Design and Implementation\n(\nNSDI\n), 2026\n.\n[Paper]\nThe TSNKit paper is accepted in the 46th IEEE Real-Time Systems Symposium (Brief Presentation) (\nRTSS\nBP\n), 2025\n. Check out our open-source TSN scheduling evaluation tool,\nTSNKit\n!\n[Paper]\nOur paper \"5G-TPS: A Two-Phase Real-Time Scheduling and Adaptation Framework for 5G Radio Access Networks\" is accepted in the IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing (\nTMC\n), 2025\n.\n[Paper]\nOur paper \"Flexibility-Aware Network Resource Partitioning for Multi-State Real-Time Mission-Critical Applications\" is accepted in the 46th IEEE Real-Time Systems Symposium (\nRTSS\n), 2025\n.\n[Paper]\nOur paper \"A Survey and Experimental Study of Real-Time Scheduling Methods for 802.1Qbv TSN Networks\" is accepted in ACM Computing Surveys (\nCSUR\n), 2025\n.\n[Service]\nServe as the TPC member for\nRTAS\n, 2025\n.\n[Paper]\nA paper \"Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN) for Industrial Automation: Current Advances and Future Direction\" is accepted in ACM Computing Surveys (CSUR), 2024\n. Check\nACM Showcase\n.\n[Service]\nServe as the TPC member for\nRTCSA\n, 2024\n.\n[Paper]\nA paper \"\nReal-Time Scheduling for 802.1Qbv Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN): A Systematic Review and Experimental Study\n\" is accepted\nin the 29th IEEE Real-Time and Embedded Technology and Applications Symposium\n(\nRTAS\n)\n, part of the\nCPS-IoT Week\n, 2024.\n[Paper]\nA paper \"\nTowards Cost-Effective Real-Time High-Throughput End Station Design for Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN)\n\" is accepted\nin the 61st ACM/IEEE Design Automation Conference\n(\nDAC\n), 2024.\n[Service]\nServe as the TPC member for\nRTAS\n, 2024\n.\n[Paper]\nA paper \"\nReal-Time Flow Scheduling in Industrial 5G New Radio\n\" is accepted\nin the 44th IEEE Real-Time System Symposium\n(\nRTSS\n), 2023,\n[Paper]\nA paper \"\nMinimizing AoI of Non-uniform Multi-source Real-time Data Updates: Model Generalization, Analysis and Performance Evaluation\n\" is accepted\nin the 44th IEEE Real-Time System Symposium\n(\nRTSS\n), 2023,\n[Paper]\nA paper \"\nContention-Free Configured Grant Scheduling for 5G URLLC Traffic\n\" is accepted\nin the 60th ACM/IEEE Design Automation Conference\nDAC'23\n.\n[Service]\nServe as the TPC member for\nRTCSA 2023\n.\n[Service]\nServe as the Registration Chair for\nCPS-IoT Week\n, 2023\n.\n[Paper]\nA paper \"\nReliable Dynamic Packet Scheduling with Slot Sharing for Real-Time Wireless Networks\n\" is accepted in IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing (TMC)."
  },
