  "serper::Changwoo J Lee": {
    "homepage": "https://changwoo-lee.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "changwoo.lee@duke.edu",
      "email@changwoo.lee"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/changwoo-lee",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/changwoo-j-lee",
    "homepage_text": "Welcome! - Changwoo Lee\nChangwoo Lee\nPostdoctoral Associate, Duke University\nFollow\nDurham, NC\nEmail\nLinkedIn\nGithub\nGoogle Scholar\nORCID\nWelcome!\nI am a postdoctoral associate in the\nDepartment of Statistical Science\nat Duke University, working with Prof.\nDavid Dunson\n. I received my Ph.D. in Statistics from the\nDepartment of Statistics\n, Texas A&M University, where I was fortunate to be advised by Prof.\nHuiyan Sang\n, and to collaborate closely with Dr.\nEun Sug Park\n.\nMy research focuses on developing statistical and machine learning methods that are robust across diverse data conditions, scalable for analysing large, high-dimensional data with complex dependence structures, and informative for scientific decision-making, with applications to environmental epidemiology and ecology.\nRecent research highlight\nWe propose a\nnew family of generalized linear models\nfor continuous proportional data, named\ncontinuous binomial (cobin) regression\nand its extension, which significantly improves robustness and scalability over traditional beta regression. The implementation is available in the\nR package cobin\n; check out\ninstall.packages(\"cobin\")\nin R!\n(Jan 2026 update) This manuscript is accepted for publication at the Journal of American Statistical Association (Theory and Methods).\nI can best be reached through email at\nchangwoo.lee (at) duke.edu\n."
  },
