    "homepage_text": "Yiling Zhang\nSkip to main content\nGo to the University of Minnesota Twin Cities home page\nOne Stop\nMyU\n: For Students, Faculty, and Staff\nSearch\nCollege of Science and Engineering\nIndustrial and Systems Engineering\nGive to ISyE\nYiling Zhang\nAssistant Professor\nContact\n[email protected]\n(612) 626-0566\nYiling Zhang\nAssistant Professor\nBiography\nResearch\nPublications & Awards\nBiography\nYiling Zhang is an Assistant Professor in the Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering at the University of Minnesota. She received her Ph.D. in Industrial and Operations Engineering at the University of Michigan, Ann Arbor. Her research interests are in stochastic programming, integer programming, nonlinear programming. Applications include risk analysis of energy systems, healthcare operations, and transportation systems. Her research has been published in journals such as Operations Research, SIAM Journal on Optimization, and Manufacturing & Service Operations Management. Her work has been recognized by several awards, including Honorable Mention for INFORMS Optimization Society Student Paper Prize, the Richard & Eleanor Towner Prize for Distinguished Academic Achievement, and the Murty Prize for Best Student Paper in Optimization.\nEducation\nPh.D., Industrial and Operations Engineering, 2019\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor, USA\nM.S., Industrial and Operations Engineering, 2015\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor, USA\nB.Eng., Automation, 2013\nTsinghua University, Beijing, China\nScientific & Professional Societies\nInstitute for Operations Research and Management Science (INFORMS)\nINFORMS Computing Society (ICS)\nMathematical Optimization Society (MOS)\nResearch\nAccepting new\ngraduate\nresearch students\nMethodologies: Stochastic Programming, Nonlinear Optimization, Integer Programming\nApplications: Power Systems and Building Energy, Healthcare Systems, Shared Mobility\nPublications & Awards\nHonors and Awards\nInstitute on the Environment Mini Grants, University of Minnesota, 2019\nHonorable Mention for INFORMS Optimization Society Student Paper Prize, 2019\nRichard F. and Eleanor A. Towner Prize for Distinguished Academic Achievement, University of Michigan, 2019\nSelected Publications\nZhang, Y., Dong, Jin. (2022). Building Load Control using Distributionally Robust Chance-Constrained Programs with Right-Hand Side Uncertainty and the Risk-Adjustable Variants. To appear in INFORMS Journal on Computing.\nZhang, Y., Lu, M., Shen, S. (2019). On the values of vehicle-to-grid selling in electric vehicle sharing. Manufacturing and Service Operations Management, 23(2), 267-545.\nZhang, Y., Jiang, R., & Shen, S. (2018). Ambiguous chance-constrained binary programs under mean-covariance information. SIAM Journal on Optimization, 28(4), 2922-2944.\nZhang, Y., Shen, S., Erdogan, S. A. (2018). Solving 0-1 semidefinite programs for the distributionally robust allocation of surgery blocks. Optimization Letters, 12(7), 1503–1521.\nJiang, R., Shen, S., &amp; Zhang, Y. (2017). Integer programming approaches for appointment scheduling with random no-shows and service durations. Operations Research, 65(6), 1638-1656.\nZhang, Y., Shen, S., & Erdogan, S. A. (2017). Distributionally robust appointment scheduling withmoment-based ambiguity set. Operations Research Letters, 45(2), 139-144.\nZhang, Y., Shen, S., & Mathieu, J. L. (2017). Distributionally robust chance-constrained optimalpower flow with uncertain renewables and uncertain reserves provided by loads. IEEE Transactions on Power Systems, 32(2), 1378-1388.\nFuture students\nFuture undergraduate students\nFuture transfer students\nFuture graduate students\nFuture international students\nScholarships and financial aid\nBeyond the classroom\nDiversity and Inclusion Opportunities\nLearn abroad\nLiving Learning Communities\nMentor programs\nPrograms for women\nResearch\nStudent groups\nCurrent students\nInformation for current students\nDepartments and 