    "homepage_text": "Christopher Chang – Princeton University Department of Chemistry\nSeminars & Events\n-\nCourses\n-\nDirections\n-\nContacts\n-\nIT Support\n-\nIntranet\n-\nSearch for:\nFaculty & Research\nFaculty\nResearch Areas\nCatalysis / Synthesis\nChemical Biology\nInorganic Chemistry\nMaterials\nSpectroscopy / Physical Chemistry\nTheoretical Chemistry\nCollaborations\nAndlinger Center for Energy and the Environment\nPrinceton Institute for Computational Science and Engineering\nPrinceton Materials Institute\nPrinceton Catalysis Initiative\nResearch Facilities\nResearch Facilities Overview\nBiophysics Core Facility\nCrystallography\nMerck Catalysis Center\nNMR Facility\nMass Spectrometry\nOther Spectroscopy\nUltrafast Laser Spectroscopy\nIndustrial Associates Program\nLibraries & Computing\nFrick Chemistry Laboratory\nHood Awareness\nDepartment\nAdministration & Staff\nBusiness & Grants Office\nStockroom\nFrick Event Guidelines\nIT Support\nFaculty & Academic Jobs\nSeminars & Events\nSpecial Symposia\nChemistry Demonstration Lab\nPostdocs\nPostdocs Overview\nNew Postdocs\nResources\nFamily-Friendly Initiatives\nGrad Program\nGraduate Program Overview\nAdmissions\nAcademic Program\nCampus Life\nLiving in Princeton\nGraduate News\nUndergrads\nUndergraduate Overview\nNew Majors\nSummer Undergrad Research Fellows in Chemistry\nOther Summer Research Opportunities\nOutside Course Approval\nPU Chemical Society\nNews\nCommunity Outreach\nOur Commitment\nResources and Reporting\nOutreach\nVisiting Faculty Research Partnership\nJoin the Department\nSearch for:\nFaculty & Research\nFaculty\nResearch Areas\nCatalysis / Synthesis\nChemical Biology\nInorganic Chemistry\nMaterials\nSpectroscopy / Physical Chemistry\nTheoretical Chemistry\nCollaborations\nAndlinger Center for Energy and the Environment\nPrinceton Institute for Computational Science and Engineering\nPrinceton Materials Institute\nPrinceton Catalysis Initiative\nResearch Facilities\nResearch Facilities Overview\nBiophysics Core Facility\nCrystallography\nMerck Catalysis Center\nNMR Facility\nMass Spectrometry\nOther Spectroscopy\nUltrafast Laser Spectroscopy\nIndustrial Associates Program\nLibraries & Computing\nFrick Chemistry Laboratory\nHood Awareness\nDepartment\nAdministration & Staff\nBusiness & Grants Office\nStockroom\nFrick Event Guidelines\nIT Support\nFaculty & Academic Jobs\nSeminars & Events\nSpecial Symposia\nChemistry Demonstration Lab\nPostdocs\nPostdocs Overview\nNew Postdocs\nResources\nFamily-Friendly Initiatives\nGrad Program\nGraduate Program Overview\nAdmissions\nAcademic Program\nCampus Life\nLiving in Princeton\nGraduate News\nUndergrads\nUndergraduate Overview\nNew Majors\nSummer Undergrad Research Fellows in Chemistry\nOther Summer Research Opportunities\nOutside Course Approval\nPU Chemical Society\nNews\nCommunity Outreach\nOur Commitment\nResources and Reporting\nOutreach\nVisiting Faculty Research Partnership\nJoin the Department\nSeminars & Events\nCourses\nDirections\nContacts\nIT Support\nIntranet\nSearch for:\nHome\n/\nFaculty & Research\n/\nChristopher Chang\nChristopher Chang\nResearch Focus\nHonors\nSelected Publications\nRelated News\nResearch Area:\nChemical Biology\nCatalysis / Synthesis\nInorganic Chemistry\nContact:\nChristopher J. Chang\nEdward and Virginia Taylor Professor of Bioorganic Chemistry\n[email protected]\nFrick Laboratory, 187\n609-258-8819\nFaculty Assistant:\nLisa Piscatelli\nFaculty / Grants Assistant\n[email protected]\nFrick Laboratory, 228\n609-258-6843\nChris Chang Group Website\nResearch Focus\nResearch Focus\nOur laboratory studies the chemistry and biology of the elements. We advance new concepts in imaging, proteomics, drug discovery, and catalysis by drawing from core disciplines of inorganic, organic, and biological chemistry. For example, we have developed activity-based sensing as a general platform to identify transition metals, reactive oxygen species, and one-carbon units as new classes of single-atom signals for allosteric regulation of protein function. These chemical tools also reveal unique metal and redox disease vulnerabilities as targets for innovative drug discovery efforts to treat neurodegeneration, cancer, and metabolic disorders. Representative project areas are summarized below.\nTransition Metal Signaling: Bioinorganic Chemistry of the Brain and Body.\nWe are advancing a new paradigm of transition metal signaling, where nutrients like copper and iron serve as dynamic signals to regulate protein function by metalloallostery beyond their traditional roles as active site cofactors. We develop activity-based sensing probes for imaging mobile transition metal pools and activity-based proteomics probes for identifying allosteric metal sites in proteins. These chemical tools enable us to decipher the complex biology of sleep, cognition, and obesity in cell, zebrafish, mouse, and non-human primate models. We also develop medicines to target cuproplasia and cuproptosis, newly recognized forms of copper-dependent cell proliferation and cell death, respectively, as metal-dependent disease vulnerabilities in cancer, neurodegeneration, and metabolic liver disorders.\nActivity-Based Sensing: Illuminating One- and Two-Carbon Signaling.\nWe have pioneered the field of activity-based sensing by developing chemical sensors for biological analytes that achieve high selectivity using reaction chemistry rather than conventional lock-and-key binding approaches. Building upon our previous work on selective reactive oxygen and sulfur species detection, we are imaging one- and two-carbon metabolites in organelles, cells, tissues, and animals to elucidate principles of how these signals influence genetic and epigenetic control of cancer metabolism, neurodegeneration, and immune response.\nActivity-Based Proteomics: Decoding and Drugging Single-Atom Signaling.\nWe are establishing a field of single-atom signaling, focusing on understanding how reversible addition or removal of a single oxygen atom on proteins can influence their function. We develop activity-based proteomics probes to identify new targets of methionine oxidation as well as writers and erasers that regulate their single-atom biology. Our broader work on protein bioconjugation methods reveal new ligandable hotspots for undruggable protein targets to accelerate the development of next-generation precision medicines that target redox disease vulnerabilities in cancer and neurodegeneration.\nHonors\nHonors\n2024 – ACS Bader Award in Bioorganic or Bioinorganic Chemistry,\n2022 – Ivano Bertini Award, International Conference on Copper Biology\n2021 – Guggenheim Fellowship\n2020 – Humboldt Award\n2019 – Sackler Prize in Chemistry\n2018 – RSC Jeremy Knowles Award\n2017 – Elected Fellow, American Academy of Arts and Sciences\n2016 – Cruickshank Award, Gordon Research Conferences\n2015 – Fellow, Royal Society of Chemistry\n2015 – Blavatnik National Award in Chemistry\n2014 – Sackler Professor, UC Berkeley/UCSF\n2013 – Baekeland Award, ACS New Jersey Section\n2013 – Noyce Prize for Excellence in Undergraduate Teaching, UC Berkeley\n2013 – ACS Nobel Laureate Signature Award in Graduate Education\n2012 – RSC Chemistry of Transition Metals Award\n2012 – ACS Eli Lilly Award in Biological Chemistry\n2011 – Wilson Prize, Harvard University\n2011 – Miller Institute Professor, UC Berkeley\n2011 – Society for Biological Inorganic Chemistry Early Career Award\n2010 – ACS Cope Scholar Award\n2009 – Novartis Early Career Award\n2009 – Astra Zeneca Excellence in Chemistry Award\n2008 – Howard Hughes Medical Institute Investigator\n2008 – Technology Review TR35 Young Innovator\n2008 – Bau Award in Inorganic Chemistry\n2008 – Hellman Faculty Award\n2008 – Amgen Young Investigator Award\n2008 – Paul Saltman Award, Metals in Biology Gordon Research Conference\n2007 – Sloan Fellowship\n2006 – Packard Fellowship\n2006 – NSF CAREER Award\n2005 – Beckman Young Investigator\n2005 – American Federation for Aging Research Award\n2004 – Dreyfus New Faculty Award\n+ View All\nSelected Publications\n“Oxidative cyclization reagents reveal tryptophan cation-pi interactions”, Xie, X.; Moon, P. J.; Crossley, S. W. M.; Bischoff, A. J.; He, D.; Li, G.; Dao, N.; Gonzalez-Valero, A.; Reeves, A. G.; McKenna, J. M.; Elledge, S. K.; Wells, J. A.; Toste, F. D.*; Chang, C. J.*\nNature\n2024\n,\n627,\n680-687.\n“Formaldehyde regulates S-adenosylmethionine biosynthesis and one-carbon metabolism”, Pham, V. N.; Bruemmer, K. J.; Toh, J. D.; Ge, E. J.; Tenney, L.; Ward, C. C.; Dingler, F. A.; Millington, C. L.; Garcia-Prieto, C. A.; Pulos-Homes, M. C.; Ingolia, N. T.; Bontel, L. B.; Esteller, M.; Patel, K. J.; Nomura, D. K.; Chang, C. J.*\nScience\n2023\n,\n382\n, 1-10.\n“Oxidation state-specific fluorescent copper sensors reveal oncogene-driven redox changes that regulate labile copper(II) pools”, Pezacki, A. T.; Matier, C. D.; Gu, X,; Kummelstedt, E.; Bond, S. E.; Torrente, L; Jordan-Sciutto, K. L.; DeNicola, G. M.; Su, T. A.; Brady, D. C.; Chang, C. J.*\nProc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA\n2022\n,\n119\n, e2202736119.\n“A tandem activity-based sensing and labeling strategy enables imaging of transcellular hydrogen peroxide signaling”, Iwashita, H.; Castillo, E.; Messina, M. S.; Swanson, R.A.; Chang, C. J.*\nProc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA\n2021\n,\n118\n, e2018513118.\n“Activity-based ratiometric FRET probe reveals oncogene-driven changes in labile copper pools induced by altered glutathione metabolism”, Chung, C. Y.; Posimo, J. M.; Lee, S.; Tsang, T.; Davis, J. M.; Brady, D. C.; Chang, C. J.*\nProc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA\n2019\n,\n116\n, 18285-18294.\n“Copper regulates rest-activity cycles through the locus coeruleus-norepinephrine system”, Xiao, T.; Ackerman, C. M.; Carroll, E. C.; Jia, S.; Hoagland, A.; Chan, J.; Thai. B.; Liu, C. S.; Isacoff, E. Y.; Chang, C. J.*\nNature Chem. Biol.\n2018\n,\n14\n, 655-663.\n“Redox-based reagents for chemoselective methionine bioconjugation”, Lin, S.; Yang, X.; Jia, S.; Weeks, A. M.; Hornsby, M.; Lee, P. S.; Nichiporuk, R. V.; Iavarone, A. T.; Wells, J. A.; Toste, F. D.; Chang, C. J.*\nScience\n2017\n, 355\n, 597-602.\n“Copper regulates cyclic-AMP-dependent lipolysis”, Krishnamoorthy, L.; Cotruvo Jr., J. A.; Chan, J.; Kaluarachchi, H...[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Songfang Huang": {
    "homepage": "https://cs.ou.edu/~songf/",
    "emails": [
      "songf@ou.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Welcome to Song's Homepage\nHOME\nTEACHING\nGROUP\nCV\nSong Fang\nPh.D., Associate Professor\nSchool of Computer Science\nUniversity of Oklahoma\n110 W. Boyd St., DEH 232, Norman, OK 73019\nPhone: 405-325-3536\nEmail: songf\nat\nou.edu\nHiring!\nI am looking for self-motivated students who are interested in security & privacy issues!\nWhat's New?\n[Sep 2025] I am invited to serve on the TPC of ACM CCS 2026.\n[Aug 2025] Our work on IoT (smart camera) security has been accepted by NDSS 2026. Congratulations to Yan and Guanchong!\n[Aug 2025] Congratulations to Lyndsey for being selected to receive the Engineering Research Fellowship from the Gallogly College of Engineering (GCoE) for Fall 2025 ($3,000)!\n[Aug 2025] Our work on VR security has been accepted by RAID 2025. Congratulations to Guanchong and Yan!\n[July 2025] Our work on a new side-channel attack for video identification has been accepted by PETS 2026. Congratulations to Guanchong!\n[July 2025] I am invited to serve on the TPC of IEEE EuroS&P 2026.\n[April 2025] I am invited to serve on the TPCs of NDSS 2026 and MILCOM 2025 Track 3 - Cyber Security and Trusted Computing.\n[Feb 2025] I have joined IEEE TDSC's Editorial Board as an Associate Editor.\n[Feb 2025] I am invited to serve on the TPCs of RAID 2025 and IEEE CNS 2025.\n[Dec 2024] Yan Zhou successfully defended her MS thesis. Congratulations to Yan!\n[Oct 2024] I am invited to serve on the TPC of IEEE EuroS&P 2025.\n[Aug 2024] Our work on a new GPS-based in-vehicle message dissemination scheme is to appear in IEEE CNS 2024.\n[Aug 2024] Our work on defenses agaist wireless breath and crowd inference has been accepted to IEEE/ACM ToN.\n[July 2024] I am invited to serve on the TPC of ACM AsiaCCS 2025.\n[April 2024] Congratulations to Qiuye, who has been selected to receive a Gallogly College of Engineering (GCoE) Dissertation Excellence Award ($5,000)!\n[Mar 2024] I will be promoted to Associate Professor with Tenure in the School of Computer Science, starting from July 1, 2024.\n[Mar 2024] Congratulations to our lab member Qiuye, who will join University of Missouri - Kansas City as a Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Fall 2024!\n[Feb 2024] Our survey paper on wireless inference against privacy has been accepted to IEEE Communications Surveys and Tutorials (COMST).\n[Dec 2023] I am invited to serve on the TPC of ACM CCS 2024.\n[Nov 2023] Our work on camera localization has been accepted to IEEE TMC.\n[Aug 2023] Our work on smart camera security has been accepted by ACM CCS 2023. Congratulations to Yan and Qiuye!\n[July 2023] Our work on wireless liveness detection has been accepted by RAID 2023. Congratulations to Qiuye!\n[Dec 2022] Yan He successfully defended his MS thesis. Congratulations to Yan! He will stay in our group and pursue his Ph.D. degree!\n[Nov 2022] Our paper \"HoneyBreath: An Ambush Tactic Against Wireless Breath Monitoring\" won EAI MobiQuitous 2022 Best Paper Award!\n[Oct 2022] Qiuye He and Edwin Yang both won the ACM CCS travel grant. Congratulations to both!\n[Sep 2022] Thanks for the support of NSF to our project \"Exploiting Stimulus-response Correlation for Wireless Hidden Device Localization\"!\n[Sep 2022] Edwin Yang won the IEEE CNS travel grant and the CS Alumni Graduate Fellowship. Congratulations to Edwin!\n[Sep 2022] Our work on proactive defenses against wireless breath inference is to appear in EAI MobiQuitous 2022. Congratulations, Qiuye and Edwin!\n[Aug 2022] Our work on driving-assisted key establishment is to appear in IEEE CNS 2022. Congratulations, Edwin!\n[April 2022] Our work on inferring numerical keystrokes is to appear in ACM CCS 2022. Congratulations, Edwin and Qiuye!\n[April 2022] I received the Faculty Investment Program award from the Office of the Vice President for Research and Partnerships (OVPRP) at OU. Thanks to OU OVPRP for the support!\nMore News >>>\n[Jan 2022] Our work on keystroke eavesdropping attack and defense has been accepted to IEEE/ACM ToN.\n[Jan 2022] Our work on a proactive anti-eavesdropping scheme has been accepted to IEEE TDSC.\n[Aug 2021] Our work on a new attack against ASR systems is to appear in ACSAC 2021. Congratulations, John and Edwin!\n[June 2021] I receive an REU supplement for the NSF CRII project. Thanks to NSF for the support!\n[May 2021] Our work on pinpointing wireless hidden cameras is to appear in ACM MobiSys 2021. Congratulations, Yan and Qiuye!\n[April 2021] I am invited to serve on the TPCs of IEEE CNS 2021 and IEEE INFOCOM 2022.\n[Mar 2021] I received the FY 21 Junior Faculty Fellowship Program award from the Office of the Vice President for Research and Partnerships (OVPRP) at OU. Thanks to OU OVPRP for the support!\n[Sep 2020] Our work on virtual step PIN pad is to appear in IEEE MASS 2020.\n[Jun 2020] I am invited to serve on the TPC of IEEE INFOCOM 2021.\n[Mar 2020] My CRII proposal is funded by NSF. Thanks to NSF for the support!\n[Mar 2020] I received the FY20 Junior Faculty Fellowship Program award from the Office of the Vice President for Research and Partnerships (OVPRP) at OU. Thanks to OU OVPRP for the support!\n[Oct 2019] I am invited to serve on the TPC of IEEE CNS 2020.\n[Aug 2019] Our work on wireless-assisted key establishment has been accepted to IEEE TMC.\n[Nov 2018] Our work on eavesdropper entrapment is to appear in IEEE INFOCOM 2019.\n[July 2018] Our work on training-agnostic keystroke inference is to appear in ACM CCS 2018.\nShort Bio\nDr. Song Fang is an Associate Professor in the School of Computer Science, University of Oklahoma. He received his Ph.D. in Computer Science from the University of South Florida under the supervision of Dr. Yao Liu in July 2018. He received his B.Eng. from the South China University of Technology and M.Eng. from the Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications.\nResearch Interests\nWireless and Mobile System Security\nCyber Physical Systems and IoT Security\nMobile Computing\nApplication of Machine Learning in Cybersecurity\nSelected Papers\nThe asterisk (*) marks students advised by me.\nPhantomMotion: Laser-Based Motion Injection Attacks on Wireless Security Surveillance Systems\nYan He*, Guanchong Huang*, and Song Fang\nThe Network and Distributed System Security Symposium (NDSS 2026)\n, pp. 1-18, 2026. (Acceptance Rate: 17.49%=113/646)\nSilhouetteTell: Practical Video Identification Leveraging Blurred Recordings of Video Subtitles (\npdf\n,\nBibTex\n)\nGuanchong Huang* and Song Fang\nThe 26th Privacy Enhancing Technologies Symposium (PETS 2026)\n, pp. 1-16, 2026. (Acceptance Rate: 23.56%=53/225)\nMotionDecipher: General Video-assisted Passcode Inference In Virtual Reality (\nBibTex\n)\nGuanchong Huang*, Yan He*, Shangqing Zhao, Yi Wu, and Song Fang\nThe 28th International Symposium on Research in Attacks, Intrusions and Defenses (RAID 2025)\n, pp. 298-313, 2025. (Acceptance Rate: 22.06%=62/281)\nCommanderGPS: Practical Hidden Command Dissemination Through Pedal Operations (\npdf\n,\nBibTex\n)\nEdwin Yang*, Guanchong Huang*, and Song Fang\nIEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (IEEE CNS 2024)\n, 2024. (Acceptance Rate: 29.4%=35/119)\nRevisiting Wireless Breath and Crowd Inference Attacks with Defensive Deception (\nBibTex\n)\nQiuye He*, Edwin Yang*, Song Fang, and Shangqing Zhao\nIEEE/ACM Transactions on Networking (ToN)\n, vol. 32, no. 6, pp. 4976-4988, Dec. 2024.\nA Survey on Human Profile Information Inference via Wireless Signals (\npdf\n,\nBibTex\n)\nQiuye He*, Edwin Yang*, and Song Fang\nIEEE Communications Surveys and Tutorials (COMST)\n,  vol. 26, no. 4, pp. 2577-2610, Fourthquarter 2024.\nPrecise Wireless Camera Localization Leveraging Traffic-aided Spatial Analysis\nYan He*, Qiuye He*, Song Fang, and Yao Liu\nIEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing (TMC)\n, vol. 23, no. 6, pp. 7256-7269, June 2024.\nWhen Free Tier Becomes Free to Enter: A Non-Intrusive Way to Identify Security Cameras with no Cloud Subscription\nYan He*, Qiuye He*, Song Fang, and Yao Liu (The first two authors are co-primary authors)\nACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security (ACM CCS 2023)\n, Nov. 2023. (Acceptance Rate: 19.1%=234/1222)\nPhantom-CSI Attacks against Wireless Liveness Detection (\npdf\n,\nBibTex\n)\nQiuye He* and Song Fang\nThe 26th International Symposium on Research in Attacks, Intrusions and Defenses (RAID 2023)\n, Oct. 2023. (Acceptance Rate: 23.5%=50/213)\nA Data-Driven Respirator Fit Test Model via Human Speech Signal\nJinmiao Chen, Zhaohe (John) Zhang*, Shangqing Zhao, Song Fang, Thomas M. Peters, Evan L. Floyd, and Changjie Cai\nIEEE/ACM international conference on Connected Health: Applications, Systems and Engineering Technologies (CHASE)\n, Jun. 2023.\nProactive Anti-Eavesdropping With Trap Deployment in Wireless Networks (\npdf\n,\nBibTex\n)\nQiuye He*, Song Fang, Tao Wang, Yao Liu, Shangqing Zhao, and Zhuo Lu\nIEEE Transactions on Dependable and Secure Computing (TDSC)\n, vol. 20, no. 1, pp. 637-649, 1 Jan.-Feb. 2023.\nHoneyBreath: An Ambush Tactic Against Wireless Breath Monitoring\nQiuye He*, Edwin Yang*, Song Fang, and Shangqing Zhao\nEAI International Conference on Mobile and Ubiquitous Systems:\r\n                            Computing, Networking and Services (EAI MobiQuitous 2022)\n, 2022. (*Best Paper Award*)\nDASK: Driving-Assisted Secret Key Establishment\n(\npdf\n,\nBibTex\n)\nEdwin Yang*, Song Fang, and Dakun Shen\nIEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (IEEE CNS 2022)\n, Oct. 2022.\nWINK: Wireless Inference of Numerical Keystrokes via Zero-Training Spatiotemporal Analysis\n(\npdf\n,\nversion with appendices\n,\nBibTex\n)\nEdwin Yang*, Qiuye He*, and Song Fang\nACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security (ACM CCS 2022)\n, Nov. 2022. (Acceptance Rate: 17.9%=58/324)\nGPSKey: GPS based Secret Key Establishment for Intra-Vehicle Environment\n(\npdf\n)\nEdwin Yang* and Song Fang\nFourth International Workshop on Automotive and Autonomous Vehicle Security (AutoSec)\n, co-located at NDSS 22, 2022.\nWireless Training-free Keystroke Inference Attack and Defense (\nBibTex\n)\nEdwin Yang*, Song Fang, Ian Markwood, Yao Liu, Shangqing Zhao, Zhuo Lu, and Haojin Zhu\nIE...[truncated]"
