    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "*\nWe use cookies to enhance your browsing experience, personalize content and analyze our traffic.\nBy clicking “OK,” you consent to our use of cookies.\nPrivacy Statement & GDPR\nOK\nSkip to main content\nEmbry-Riddle Home\n×\nMenu\nFind by Name\nFind by College\nFind by Department\nMenu\nHomepage\nFind Faculty By:\nName\nCollege\nDepartment\nJianhua Liu\nTitle\nAssociate Professor and Program Coordinator\nEmail\nJianhua.Liu@erau.edu\nEmail\nDepartment\nElectrical Engineering and Computer Science Dept\nCollege\nCollege of Engineering\nAreas of Expertise\nDigital signal processing, wireless communications, embedded systems, and avionics\nBiography\nJianhua Liu the Ph.D. degree majoring in Electrical Engineering, minoring in Statistics, from University of Florida in 2004. He joined the faculty in August 2004 and currently is an Associate Professor.\nAt Embry-Riddle, Dr. Liu has been developing teaching expertise mainly in two areas: devices/circuits and signals/systems. In the first area, he has taught EE223 Linear Circuit I, EE302 Electronic Devices and Circuits, EE335 Electrical Engineering I, and ES 405 Electrical Engineering II; in the second area, has taught AE430 Control System Analysis and Design, CEC315 Signals and Systems, EE308 Introduction to Electrical Communications. In addition, he has taught many sections of independent study in the above two areas as well as topics related to student robotics competitions.\nDr. Liu's research interests fall in the following areas: wireless communications, signal processing, embedded systems, sensor networks, and avionics. He has published extensively in the above research areas, including 16 papers in\nIEEE\ntransactions and other refereed international academic journals.\nRecently, Dr. Liu is developing RC airplane building/flying skills, aiming at improving enrollments/retention, better serving students' projects advising, and preparing for research in the area of autopilot. And also because it is a lot of fun!\nEducation\nPh.D. - Doctor of Philosophy in Electrical and Computer Engineering: Statistics,\nUniversity of Florida\nCurrently Teaching\nCESC 410L\n:\nCS Digital Signal Process Lab\nCEC 320\n:\nMicroprocessor Systems\nCESC 320\n:\nMicroprocessor Systems\nCESC 320L\n:\nMicroprocessor Systems Lab\nCEC 322\n:\nMicroprocessor Systems Lab\nCEC 315\n:\nSignals & Systems\nCEC 599\n:\nSpec Topics Comp Engineering\nResearch Projects\nVisual Guidance Remote Airport Lighting Systems\nDeveloping Aviation ASR and NLP Datasets and Tools\nRobust Automatic Speech Recognition for Aviation Applications\nLabs\nEECS Capstone Design Laboratory"
  },
