    "homepage_text": "Yifeng Huang's homepage\nYifeng Huang\nLast modified on Jan 29, 2026\nI am currently an Assistant Profesor (RTPC) at the\nUniversity of Southern California\n.\nI was a postdoctoral fellow at the\nUniversity of British Columbia\nin academic years 2022-2024.\nI received my PhD at the\nDepartment of Mathematics\nat the\nUniversity of Michigan–Ann Arbor\nin Apr 2022. My thesis advisor is\nMichael Zieve\nand my co-advisor is\nJeffrey Lagarias\n. I am interested in the interface of algebraic geometry, number theory and combinatorics. Among other things, I have worked on arithmetic statistics, moduli spaces, discrete random matrix theory, partitions, random permutations, Diophantine equations and arithmetic dynamics.\nHere are my\nCV\nand my\nresearch statement\nas of Nov 2025.\nEmail:\nyifeng.huang αt usc ∂οt edu\nMy previous email huangyf@math.ubc.ca is discontinued.\nOffice:\nKAP 438D\nPronunciation: ee-FENG or yee-FENG\nPronouns: he/him/his\nSeminars\nCaltech/USC joint algebra and geometry seminar\nI used to organize the\nUBC Algebraic Geometry Seminar\nwith Jim Bryan. (2022-2024)\nPreprints and Publications\nMultiple Rogers–Ramanujan type identities for inert quadratic orders\n, with\nShane Chern\n, submitted.\narXiv page\nMatrix points on varieties\n, with\nAsvin G\n, Ruofan Jiang, and\nYifan Wei\n, submitted.\narXiv page\nCohen–Lenstra flag universality for random matrix products\n, with\nHoi Nguyen\nand\nRoger Van Peski\n, submitted.\narXiv page\nCoh zeta functions for inert quadratic orders\n, submitted.\narXiv page\n,\nslides\nHilbert Series for Configuration Spaces of Punctured Surfaces\n, with\nEric Ramos\n, submitted.\narXiv page\n.\nLow degree subvarieties of universal hypersurfaces\n, with\nBorys Kadets\nand\nOlivier Martin\n,\nJ. Reine Angew. Math.\n(2026). accepted.\narXiv page\n.\nA Mordell–Lang type problem for GL\nm\n, with\nJ. Bell\nand\nD. Ghioca\n,\nBull. Aust. Math. Soc.\n111(3)\n(2025), 433–444.\npublished version\nCokernels of random matrix products and flag Cohen–Lenstra heuristic\n,\nForum Math.\n37(5)\n(2025), 1325–1332.\narXiv page\nCommuting matrices via commuting endomorphisms\n, submitted.\npdf\n,\narXiv page\nA non-abelian variant of the classical Mordell-Lang conjecture\n, with Dragos Ghioca,\nManuscripta Math.\n176\n, Paper No. 71 (2025).\npdf\nMotivic Coh and Quot zeta functions of singular curves\n, with Ruofan Jiang.\npdf\n,\narXiv page\n, slides in\nalgebraic geometry\n,\nnumber theory\n,\ncombinatorics\n,\nposter in a Ramanujan conference\nThe cokernel of a polynomial push‑forward of a random integral matrix with concentrated residue\n, with\nG. Cheong\n,\nMath. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc.\n178(2)\n(2025), 229–257.\narXiv page\nPunctual Quot schemes and Cohen–Lenstra series of the cusp singularity\n, with Ruofan Jiang.\narXiv page\n,\nposter\nCounting matrix points on certain varieties over finite fields\n, with Ken Ono and Hasan Saad,\nContemp. Math.\n818\n(2025), 181–195.\narXiv page\nLattices in Fq[[T]]^d and spiral shifting operators\n, with Ruofan Jiang.\nAdv. Applied Math.\n171\n(2025), 102950.\npublished version\n,\narXiv preprint\nCounting on the variety of modules over the quantum plane\n,\nAlgebr. Comb.\n5(3)\n(2022), 583–592.\npdf\n,\narXiv page\n,\nslides\n,\nvideo\nMutually annihilating matrices, and a Cohen–Lenstra series for the nodal singularity\n,\nJ. Algebra\n619\n(2023), 26–50.\npdf\n,\narXiv page\n,\nslides\n,\nposter\nContinuously Increasing Subsequences of Random Multiset Permutations\n, with A. Clifton, B. Deb, S. Spiro and S. Yoo,\nEuropean J. Combin.\n110\n(2023), 103708.\npdf\n,\narXiv page\n. Also published in\nSéminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire\n86B\n(2022) as proceedings of FPSAC 2022.\nproceedings webpage\n,\nextended abstract pdf\n,\nposter link\nCohomology of configuration spaces on punctured varieties\n.\npdf\n,\narXiv page\n,\nslides\nBetti and Hodge numbers of configuration spaces of a punctured elliptic curve from its zeta functions\n, with\nG. Cheong\n,\nTrans. Amer. Math. Soc.\n375(9)\n(2022), 6363–6383.\npdf\n,\narXiv page\n,\nslides\n,\nvideo\nUnit equations on quaternions\n,\nQ. J. Math.\n71\n(2020), 1521–1534.\npdf\n,\npublished version\n,\narXiv page\n,\nslides\nCohen–Lenstra distributions via random matrices over complete discrete valuation rings with finite residue fields\n, with\nG. Cheong\n,\nIllinois J. Math.\n65(2)\n(2021), 385–415.\npdf\n,\npublished version\n,\narXiv page\nInvited Talks (upcoming and past)\nMini-course on Cohen-Lenstra-type heuristics for random matrices, Ohio State (May 2026)\nAMS Spring Eastern Sectional, Special Session on Configuration Spaces and Applications, Seattle (Mar 2026)\nAMS Fall Virtual Eastern Sectional, Special Session on Representations of p-adic Groups and Noncommutative Geometry (Oct 25, 2025)\nNumber Theory Seminar, UC Irvine (Oct 9, 2025)\nThe Third Joint SIAM/CAIMS Annual Meetings (AN25), Special Session on Hypergeometric Series and Their Applications, Montréal (Aug 1, 2025)\nAlgebra and Geometry Seminar, HKUST (Jun 18, 2025)\nAlgebra Seminar, UCSD (Mar 10, 2025)\n(20 minute talk) AMS Joint Mathematics Meeting, Special Session on Quaternions, Seattle (Jan 2025)\nValley Geometry Seminar\n, UMass Amherst (Oct 11, 2024)\nANTC Seminar\n, Claremont McKenna (Oct 8, 2024)\nAlgebra Seminar\n, Virginia Tech (Nov 10, 2023)\nCombinatorics Seminar\n, UCSD (Oct 10, 2023)\n(8 min lightning talk) Southern California Number Theory Day, UC Irvine (Oct 7, 2023)\nAlgebraic Geometry Seminar, UCSD (Oct 6, 2023)\nAlgebra and Algebraic Geometry Seminar\n, University of Washington (Apr 18, 2023)\nNumber Theory and Algebraic Geometry Seminar\n, Simon Fraser University (Mar 2, 2023)\nAlgebra Seminar\n, Virginia Tech (Feb 24, 2023)\n(20 min talk) AMS Joint Mathematics Meeting, Special Session on Quaternions, Boston (Jan 4-6, 2023)\n(20 min talk) AMS Joint Mathematics Meeting, Special Session on the Combinatorics and Geometry of Jordan Type and Lefschetz Properties, Boston (Jan 4-6, 2023)\nAlgebra Seminar\n, Virginia Tech (Nov 15, 2022)\nAlgebra Seminar\n, University of Virginia (Nov 9, 2022)\n(20 min talk) AMS Eastern Sectional Meeting, Special Session on Young Voices in Combinatorics, Amherst (Oct 1-2, 2022)\nNumber Theory Seminar\n, University of California, Irvine (Sep 27, 2022)\nCombinatorics Seminar\n, University of Southern California (Sep 26, 2022)\nGraduate Algebra and Representation Theory Seminar\n, Rutgers University (May 2, 2022)\nNYC Noncommutative Geometry Seminar\n, St Johns University - New York City (Dec 1, 2021)\nAlgebra Seminar\n, University of Waterloo (Nov 25, 2020)\nDiscrete Mathematics Seminar\n, UBC (Nov 24, 2020)\nAlgebra and Algebraic Geometry Seminar\n, UBC (Nov 06, 2020).\nRutgers Algebra Seminar\n, Rutgers University (Sep 23, 2020).\nUMN Combinatorics and Commutative Algebra Seminar\n, University of Minnesota (Sep 18, 2020).\nThesis\nTopics on Polynomial Equations in Noncommutative Rings and Motivic Aspects of Moduli Spaces\n.\nthesis pdf\n,\ndefense slides\n,\ndefense video on Youtube\nA\nthesis template (Rackham 2022)\nbased on a Rackham 2021 template developed by Angus Chung and\nEamon Quinlan-Gallego\n, with updates according to the changes of requirements of the Rackham Graduate School in 2022.\nMentoring\nI am mentoring Kosuke Sato (undergraduate USC) on a project on truncations of flag Cohen–Lenstra measures.\nI have co-mentored Danny Vargas (undergraduate Harvey Mudd, PhD student Berkeley since Fall 2025) on the paper\nDynamical structure of AGM over finite fields with order congruent to 5 mod 8\n, accepted by\nRamanujan journal\n.\narXiv page\nI was a mentor of a research group in the\nREU program\nin Number Theory at the University of Virginia, joint with Hasan Saad (Jun 5–Jul 14, 2023). They published\nSato–Tate type distributions for matrix points on elliptic curves and some K3 surfaces\n,\nJ. Number Theory\n.\npublished version\nOutreach\nUCLA Olga Radko Endowed Math Circle (ORMC)\n, Advanced III lead instructor, joint with Wenyuan Li, UCLA (Fall 2024–Spring 2025). Worksheets (designed for 2-hour self work) are publicly available\nhere\n. Some selected worksheets:\nFractional Linear Transforms\n,\nPermutations\n,\nSymmetric Functions\n,\nNumerical Semigroups\n.\nBlacksburg Math Circle\n, guest lecture on Pólya enumaration theorem, joint with Weihong Xu, Virginia Tech (Feb 25, 2023)\nContributed Talks to local seminars\nCombinatorics Seminar, USC (Nov 10, 2025)\nTopology Seminar, USC (Oct 20, 2025)\nUSC/Caltech Joint Algebra and Geometry Seminar, Caltech (Oct 2, 2025)\nAlgebra Seminar, USC (Sep 8, 2025)\nNumber Theory Seminar, Caltech (Mar 6, 2025)\nCombinatorics Seminar, USC (Sep 23, 2024)\nAlgebraic Geometry Seminar\n, UBC (Nov 6, 2023)\nNumber Theory Seminar\n, UBC (Sep 14, 2023)\nDiscrete Mathematics Seminar\n, UBC (Jan 17, 2023)\nAlgebraic Geometry Seminar\n, UBC (Jan 16, 2023)\nNumber Theory Seminar\n, UBC (Nov 30, 2022)\nRTG Seminar on Number Theory, University of Michigan (Sep 27, 2021)\nRTG Seminar on Number Theory, University of Michigan (Jul 29, 2020).\nTeaching\nAt USC, I am currently (Fall 2025) teaching Calculus (MATH 118) and Number Theory (MATH 430). I have taught Linear Algebra and Linear Differential Equations (MATH 225) and Applied Combinatorics (MATH 432).\nAt UBC, I previously taught Differential and Integral Calculus (MATH 100, 101), Linear Algebra (MATH 221), and Multivariable Calculus (MATH 200).\nAt the University of Michigan, I previously taught\nMATH 105: Data, Functions and Graphs\n,\nMATH 115: Calculus I\n,\nMATH 116: Calculus II\nand\nEECS 203: Discrete Mathematics\n.\nUseful Links:\nMathSciNet BibTeX lookup\n(useful if you cannot log into MathSciNet; search with multiple authors does not function; pick one author instead.)"
  },
