  "serper::Yan Liu": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.google.com/view/yanliu-ai/home",
    "emails": [
      "firstnamelastname.cs@usc.edu",
      "yanliu@usc.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Homepage of Prof. Yan Liu\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nHomepage of Prof. Yan Liu\nYan Liu\nProfessor, Thomas Lord Department of Computer Science\nDirector,\nUSC Machine Learning Center\nco-Director,\nUSC Insitutue of  Ethics and Trust in Computing\nMing Hsieh Department of Electrical Engineering (by courtesy)\nViterbi School of Engineering\nUniversity of Southern California\nEmail:FirstnameLastname.cs@usc.edu\n(notice that yanliu@usc.edu is NOT me)\nPhone: 1-213-740-4371\nLab\n: RTH 3\n21\nI am a full professor in the\nComputer Science Department\n,\nViterbi School of Engineering\nat\nUSC\n. I was an assistant professor from 2010 to 2016, and an associate professor from 2016 to 2020. Before joining USC, I was a research staff member at the\nIBM T.J. Watson Research Center\nfrom 2006 to 2010. I received my M.S. and Ph.D. from\nCarnegie Mellon University\n. My research interests include machine learning for time series, physics-informed machine learning, and interpretable machine learning,  with applications to health, sustainability, and social media.\nI'm a fellow of\nIEEE\nand\nAAAI\n.\nResearch\n: Here's my\ngoogle scholar page\nfor a list of all publications and the\nMelady group website\nfor details about my research group and research projects.\nService\n: I'm serving as associate Editor-in-Chief of\nTPAMI\n, Board Member and Finance Chair of ICLR.\nI previously served as general chair (or co-chair) for ICLR 2023 and ACM KDD 2020, program chair (or co-chair)  of ICLR 2022, ACM KDD 2022, SDM 2020, ACM BCB 2020, WSDM 2018, and associate program co-chair for AAAI 2021.\nSelected Recent Publications\nA Theoretical and Empirical Analysis of Divide-and-Conquer Prompting in LLMs\n.  Y Zhang, L Du, D Cao, Q Fu, Y Liu.  COLM 2025.\nInstaSHAP: Interpretable Additive Models Explain Shapley Values Instantly\n.  J. Enouen, Y. Liu. ICLR 2025.\nAn Empirical Examination of Balancing Strategy for Counterfactual Estimation on Time Series\n.  Q. Huang, C. Meng, D. Cao, B. Huang, Y. Chang, Y.  Liu. ICML 2024.\nTEMPO: Prompt-based Generative Pre-trained Transformer for Time Series Forecasting\n.  D. Cao, F. Jia, S. O Arik,  T. Pfister, Yi.  Zheng, W.  Ye, Y.  Liu. ICLR 2024.\nSparse Interaction Additive Networks via Feature Interaction Detection and Sparse Selection\n. J. Enouen, Y. Liu. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 35 (NeurIPS 2022)\nInterpretability and fairness evaluation of deep learning models on MIMIC-IV dataset\n. C. Meng, L. Trinh, N. Xu, J. Enouen, Y. Liu. Nature Scientific Reports, 2022\nMiscellaneous\n(wonderful stories about our research work for non-experts in AI)\nThe Perfect Stitch: Training for Surgery Using AI\nUSC Researchers Analyze Coronavirus Misinformation on Twitter\nFighting Addiction Before It Begins\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "serper::Ruoxia Qi": {
    "homepage": "https://ruoxijia.net/",
    "emails": [
      "ruoxijia@vt.edu"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/reds-lab",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Ruoxi Jia\nRuoxi Jia\nHome\nReDS Lab\nResearch\nPublications\nTeaching\nGitHub\nContact Information\nRuoxi Jia\nis an Assistant Professor in the\nBradley Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering\nat\nVirginia Tech\n, where she directs the\nResponsible Data Science Lab (ReDS Lab)\n. She is also affiliated with the\nSanghani Center for Artificial Intelligence and Data Analytics\n.\nPrior to joining Virginia Tech, she completed her postdoctoral training and Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering and Computer Sciences at\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nand received her B.S. from\nPeking University\n.\nHer research aims to build AI systems that are efficient, reliable, safe, and socially beneficial. Much of her work develops the theory and systems for\ndata intelligence\n—methods that diagnose how training data shapes model behavior, design principled data interventions to improve capability, efficiency, and alignment, and secure AI pipelines against adversarial manipulation.\nHer work has influenced industry practices at companies such as\nAmazon\nand\nGoogle\n, and has been featured in outlets including\nThe New York Times\n,\nIEEE Spectrum\n, and\nMIT Technology Review\n.\nSelected Honors and Awards\n:\nUniversity Award for Excellence in Graduate Academic Advising\nOutstanding Paper Honorable Mention at ICLR 2025: “\nData Shapley in One Training Run\n“\nEarly Career Scholarly Impact Award\nBest Social Impact Paper Award at ACL 2024: “\nHow Johnny Can Persuade LLMs to Jailbreak Them: Rethinking Persuasion to Challenge AI Safety by Humanizing LLMs\n“\nDean’s Award for Outstanding New Assistant Professor\nNSF CAREER Award\nFor Prospective Students:\nI am looking for self-motivated students at all levels, including postdoctoral researchers, Ph.D. students, interns, master’s students, and undergraduates, to join our group. If you are interested, please email me your CV and make sure to apply through the\nVirginia Tech\nElectrical and Computer Engineering (ECE)\nDepartment\n."
  },
