    "homepage_text": "SangEun Han\nTop\nCV (PDF)\nPublication\nToday's arXiv\nContact\nSangEun Han\nRecent News\n[Mar. 2026]\nI will give a\ncontributed talk\n\"Beyond one-loop: higher-order effects on Gross-Neveu-Yukawa tensorial criticality\" (2026/03/16 08:24 AM, Meeting Room 404\nat APS march meeting 2026, Denver, Colorado, USA.\n[Oct. 2025]\nMy paper\n\"Beyond one-loop calculation: Higher-order effects on Gross-Neveu-Yukawa tensorial criticality\"\nhas been published in\nPhysical Review B\nas a regular article\n.\n[Oct. 2025]\nMy paper\n\"Fermi surface bosonization for non-Fermi liquids\"\nhas been published in\nPhysical Review B\nas a regular article\n.\n[Oct. 2025]\nMy recent paper\n\"Beyond one-loop: higher-order effects on Gross-Neveu-Yukawa tensorial criticality\" (arXiv:2506.20710)\"\nhas been accepted for publication as a Regular Article in\nPhysical Review B\n!\n[Sep. 2025]\nMy paper\n\"Fermi surface bosonization for non-Fermi liquids (arXiv:2306.14955)\"\nhas been finally accepted for publication as a Regular Article in\nPhysical Review B\n!\n[Sep. 2025]\nI joined\nProfessor Han Ma's group\n, at\nStony Brook University, Stony Brook, USA\n.\n[Jun. 2025]\nThe\npreprint of my new paper\n\"Beyond one-loop: higher-order effects on Gross-Neveu-Yukawa tensorial criticality (arXiv:2506.20710)\"\nhas been posted on arXiv!\n[Mar. 2025]\nI have given a\ncontributed talk\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa theory of $\\text{SO}(2N)\\rightarrow\\text{SO}(N)\\times\\text{SO}(N)$ spontaneous symmetry breaking\" (2025/03/18 03:00 PM, Room 251A (Level 2))\nat APS march meeting 2025, Anaheim, California, USA!\n[Mar. 2025]\nMy paper\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa SO(2) and SO(3) tensorial criticality\"\nhas been published in\nPhysical Review B\nas a regular article\n.\n[Feb. 2025]\nMy recent paper\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa SO(2) and SO(3) tensorial criticality (arXiv:2411.16842)\"\nhas been accepted for publication as a Regular Article in\nPhysical Review B\n!\n[Feb. 2025]\nI came back to Canada.\n[Feb. 2025]\nI will give a\ntalk at Institute for Solid State Physics at the University of Tokyo in Kashiwa, Japan on February 5th\n.\n[Jan. 2025]\nI will give a\ntalk at Korea Institute for Advanced Study in Seoul, Korea on January 21st\n.\n[Jan. 2025]\nI will give a talk at Seoul National University in Seoul, Korea on January 17th.\n[Jan. 2025]\nI will give a talk at Hanyang University in Seoul, Korea on January 9th.\n[Jan. 2025]\nI will give a\ntalk at KAIST in Daejeon, Korea on January 8th\n.\n[Jan. 2025]\nI have been Korea for the month of January 2025.\n[Nov. 2024]\nThe\npreprint of my new paper\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa SO(2) and SO(3) tensorial criticality (arXiv:2411.16842)\"\nhas been posted on arXiv!\n[Nov. 2024]\nI have been visiting the Institute for Solid State Physics, the University of Tokyo in Japan from November 18th 2024 to early January 2025. And I plan to visit Korea for the month of January 2025.\n[Sep. 2024]\nMy paper\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa theory of $\\text{SO}(2N)\\rightarrow\\text{SO}(N)\\times\\text{SO}(N)$ spontaneous symmetry breaking\"\nhas been published in\nPhysical Review B\nas a regular article\n.\n[Sep. 2024]\nMy recent paper\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa theory of $\\text{SO}(2N)\\rightarrow\\text{SO}(N)\\times\\text{SO}(N)$ spontaneous symmetry breaking (arXiv:2406.01681)\"\nhas been accepted on\nPhysical Review B\n!\n[Jun. 2024]\nThe\npreprint of my new paper\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa theory of $\\text{SO}(2N)\\rightarrow\\text{SO}(N)\\times\\text{SO}(N)$ spontaneous symmetry breaking (arXiv:2406.01681)\"\nhas been posted on arXiv!\n[May 2024]\nMy paper\n\"Spontaneous breaking of the SO(2N) symmetry in the Gross-Neveu model\"\nhas been published in\nPhysical Review D\nas a regular article\n.\n[Apr. 2024]\nMy recent paper\n\"On spontaneous breaking of the SO(2N) symmetry in the Gross-Neveu model (arXiv:2403.09627)\"\nhas been accepted on\nPhysical Review D\n!\n[Mar. 2024]\nThe\npreprint of my new paper\n\"On spontaneous breaking of the SO(2N) symmetry in the Gross-Neveu model (arXiv:2403.09627)\"\nhas been posted on arXiv!\n[Mar. 2024]\nI have given a\ncontributed talk\n\"Bosonization of Non-Fermi Liquids\" (2023/03/04 08:48 AM, Room L100H)\nat APS march meeting 2024, Minneapolis, Minnesota, USA!\n[Sep. 2023]\nI joined\nProfessor Igor Herbut's group\n, at\nSimon Fraser University, Burnaby, Canada\n.\n[Aug. 2023]\nI have moved to Vancouver! My new email address is 'sangeun_han at sfu.ca'.\n[Aug. 2023]\nMy paper\n\"Quantum impurity model for two-stage multipolar ordering and Fermi surface reconstruction\"\nhas been published in\nPhysical Review B\nas a\nLetter\n.\n[Jul. 2023]\nMy paper\n\"Theory of Quantum impurity model for two-stage multipolar ordering and Fermi surface reconstruction\"\nhas been accepted as a\nLetter\nin\nPhysical Review B\n.\n[Jul. 2023]\nI have given a poster presentation\n\"Theory of a quantum impurity model for two-stage multipolar ordering and Fermi surface reconstruction\" (5-1952)\n, at\nJul 3 (Mon) 12:15-13:45, Place: Premier Ballroom C (2F), International Conference on Strongly Correlated Electron Systems 2023 (SCES 2023)\n, Songdo, South Korea\n.\n[Jun. 2023]\nMy paper\n\"Complex fixed points of the non-Hermitian Kondo model in a Luttinger liquid\"\nhas been published in Physical Review B!\n[Jun. 2023]\nThe\npreprint of my new paper\n\"Bosonization of Non-Fermi Liquids (arXiv:2306.14955)\"\nhas been posted on arXiv!\n[Jun. 2023]\nI have given a talk\n\"Theory of a quantum impurity model for two-stage multipolar ordering and Fermi surface reconstruction\"\n, at\nASG Mini-workshop, Daejeon, South Korea\n.\n[May 2023]\nMy paper\n\"Complex fixed points of the non-Hermitian Kondo model in a Luttinger\"\nhas been accepted in\nPhysical Review B\n.\n[May 2023]\nI have given a talk\n\"Theory of a quantum impurity model for two-stage multipolar ordering and Fermi surface reconstruction\"\n, at\nCondensed Matter Seminar, Simon Fraser University, Burnaby, British Columbia, Canada\n.\n[Mar. 2023]\nI have given a\ncontributed talk\n\"Microscopic theory of multi-stage Fermi surface reconstruction in higher-rank moment quantum materials\" (2023/03/08 08:36 AM, Room 222/223)\nat APS march meeting 2023, Las Vegas, Nevada, USA!\nAnd\nmy another work\n\"Non-Fermi liquid behavior and quantum criticality in cubic heavy fermion systems with non-Kramers multipolar local moments\"\nwill be presented by Daniel, at 2023/03/09 8:12 AM, Room 221.\n[Feb. 2023]\nThe\npreprint of my new paper\n\"Complex fixed points of the non-Hermitian Kondo model in a Luttinger liquid (arXiv:2302.07883)\"\nhas been posted on arXiv!\npdf version of CV:\nhere (updated at 2025/10/27)\nPublication\nW\nN\nE\n\"Beyond one-loop: higher-order effects on Gross-Neveu-Yukawa tensorial criticality\"\nD\nP\nU\nB\nL\nI\nS\nH\nE\nSangEun Han\nand\nIgor F. Herbut\nPhys. Rev. B\n112\n, 155161 (2025)\n(\narXiv:2506.20710\n)\nWe study the Gross-Neveu-Yukawa field theory for the  SO($N$) symmetric traceless rank-two tensor order parameter coupled to Majorana fermions using the $\\epsilon$-expansion around upper critical dimensions of $3+1$ to two loops. Previously we established in the one-loop calculation that the theory does not exhibit a critical fixed point for $N \\geq 4$, but that nevertheless the stable fixed point inevitably emerges at a large number of fermion flavors $N_f$.  For $N_f < N_{f,c1} \\approx N/2$, no critical fixed point exists; for $N_{f,c1} < N_f < N_{f,c2}$, a real critical fixed point emerges from the complex plane but fails to satisfy the additional stability conditions necessary for a continuous phase transition; and finally only for $N_f > N_{f,c2} \\approx N$, the fixed point satisfies the stability conditions as well. In the present  work we compute the $O(\\epsilon)$ (two-loop) corrections to the critical flavour numbers $N_{f,c1} $ and $N_{f,c2}$. Most importantly, we observe a sharp decrease in $N_{f,c2}$ from its one-loop value, which brings it closer to the point $N_f =1$ relevant to the standard Gross-Neveu model. Some three-loop results are also presented and discussed.\n\"Fermi Surface Bosonization for Non-Fermi Liquids\"\nD\nP\nU\nB\nL\nI\nS\nH\nE\nSangEun Han\n,\nFélix Desrochers\n, and\nYong Baek Kim\nPhys. Rev. B\n112\n, 165116 (2025)\n(\narXiv:2306.14955\n)\nUnderstanding non-Fermi liquids in dimensions higher than one remains one of the most formidable challenges in modern condensed matter physics. These systems, characterized by an abundance of gapless degrees of freedom and the absence of well-defined quasiparticles, defy conventional analytical frameworks. Inspired by recent work on the bosonization of Fermi surfaces [Delacretaz, Du, Mehta, and Son, Physical Review Research, 4, 033131 (2022)], we present a procedure for bosonizing non-Fermi liquids, which provides a holistic approach capable of addressing their intricate physics. Our method involves parameterizing the generalized fermionic distribution function through a bosonic field that describes frequency-dependent local variations of the chemical potential in momentum space. We propose an effective action that produces the collisionless quantum Boltzmann equation as its equation of motion and can straightforwardly be used for any dimension and Fermi surface of interest. Even at the quadratic order, this action reproduces highly non-trivial results obtainable only through involved analysis with alternative means. By offering a comprehensive description of the physics of non-Fermi liquids, our work stands as an important building block in advancing the comprehension of strange metals and associated phenomena.\n\"Gross-Neveu-Yukawa SO(2) and SO(3) tensorial criticality\"\nSangEun Han\n,\nShouryya Ray\n, and\nIgor F. Herbut\nPhys. Rev. B\n111\n, 115131 (2025)\n(\narXiv:2411.16842\n)\nWe investigate the relativistic SO(2)- and SO(3)-invariant Gross-Neveu-Yukawa field theories for real, rank-two, symmetric, traceless tensor order parameters coupled to $N_{\\text{f}}$ flavors of two-component Dirac fermions. These field theories arise as an effective description of fractionalized spin-orbital liquids. The two theories are the simplest and special cases of the more general class of field theories with SO($N$) symmetric tensor order parameter coupled to Dirac fermions, in which the symmetry is low enough to allow only one, and not the usual two...[truncated]"
  },
