    "homepage_text": "Dr. Yi-Yu Cai | Link Research Group | Illinois\nLink Research Group\nSearch\nDr. Yi-Yu Cai\nPh.D. in Chemistry\n, Rice University (2019)\nM.S. in Chemistry\n, Shanghai Jiao Tong University (2014)\nDissertation\nLight Emission from Single Plasmonic Nanoparticles\nPublications\nK. Shiratori, L. D. C. Bishop, B. Ostovar, R. Baiyasi,\nY.-Y. Cai\n, P. J. Rossky, C. F. Landes, and S. Link,\nMachine-Learned Decision Trees for Predicting Gold Nanorod Sizes from Spectra\n, J. Phys. Chem. C, 125 (35), 19353 (2021).\nY.-Y. Cai\n, L. J. Tauzin, B. Ostovar, S. Lee, and S. Link,\nLight emission from plasmonic nanostructures\n, J. Chem. Phys., 155, 060901 (2021).\nS. S. E. Collins, E. K. Searles, L. J. Tauzin, M. Lou, L. Bursi, Y. Liu, J. Song, C. Flatebo, R. Baiyasi,\nY.-Y. Cai\n, B. Foerster, T. Lian, P. Nordlander, S. Link, and C. F. Landes,\nPlasmon Energy Transfer in Hybrid Nanoantennas\n, ACS Nano, 15 (6), 9522 (2021).\nB. Ostovar,\nY.-Y. Cai\n, L. J. Tauzin, S. A. Lee, A. Ahmadivand, R. Zhang, P. Nordlander, and S. Link,\nIncreased Intraband Transitions in Smaller Gold Nanorods Enhance Light Emission\n, ACS Nano, 14 (11), 15757 (2020).\nY.-Y. Cai\n, S. S. E. Collins, M. J. Gallagher, U. Bhattacharjee, R. Zhang, T. H. Chow, A. Ahmadivand, B. Ostovar, A. Al-Zubeidi, J. Wang, P. Nordlander, C. F. Landes, and S. Link,\nSingle particle emission spectroscopy resolves d-hole relaxation in copper nanocubes\n, ACS Energy Letters, 4, 2458 (2019).\nC. Flatebo, S. S. E. Collins, B. S. Hoener,\nY.-Y. Cai\n, S. Link, and C. F. Landes,\nElectrodissolution Inhibition of Gold Nanorods with Oxoanions\n, J. Phys. Chem. C, 123(22), 13983 (2019).\nY.-Y. Cai\n, E. Sung, R. Zhang, L. J. Tauzin, J. G. Liu, B. Ostovar, Y. Zhang, W.-S. Chang, P. Nordlander, and S. Link,\nAnti-Stokes Emission from Hot Carriers in Gold Nanorods\n, Nano Lett., 19 (2), 1067 (2019).\nC. Yi, M.-N. Su, P. D. Dongare, D. Chakraborty,\nY.-Y. Cai\n, D. M. Marolf, R. N. Kress, B. Ostovar, L. J. Tauzin, F. Wen, W.-S. Chang, M. R. Jones, J. E. Sader, N. J. Halas, and S. Link,\nPolycrystallinity of Lithographically Fabricated Plasmonic Nanostructures Dominates Their Acoustic Vibrational Damping\n, Nano Lett., 18 (6), 3494 (2018).\nS. R. Kirchner, K. W. Smith, B. S. Hoener, Sean S. E. Collins, W. Wang,\nY.-Y. Cai\n, C. Kinnear, H. Zhang, W.-S. Chang, P. Mulvaney, C. F. Landes, S. Link,\nSnapshot Hyperspectral Imaging (SHI) for Revealing Irreversible and Heterogeneous Plasmonic Processes\n, J. Phys. Chem. C 122, 6865 (2018).\nY.-Y. Cai\n, J. G. Liu, L. J. Tauzin, D. Huang, E. Sung, H. Zhang, A. Joplin, W.-S. Chang, P. Nordlander, S. Link,\nPhotoluminescence of Gold Nanorods: Purcell Effect Enhanced Emission from Hot Carriers\n, ACS Nano Article, 12 (2), 976 (2018).\nS. Indrasekara, B. Shuang, F. Hollenhorst, B. Hoener, A. Hoggard, S. Chen, E. Villarreal,\nY.-Y Cai\n, L. Kisley, P. Derry, W.-S Chang, E. Zubarev, E. Ringe, S. Link and C. Landes,\nOptimization of Spectral and Spatial Conditions to Improve Super-Resolution Imaging of Plasmonic Nanoparticles\n, J. Phys. Chem. Lett., 8(1), 299 (2017).\nM. Yorulmaz, S. Nizzero, A. Hoggard, L.-Y. Wang,\nY.-Y. Cai\n, M.-N. Su, W.-S. Chang, and S. Link,\nSingle-Particle Absorption Spectroscopy by Photothermal Contrast\n, Nano Lett 15, 3041(2015).\nLink Research Group\nChemical & Life Sciences Laboratory\n601 S. Goodwin Avenue\nUrbana, IL 61801\nEmail:\nslink@illinois.edu"
  },
  "serper::F. Wei": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.google.com/view/fan-wei/home",
    "emails": [
      "fan.wei@duke.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Home\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nFan Wei's homepage\nPhoto credit: Andrea Kane/Institute for Advanced Study\nWelcome to my website!\nI am actively seeking motivated students (undergrads or grads) to join my reading group or research group. If you are interested in one or more of my research areas, please feel free to email me with a brief indication of your interests. A list of publications by topic appears below.\nResearch Interests:\nExtremal combinatorics, probabilistic combinatorics, applications of combinatorics to computer science.\nI am especially interested in using tools from probability, analysis and algebra to analyze large networks or other combinatorial objects.\nShort Bio:\nI am currently an assistant professor of Mathematics and assistant professor of Computer Science (secondary) at Duke University.\nI completed my PhD in mathematics in 2019 from\nStanford University\n, where I was fortunate to be advised by\nJacob Fox\n.\nThen I spent one year as\na post-doctoral member at the\nInstitute for Advanced Stud\ny where\nI was part of\nAvi Wigderson\n's\nCSDM (computer science and discrete math\n) program.\nPrior to that, I received my bachelors degree in Mathematics from MIT, and a\nMaster of Advanced Study\nwith Distinction from Cambridge University, UK. My previous internships include conducting research at Microsoft Research New England and Microsoft Research Redmond Theory group.\nFunding\n:\nI am grateful to be /have been funded by the following sources:\nNSF Award\nDMS-2401414 (2023-202\n8),\nNSF Award DMS-1953958,\nDMS-2404167\n(2020-2023).\nSimons Foundation, Algorithms & Geometry Unit (for the academic year 2020)\n.\nFounders’ Circle Member, Cynthia and Robert Hillas (for the academic year 2019 while I was at IAS).\nPublications:\nAlso available at\nGoogle Scholar\n.\nBelow is a rough classification of my papers by topic. Some papers have been significantly delayed at the polishing stage, so please feel free to ask me about them.\nGraph Densities, Ramsey, etc\nOn Domination Exponents for Pairs of Graphs\n, Grigoriy Blekherman, Annie Raymond, Alexander Razborov, F. Wei.\narXiv:2506.12151\nNew Sidorenko-type inequalities in tournaments\n,\nXiaoyu He\n,\nNitya Mani\n,\nJiaxi Nie\n,\nNathan Tung\n,\nF. Wei\n, arxiv: 2512.11222\nUndecidability of polynomial inequalities in weighted graph homomorphism densities\n, Grigoriy Blekherman, Annie Raymond, F. Wei.\nForum of Math, Sigma\n(\narXiv\n)\nUndecidability of polynomial inequalities in tournaments,\nHao Chen\n,\nYupeng Lin\n,\nJie Ma\n,\nF. Wei\n, IMRN.\nThe binomial random graph is a bad inducer\n, Vishesh Jain, Marcus Michelen, F. Wei, (\narXiv\n).\nCommon graphs with arbitrary chromatic number\n, Dan Kral', Jan Volec, F. Wei.\nCompositio Mathematica\n(\narXiv\n)\nThreshold Ramsey multiplicity for paths and even cycles,\nDavid Conlon, Jacob Fox, Benny Sudakov, F. Wei, submitted. (\narXiv\n)\nThreshold Ramsey multiplicity for odd cycles,\nDavid Conlon, Jacob Fox, Benny Sudakov, F. Wei, submitted. (\narXiv\n)\nNon-bipartite k-common graphs,\nDaniel Kr\ná\nl', Jonathan A. Noel, Sergey Norin, Jan Volec, F. Wei,\nCombinatorica\n. 2021 (\narXiv\n)\nOn the inducibility problem for random Cayley graphs of abelian groups with a few deleted vertices\n, Jacob Fox,  Lisa Sauermann, F. Wei.\nRandom Structures & Algorithms.\n2021 (\narXiv\n)\nGraphs with the local Sidorenko property\n, Jacob Fox, F. Wei, (\nconference shortened version\n). Paper version in preparation.\nRemoval Lemmas and Regularity\nLemmas\nLocal graph removal lemma\n, Jacob Fox, F. Wei. coming out soon!\nLocal induced graph removal lemma\n, Ruilin Shi, F. Wei, (hopefully coming out soon)\nLocal permutation removal lemma\n, Hung Le, Ruilin Shi, F. Wei, coming out soon.\nRuilin Shi (currently postdoc at Duke) will be on the job market next year!\nPermutations (some are also related to removal lemmas above)\nOn the growth rate of the Stanley-Wilf limit of blockable permutations,\nSaksham Sethi, F. Wei,\npaper\n.\nStrongly testing hereditary permutation properties with polynomial query complexity\n, Jacob Fox, F. Wei,\nin preparation\n.\nFast permutation property testing and metrics of permutations\n, Jacob Fox, F. Wei,\nCombinatorics, Probability and Computing,\n2018.(\narXiv\n).\nPermutation property testing under different metrics with low query complexity\n, Jacob Fox, F. Wei,\nSODA\n, 2017.\nTheoretical Computer Science (broadly interpreted)\nLanguage Generation and Identification From Partial Enumeration: Tight Density Bounds and Topological Characterizations,\nJon Kleinberg, F. Wei.\nPreprint\n,\nSTOC 2026\nDensity Measures for Language Generation\n, Jon Kleinberg, F. Wei.\narXiv:2504.14370, FOCS 2025\nSo\ncial Networks: Enumerating Maximal Community Patterns in c-closed Graphs\n, Gabriela Bourla, Kaixin Wang, F. Wei, Runtian Zhou, arXiv:2506.11437.\nFinding Cliques in Social Networks: A New Distribution-Free Model\n, Jacob Fox, Tim Roughgarden, C. Seshadhri,  F. Wei, Nicole Wein.\nSIAM Journal on Computing (SICOMP).\n2020\n(\narXiv\n), (a quick overview and motivation can be found in\nTim Roughgarden's homepage\n, under section \"Beyond the Worst-Case Analysis of Algorithms\".）\nLocal max-cut in smoothed polynomial time\n, Omer Angel, Sébastien Bubeck, Yuval Peres, F. Wei,\nSTOC\n2017\n. (\narXiv\n), (A very nice\nblog\nwritten by Sébastien Bubeck about this project.) (\nSlides\n)\nSpectral Graph and Spectral Techniques\nThe limit points of the top and bottom eigenvalues of regular graphs\n, Noga Alon, F. Wei,\nIsrael Journal of Mathematics\n(\narXiv\n)\nNear Ramanujan graph with many localized eigenvectors\n, Noga Alon, F. Wei, (\npreprint\n)\nCommon graphs with arbitrary chromatic number\n, Dan Kral', Jan Volec, F. Wei.\nCompositio Mathematica\n(\narXiv\n)\nSpectral Rigidity for for planar cubic graphs\n, Alicia Kollar, Peter Sarnak, F. Wei  (draft preprint available)\nNon-bipartite k-common graphs,\nDaniel Kr\ná\nl', Jonathan A. Noel, Sergey Norin, Jan Volec, F. Wei,\nCombinatorica\n. 2021 (\narXiv\n)\nOther Extremal Combinatorics\nExtremal number of cliques of given orders in graphs with a forbidden clique minor\n, Ruilin Shi, F. Wei, (\narXiv\n)\nJournal of London Math Society.\nOn the number of cliques in graphs with a forbidden minor\n, Jacob Fox, F. Wei,\nJournal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B,\n2017. (\narXiv\n)\nOn the asymptotic confirmation of the Faudree-Lehel Conjecture for general graphs\n, Jakub\nPrzybyło, F. Wei,\nCombinatorica\n. (\narXiv\n)\nShort proof on the asymptotic confirmation of the Faudree-Lehel conjecture\n,\nJakub\nPrzybyło, F. Wei, submitted. (\narXiv\n) (a companion paper of the one above. This short note focuses on regular graphs.)\nIrregular subgraphs,\nNoga Alon, F. Wei,\nCombinatorics, Probability and Computing\n. (\narXiv\n)\nPhase transition of degeneracy in minor-closed families\n, Chun-Hung Liu, F. Wei,\nAdvances in Applied Mathematics\n. (\narXiv\n)\nAn asymptotically sharp bound on the maximum number of independent transversals\n, Jake Ruotolo, Kevin Wang, F. Wei,\nElectronic Journal of Combinatorics\n.  (\narXiv\n)\nMiscellaneous\nProduct decompositions of the symmetric group induced by separable permutations\n, F. Wei,\nEuropean Journal of Combinatorics\n, 2012. (\narXiv\n)\nDvoretzky--Kiefer--Wolfowitz inequalities for the two-sample case\n, F. Wei, Richard Dudley,\nStatistics & Probability Letters\n, 2011. (\nJournal Version\n,\narXiv\n)\nArithmetic Properties of Generalized Rikuna Polynomials\n,  J. Cullinan, H. Hausman, A. Pacelli, S. Pegado, F. Wei,\nPublications Math\né\nmatiques de Besan\nç\non, Alg\nè\nbre et Th\né\norie des Nombres\n, 2014. (\nJournal Version\n)\nInvolutions on standard Young tableaux and divisors on metric graphs\n, R. Agrawal, M. Gregg, V. Sotirov, F. Wei,\nElectronic Journal of Combinatorics\n, 2013. (\nJournal Version\n)\nTwo-Person Fair Division of Indivisible Items: Bentham vs. Rawls on Envy\n,  S. Brams, D. Kilgour, C. Klamler, F. Wei,\nThe Journal of Philosophy\n, 2023.\nFor the list by not arranged by topics,\nclick here\n.\nTeaching / Mentoring Experience\nI was a mentor of\nNYC Discrete Math REU\norganized by\nProf. Adam Sheffer\nin summer 2021, where I mentored three undergraduate students. The papers are in preparation.\nI am currently\nteaching MATH 572 Combinatorics (Probablistic Methods in Combinatorics) at Duke University. Last semester I taught a minicourse in regularity methods in combinatorics. Previously I have also taught MATHH 371 Combinatorics. Let me know if you are interested in hearing more about future course offerings, or would like to join the reading group.\nPreviously, I have been teaching assistants for the following courses:\nCalculus several times (led  problem sessions and lectures)\nMath undergraduate research course (guided groups of undergraduate students to work on math research projects)\nDiscrete math series (led problem sessions; topics included basic real analysis and topology, graph theory and combinatorics)\nI have also been course assistants for many other courses, where the duties included hosting office hours, grading exams and homework.\nMiscellaneous\n2022 Barbados Graph Theory Workshop\n-\nOpen questions doc\n.\nContact Me:\nfan.wei [at] duke [dot] edu\nPhysics 215\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
