  "serper::Xumeng Gong": {
    "homepage": "https://www.gong-research.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Gong Research Group\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nGong Research Group\n_______________\nGong Research Group\nJoin us\nto Advance the Next Generation Bio-Electronics\nOur Mission\n.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .\nThe\nNEXT\ngeneration of electronics is going to be soft, smart, and more interactive. Their capacity of interfacing with our body enables them to collect and transmit the data of biological activity with the high performance (e.g. accuracy, speed) which are unimaginable before. This is going to change the landscape of healthcare, robotics, agriculture, and beyond.\nThe Gong Research Group at the University of Michigan is developing the soft electronic materials and devices. We utilize a transdisciplinary approach that unites physics, chemistry, and engineering. Our goal is to innovate soft electronic materials (e.g. nanomaterials, polymers, perovskites) from the molecular level, understand their fundamental properties using advanced spectroscopies, and advance the next generation of bio-electronics for the applications in biomedical sensing, soft robotics, energy harvesting and storage.\nResearch\n.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .\nNew Materials\nInnovate materials at molecular level.\nSpectroscopy\nUnderstand materials using light.\nBio-electronics\nInterface electronics with body.\nResearch\nPublications\nGet iN Touch\n.  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .\nnewS\nGong Research Group\nReceives\nCoE ETR Award\nPosted on:\nJune 19th\n, 2025\nOur group received funding from the U-M CoE\nDean's Engineering Translation Research (ETR) Fund to assist with commercialization of\nperovskite solar cells\n.\nGong Research Group Receives MTRAC Award\nPosted on:\nMay 29th\n, 2025\nOur group received funding to c\nontinue the\ndevelopment of l\now-cost, large-scale perovskite solar cells with high efficiency and stability\n.\nGong Research Group Receives MMRI Award\nPosted on: March 17th, 2025\nOur group received Michigan Materials Research Institute (MMRI) Faculty Proposal Team Award to develop self-adhesive, solid-state hydrogel electrodes for transcranial electrical stimulation (tES).\nZach Pizzo awarded\nIES Fellowship\nPosted on: November 18th, 2024\nZach selected for\none-year fellowship that brings together graduate students and postdoctoral scholars at the University of Michigan from diverse disciplines who are interested in advancing future energy solutions via interdisciplinary networks\nZach Pizzo Receives Joseph J. Martin Award\nPosted on:\nSeptember 18th\n, 2024\nZach selected for departmental award that recognizes exceptional graduate students who demonstrate outstanding scholarship, athletic excellence, and a strong commitment to service.\nGong Research Group\nReceives\nMTRAC Kickstart Award\nPosted on:\nAugust 9th, 2024\nOur group received\nMTRAC funding\nto develop\nl\now-cost, large-scale perovskite solar cells with high efficiency and stability needed for potential applications in the electric and hybrid vehicle and industry.\nRead more from\nChemical Engineering News\n.\nGong Research Group awarded ACS PRF Doctoral New Investigator Grant\nPosted on:\nJune\n26\nth, 2024\nOur group received ACS grant to develop recyclable, durable electrodes. Read more from\nChemical Engineering News\n.\nXiwen Gong\nReceives NSF CAREER Award\nPosted on:\nJan 9th, 2024\nXiwen\nawarded the NSF CAREER Award entitled,\n\"\nCAREER: Controlling the Deformability of Quantum Dots Solids for Wearable NIR Optoelectronics.\n\"\nMedia\nWe are hiring\nWe\nhave openings for\npostdoctoral fellow\ns, graduate students, undergraduate interns, and visiting students.\nClick Join Us for further information.\nJoin Us\nTeam\nResearch\nPublications\nJoin Us\nNews\nContact\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "serper::X. Shen": {
    "homepage": "https://www.fxshen.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "FRANCIS X. SHEN – Professor. Innovator. Athlete.\nSkip to content\nNavigation Menu\nAbout\nCV\nBiography\nNews Archive\nResearch\nShen Lab\nResearch Assistants\nAPPLY for Lab Position\nEvents\nCollaborators\nSSRN\nPubMed\nTeaching\nCourses Taught\nLaw & Neuroscience Coursebook\nAthletics\nWhat we’re discussing today comes from research done by the brilliant people at the Shen Neurolaw Lab.\nCBS All Rise (March 14, 2021)\nTo view this content, you need to have JavaScript enabled in your browser.\nTo do so, please follow these instructions\n.\nInnovator\nWith many amazing people, I am helping to build the new field of law and neuroscience, and I examine the ethical and legal challenges associated with technological innovations such as artificial intelligence. In recognition of this work, I was awarded the\nEarly Career Scholars Medal\nby the American Law Institute, one of two medals awarded every other year by the ALI. In announcing the award, the ALI recognized me as “a pioneer in establishing the interdisciplinary field of law and neuroscience.” More details are available on\nmy CV\nand you can learn more about the\nShen Neurolaw Lab here\n. (The photo above is from a\nprofile in the Minnesota\nStar Tribune\nnewspaper.\n)\nEducator\nI’ve spent much of my career in the classroom, teaching over 1,500 students since 2008. I’ve taught students at many levels, including high school, college, law students, Masters, and PhD students. I’ve taught at eight different schools, on a wide array of topics such as criminal law, law and neuroscience, bioethics, artificial intelligence, evidence, sports concussions, student frees speech, and more. You can read\ndescriptions of my courses,\nif you’re a Harvard student you can enroll, and if you’re a student elsewhere you can look at the\nevents page\nto learn more.\nAthlete\nI still train and compete in the sprint hurdles, which I’ve been doing for over 30 consecutive years. I started hurdling in high school in 1993, and the photo above is from competition in 2022. I’ve enjoyed success both nationally and globally, winning 10 individual age-group championships in USA Track & Field Masters Championships meets, and placing 5th in the 400m Hurdles at the 2022 World Masters Athletics championships. As “Coach Francis” I’ve also had the pleasure of coaching in both Minnesota and Louisiana. You can\nlearn more about my track and field career\nand see 30+ years of\nhurdling photos\n. And if you are interested in (re)starting your Masters track and field journey, come join us on the track!"
  },
  "serper::Meng Chen": {
    "homepage": "https://meng-chen.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/Casardo-Chen",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/mengchen24",
    "homepage_text": "Meng Chen\nMeng Chen\nHCI + AI Researcher\nI work on\nHuman-AI Interaction\n.\n          I am interested in making the communication between humans and LLM-powered systems more effective and reliable.\nExecution\nHumans and LLMs now collaborate on open-ended complex tasks. How can we make interactions grounded and communicative to complement their strengths?\nEvaluation\nLLM outputs (e.g., reports, series of actions) are long. How can we make oversight less cognitvely demanding when misalignments and threats are subtle?\nI am a 2nd-year CS PhD student at\nUC Berkeley\nadvised by\nAmy Pavel\n. I obtained my Bachelor's degree in Computer Science and Philosophy from the\nUniversity of Notre Dame\nwhere I worked closely with\nToby Jia-jun Li\n.\nFeatured Projects\n[All Projects]\nSurfacing Variations to Calibrate Perceived Reliability of MLLM-Generated Image Descriptions\nMeng Chen\n, Akhil Iyer, Amy Pavel\nASSETS 2025\nProject Page\nPaper\nLuminate: Structured Generation and Exploration of Design Space with Large Language Models for Human-AI Co-Creation\nSangho Suh*,\nMeng Chen*\n, Bryan Min, Toby Jia-Jun Li, Haijun Xia\nCHI 2024\nProject Page\nVideo\nPaper\nNews\n[Show More +]\nOct 26 - 29, 2025\nPresented\nSurfacing Variations to Calibrate Perceived Reliability of MLLM-Generated Image Descriptions\nat ASSETS 2025 in Denver, CO, USA.\nSep 28 - Oct 1, 2025\nPresented a poster for\nTaskArtisan\nat UIST 2025 in Busan, South Korea.\nJan 2, 2025\nOur paper\nLotus: Creating Short Videos From Long Videos With Abstractive and Extractive Summarization\nis accepted to IUI 2025!\nOct 13 - 16, 2024\nServed as a student volunteer for UIST 2024 in Pittsburgh, PA, USA.\nMay 12 - 16, 2024\nAttended CHI 2024 in Honolulu, HI, USA. Hosted demo desk for\nLuminate\n.\nMar 14, 2024\nOur Paper\nLuminate: Structured Generation and Exploration of Design Space with Large Language Models for Human-AI Co-Creation\nhas been accepted to CHI 2024!\nOct 29 - Nov 01, 2023\nAttended UIST 2023 in San Francisco, CA, USA.\nJun 05, 2023\nStarted summer research internship at UCSD Design Lab.\nFeb 13 - 14, 2023\nPresented\nAn Empirical Study of Developer Behaviors for Validating and Repairing AI-Generated Code\nin PLATEAU workshop at CMU!\nMeng Chen\nPronouns: He/Him\nPhD student @ UC Berkeley\n📍 Berkeley, CA, USA\nAbout\nPublications\nArts\nContact\nLast updated: Oct 31, 2025"
  },
