  "serper::Rasheed El-Bouri": {
    "homepage": "https://oxc.ai/who-we-are/",
    "emails": [
      "info@oxc.ai",
      "nigel.mehdi@oxc.ai"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Who we are\nSkip to primary navigation\nSkip to main content\nSkip to footer\nWho we are\ninfo@oxc.ai\n+44 (0)1865 593065\nAcademic Rigor\nTransformative Mission\nEthics at the Core\nAt the Oxford Centre for Artificial Intelligence, our engineers, data scientists, and social scientists blend academic insight with practical experience. Ethics and societal impact are built into every project, ensuring solutions that are both technically sound and socially responsible.\nOur mission is to help individuals and organisations harness the transformative power of AI, driving innovation while ensuring its use is responsible and delivers meaningful impact across industries.\nMeet our team\nProfessor Nigel Mehdi\nDirector of Teaching and Learning\n“AI isn’t just for data scientists; it’s a new language of leadership. My role is to make leaders fluent.”\nRead more\nProfessor Nigel Mehdi\nNigel is dedicated to equipping leaders with the knowledge to navigate the intersection of sustainable urban development, economics, and emerging technologies. His work explores how economic frameworks and artificial intelligence can be harnessed to create more resilient cities, enhance decision-making, and drive innovation across sectors. Through his teaching and leadership, he prepares the next generation of professionals to tackle complex urban, economic, and technological challenges responsibly and effectively.\nNigel is Professor of Computer Science at the University of Birmingham and teaches on Oxford’s Software Engineering Programme. He also lectures in real estate economics and property technology at Oxford Brookes University. He is the former Director of the DPhil in Sustainable Urban Development at the University of Oxford and directed the Master’s programme from 2017 to 2022, during which he received the OUSU Outstanding Tutor Award in 2017. Since 2018, he has served as Course Director for both the UAE Artificial Intelligence Programme and the Foundations for Chief AI Officers initiative for the UAE federal government.\nEmail Nigel\nRory Young\nStrategy & Programmes\n“The best AI strategy isn’t about the technology, it’s about transforming a firm’s workflow. I focus on the ‘how’ – moving from a good idea to measurable business impact.”\nRead more\nRory Young\nRory advocates for the responsible integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) that enable businesses to thrive within their communities, contributing positively to both economic innovation and societal well-being. His insights into organisational behaviour highlight the importance of aligning business goals with ethical AI deployment, ensuring that companies not only succeed financially but also harness LLMs to make a meaningful impact on local communities, society, and the planet.\nBuilding on a background in economics, Rory earned an MSc in Sustainable Urban Development from the University of Oxford. He also holds a Bachelor’s degree in Economics, Finance, and International Business, giving him a strong foundation in the relationship between economic growth and sustainable development.\nEmail Rory\nDr David Johnson\nSenior Consultant\n“AI models are built on one thing: data. Get the data infrastructure right, and you unlock incredible value; get it wrong, and you’re just scaling up your mistakes.”\nRead more\nDr David Johnson\nDavid is an award-winning educator and advocate for the thoughtful integration of data science and computational methods into education, governance, and society. His work emphasises the responsible use of technology to enhance learning, improve decision-making, and contribute positively to community development. As a consultant for the United Nations, he has advised on big data strategies that align technological innovation with societal needs, ensuring that advancements in AI and data science deliver meaningful global impact.\nHe is a Visiting Fellow at the University of Oxford’s Kellogg College, having previously held a Junior Research Fellowship there from 2015–2018. David is also a Senior Associate Tutor (Honorary) at Oxford’s Department for Continuing Education. He has served as a Senior Researcher in Oxford’s Department of Engineering Science and held research positions at the Data Science Institute at Imperial College London and Oxford’s Department of Computer Science. He currently directs the Master’s Programme in Information Systems at Uppsala University, where he also leads the Information Systems section and contributes to the Uppsala University Computational Social Science Lab. David holds a PhD in Computer Science from the University of Reading and a Postgraduate Certificate in Teaching and Learning in Higher Education from the University of Oxford.\nEmail David\nDr Farah Shamout\nSenior Consultant\n“As an engineer, I see AI not as magic, but as a system to be built and understood. My passion is translating that engineering rigor into practical skills for professionals.”\nRead more\nDr Farah Shamout\nFarah is committed to advancing clinical decision-making through responsible, high-performance artificial intelligence. As the leader of the Clinical AI Lab at NYU Abu Dhabi, she focuses on developing machine learning methods that harness real-world data to drive innovations in computational precision health. Her work spans electronic health records, medical imaging, multi-modal fusion, and human-AI collaboration, all aimed at augmenting diagnostics, prognostics, and ultimately improving patient outcomes in clinical practice.\nFarah is an Assistant Professor of Computer Engineering at NYU Abu Dhabi, Associated Faculty at NYU Tandon School of Engineering (Computer Science & Engineering and Biomedical Engineering Departments), and Affiliated Faculty at NYU Langone Health (Radiology). She previously served as an Assistant Professor Emerging Scholar at NYU, where she received the Campus Life Faculty Leadership Award in 2021. Farah completed her DPhil (PhD) in Engineering Science at the University of Oxford as a Rhodes Scholar, with research focusing on early warning models for clinical deterioration. She holds a BSc in Computer Engineering (cum laude) from NYU Abu Dhabi.\nEmail Farah\nDr Rasheed El-bouri\nSenior Consultant\n“A model that works in a lab is one thing; a model that delivers value in a business is another. My focus is on bridging that gap between pure theory and real-world industrial application.”\nRead more\nDr Rasheed El-bouri\nRasheed is passionate about advancing machine learning techniques that enhance real-world decision-making, particularly in healthcare. His research focuses on developing robust meta-learning and reinforcement learning methods that can adapt to noisy, complex datasets such as time-series data and medical imaging, improving the efficiency and effectiveness of clinical care. He is especially interested in how machine learning can be used for planning, control, and real-time decision support in high-stakes environments.\nRasheed has spent the past five years working with the Oxford Centre for Artificial Intelligence. During his PhD, his research concentrated on creating machine learning models to optimise healthcare delivery, with a focus on improving outcomes while navigating the challenges of imperfect real-world data.\nEmail Rasheed\nJan-Hendrik Hoehnk\nESG & Strategic Technology\n“ESG reporting is a considerable data challenge for every firm. I show leaders how AI can turn that challenge into a strategic opportunity for transparency and impact.”\nRead more\nJan-Hendrik Hoehnk\nAt the Oxford Centre for Artificial Intelligence, Jan brings a critical lens to how AI can be practically applied to sustainability challenges – from decarbonising property portfolios to enabling more transparent ESG reporting. With extensive experience in real estate and corporate sustainability strategy, he helps bridge the gap between technological promise and practical delivery, ensuring our programmes drive measurable impact and support the transition to a net-zero, circular economy.\nJan holds a Master of Architecture and a Master of Science in Sustainable Urban Development from the University of Oxford. He has broad experience in built-environment sustainability, with a focus on applying data-driven decarbonisation strategies.\nEmail Jan\nFiras Darwish\nTutor\n“This field changes weekly. My role is to bring the latest breakthroughs from the cutting edge of academic research into our curriculum, ensuring our teaching is always current.”\nRead more\nFiras Darwish\nFiras Darwish is dedicated to advancing responsible and interpretable artificial intelligence, with a particular focus on perception, interpretability, and uncertainty in machine learning systems. He joins the Oxford Centre for Artificial Intelligence Programme as a tutor, bringing experience in both technical research and AI governance.\nHis work spans clinical AI, where he has developed diagnostic and prognostic models at the Center for Artificial Intelligence and Robotics, as well as enterprise AI oversight, having contributed to monitoring frameworks and ethical evaluation methods at e&’s AI and Data division. Firas has also served on NYU Abu Dhabi’s AI Advisory Committee, helping shape strategic approaches to emerging technologies in education and research.\nFiras earned his BSc in Computer Engineering from NYU Abu Dhabi and is set to begin his PhD studies in Statistics and Machine Learning at the University of Oxford as a UAE Rhodes Scholar.\nEmail Firas\nDhiyaa Al Jorf\nTutor\n“My focus isn’t just on algorithms, but on ‘Machine Intelligence’ – how we can build systems that augment human decision-making. I bring a practical, builder’s perspective to our workshops.”\nRead more\nDhiyaa Al Jorf\nDhiyaa Al Jorf is passionate about building intelligent systems that bridge the gap between machine learning research and real-world impact. He joins the Oxford Centre for Artificial Intelligence Programme as a tutor, with a background in clinical AI, human-robot interaction, and embodied intelligence. At NYU Abu Dhabi, Dhiyaa led interdisciplina...[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Mojtaba Sahraee-Ardakan": {
    "homepage": "https://kfmei.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Kangfu Mei, Google DeepMind\nKang-Fu Mei 梅康夫\nResearch Scientist @ Google DeepMind\nEmail\n/\nGoogle Scholar\n/\nTwitter\n/\nService\n/\nMisc.\nAbout Me\nI am a Research Scientist at\nG\no\no\ng\nl\ne\nDeepMind\n, on the GenAI Veo team for video generation.\nMy research lies at the intersection of Multimodal Generative AI and World Model. I am interested in building AI systems that can\nsimulate\nour dynamic world for\ncreative control\n, leveraging these capabilities to enable more robust autonomous systems or advance entertainment applications.\nI was a key contributor to Google's first launched fully on-device diffusion model that runs for real-time generation, and my research has contributed to multiple Google products.\nI received my Ph.D. from\nECE, Johns Hopkins University\n, where I worked with\nProf. Vishal M. Patel\nI completed my M.S. at\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen\n, where I was advised by\nProf. Rui Huang\n.\nProspective collaborators:\nI am always looking for motivated and talented students! If you are interested in collaborating or intern, please don't hesiate to drop me a e-mail\n.\n2025 - Present\n2023 - 2025\nSummer 2022\n2021 - 2024\nSummer 2020\n2019 - 2021\nRecent News\nNEW\nDec 2025\n:\nKDS\nis accepted by NeurIPS25 for poster presentation. 🔥\nNEW\nOct 2025\n: Our team at Google DeepMind released\nVeo 3.1\n-- a new SOTA in video gen!\nNEW\nOct 2025\n: I gave a talk at\nKnowledge-Intensive Multimodal Reasoning Workshop, ICCV 2025\nfor \"The Power of Context: How Multimodality Reasoning Improves Image Generation\"\nJul 2025\n: I am serving as an Area Chair for WACV 2026. 👨‍💻\nFeb 2025\n:\nMMSR\nis accepeted by CVPR25. 📷\nJan 2025\n:\nField-DiT\nis accepted by ICLR25. 🧠\nJan 2025\n: I am now Dr. Mei! Thank you, Prof. Patel, for your guidance and tremendous support!\nResearch Projects\n(\nshow selected\n/\nshow by date\n)\nThesis\nEfficient and Scalable Generative Model Control for High-Quality Multimodal Synthesis\nKangfu Mei\nPh.D. Thesis\nService\nReviewer of CVPR, ICCV, ECCV, WACV, ICPR, ICIP, AAAI\nReviewer of IJCV, CVIU, and IEEE TIP, TMM, TCSVT, TCI\nMisc.\nI like chinese pop music and my favorite singer is\nJay Chou\n.\nTemplate"
  },
